\id ACT - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Acts
\toc1 The Acts of the Apostles
\toc2 Acts
\toc3 Act
\mt1 Acts

\imt Introduction
\ip This is the second half of a two-volume work. It opens by referring back to "the first account I made, Theophilus" — the book we call the Gospel of Luke, addressed to the same man. The writer never names himself. What he says about his method he says in the opening of the first volume: he was not an eyewitness, he investigated everything carefully, he depended on those "who from the beginning were eyewitnesses and servants of the word," and he wrote so that Theophilus might know the certainty of what he had been taught. In four stretches of this second volume the narration slips without explanation from "they" into "we" (16:10-17; 20:5-15; 21:1-18; 27:1-28:16), which is as close as the book comes to telling us that its author was there for part of it.
\ip The story runs about thirty years, from a hillside outside Jerusalem to a rented room in Rome, and its shape is announced in advance in the first chapter: witnesses "in Jerusalem and in all Judea and Samaria and to the end of the earth" (1:8). Chapters 1-7 are Jerusalem, 8-12 Judea and Samaria, and from 13 on the road runs out through Cyprus, Asia Minor, Greece, and finally Italy. Peter carries the first half; Paul carries the second. The last word of the book is "unhindered."
\ip The question the book keeps returning to is how a movement that began entirely inside Israel came to be full of people who were not Jews at all, and whether that happened by God's doing or by human innovation. It is not settled in a single argument but by a sequence of events the characters are forced to interpret after the fact — the Spirit falling on Samaritans, then on an Ethiopian official, then on a Roman centurion's household before anyone had baptized them, then on whole cities in Asia and Greece. When the leaders meet in Jerusalem to decide the matter (chapter 15), what they argue from is not principle but what God has already visibly done.
\ip Running alongside that is a second concern, and it is a legal one. Paul is arrested, tried, and examined by a tribune, two Roman governors, and a Jewish client king, and every one of them concludes he has committed no crime. The dispute, they keep saying, is an internal Jewish question about the law and about a dead man Paul says is alive. Whoever Theophilus was — the honorific "most excellent" attached to his name in the first volume is the address for a man of rank — the book takes considerable trouble to show him that this movement is neither a sedition nor a novelty, but the continuation of Israel's own hope.

\c 1
\s1 What the First Volume Left Unfinished
\p
\v 1 The first account I made, Theophilus\f + \fr 1:1 \ft The Gospel of Luke, addressed to the same man (Luke 1:1-4), where he is called "most excellent Theophilus" — the form of address used for men of official rank (compare 23:26; 24:3; 26:25). The name means "friend of God," and whether it belongs to a particular patron or stands for any reader who loves God cannot be settled from the text.\f*, was about everything Jesus began\f + \fr 1:1 \ft The word is deliberate. Not "everything Jesus did and taught" but everything he began to do and teach. The first volume was the beginning; what follows in this one is the same work continuing, now through the Spirit and the men he sent.\f* both to do and to teach,
\v 2 up to the day when, having given his orders through the Holy Spirit\f + \fr 1:2 \ft The Greek phrase can attach either to the giving of the orders or to the choosing of the apostles — "gave orders through the Holy Spirit" or "the apostles he had chosen through the Holy Spirit." English has to pick one; this reading takes the first.\f* to the apostles he had chosen, he was taken up.
\v 3 To them he also presented himself alive after he had suffered, by many sure proofs\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \tl tekmēria\tl*, which occurs nowhere else in the New Testament. It is the strongest evidence word Greek has: in Aristotle a \tl tekmērion\tl* is a proof that settles the matter, as against a \tl sēmeion\tl*, a sign that merely points.\f*, appearing to them over forty days\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Forty is the length of a formation: Moses on the mountain (Exodus 24:18), Israel in the wilderness (Numbers 14:33-34), Elijah's journey to Horeb (1 Kings 19:8), Jesus's own testing (Luke 4:2). It is the span before a new thing begins.\f* and speaking of the things concerning the kingdom of God.
\v 4 And while he was gathered with them\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Greek \tl synalizomenos\tl*, which is either "eating salt with them" — sharing a meal — or "assembling with them." Both fit; the risen Jesus both gathers them and eats with them (Luke 24:30, 41-43).\f* he charged them not to leave Jerusalem, but to wait for the promise of the Father — "which you heard from me;
\v 5 for John immersed\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Greek \tl baptizō\tl*, to dip or plunge under. Washing by immersion was ordinary in Jewish practice, for ritual uncleanness and for a Gentile entering Israel; John's innovation was doing it to Israelites. The word is rendered by its meaning throughout this book rather than transliterated as "baptize."\f* with water, but you will be immersed in the Holy Spirit not many days from now."
\s1 The Last Question, and the Commission
\p
\v 6 So when they had come together they asked him, "Lord, is it at this time that you are restoring the kingdom to Israel?"\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Not a foolish question. The prophets tied the pouring out of the Spirit to the restoration of the nation (Ezekiel 36:24-28; 37:11-14; Joel 2:28-32), and the verb here, \tl apokathistēmi\tl*, is the word for putting a thing back as it was. If the Spirit is about to come, they reason, the restoration must be at hand. Jesus does not deny the restoration; he answers about the timing and the task.\f*
\v 7 He said to them, "It is not yours to know times or seasons, which the Father has set by his own authority.
\v 8 But you will receive power when the Holy Spirit comes upon you, and you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem and in all Judea and Samaria and to the end of the earth."\f + \fr 1:8 \ft "To the end of the earth" is taken from Isaiah 49:6, where God tells his servant that raising up the tribes of Jacob is too small a thing — he is to be a light to the nations so that God's rescue reaches the end of the earth. Paul and Barnabas will quote that verse to explain turning to the Gentiles (13:47). The sentence is also the plan of this book: Jerusalem (chapters 1-7), Judea and Samaria (8-12), and outward from there to Rome (13-28).\f*
\v 9 And when he had said these things, while they were looking on, he was lifted up, and a cloud took him from their eyes.\f + \fr 1:9 \ft The cloud is not weather. It is the cloud that led Israel out of Egypt (Exodus 13:21), settled on Sinai (Exodus 24:15-16), filled the tabernacle and then the temple so that no one could stand in them (Exodus 40:34-35; 1 Kings 8:10-11), and overshadowed the mountain of the transfiguration (Luke 9:34-35). In Daniel 7:13 one like a son of man comes with the clouds of heaven and is given dominion. He is not disappearing into the sky; he is going in.\f*
\v 10 And as they were staring into the sky while he went, look — two men in white clothing had come and stood beside them,
\v 11 and they said, "Men of Galilee, why do you stand looking into the sky? This Jesus, who was taken up from you into the sky, will come in the same way as you saw him going into the sky."
\s1 The Waiting Community
\p
\v 12 Then they went back to Jerusalem from the mountain called Olives\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Greek \tl Elaiōn\tl*, "Olive Grove, Olivet." Rendered here to match the same phrase in the first volume (Luke 19:29; 21:37). Zechariah 14:4 puts the LORD's feet on this mountain on the day he comes to reign; the place he leaves from is the place the prophet says he returns to.\f*, which is near Jerusalem, a Sabbath day's journey away\f + \fr 1:12 \ft About two thousand cubits, a little over half a mile — the distance a man was permitted to travel from the city on a Sabbath. The author is giving his readers the distance in the measure they would know.\f*.
\v 13 And when they had come in, they went up to the upper room where they were staying — Peter and John and James and Andrew, Philip and Thomas, Bartholomew and Matthew, James son of Alphaeus and Simon the Zealot and Judas son of James \add [i.e. eleven names, and the twelfth place standing empty]\add*.
\v 14 All these were holding fast together in prayer\f + \fr 1:14 \ft "And supplication" is added after "in prayer" in the majority text and appears in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*, with the women and Mary the mother of Jesus and with his brothers \add [i.e. the brothers who had not believed him during his ministry]\add*.
\s1 Filling the Place Judas Left
\p
\v 15 And in those days Peter stood up in the middle of the brothers\f + \fr 1:15 \ft NA28 reads "brothers"; most older English versions read "the disciples."\f* — the crowd of names gathered in one place was about a hundred and twenty — and said,
\v 16 "Men, brothers, the scripture had to be filled full which the Holy Spirit spoke beforehand through David's mouth about Judas, who became the guide for those who arrested Jesus,
\v 17 because he was counted among us and was allotted his share in this service."
\v 18 (Now this man got himself a field out of the wages of unrighteousness\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Matthew 27:3-10 has the chief priests buy the field with the returned money after Judas hangs himself. Semitic usage will credit a man with what his money accomplishes, which is how both accounts can stand.\f*, and falling headlong\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Greek \tl prēnēs genomenos\tl*, either "falling headlong" or "swelling up." Both readings are old; this one takes the first.\f* he burst open in the middle and all his insides poured out.
\v 19 And it became known to everyone living in Jerusalem, so that the field was called in their own language Akeldama, that is, Field of Blood.)
\v 20 "For it is written in the book of Psalms, 'Let his homestead become deserted, and let there be no one living in it,'\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Psalm 69:25, where the curse is plural, against the persecutors of the righteous sufferer; Peter reads it in the singular, of one man.\f* and, 'Let another take his charge.'\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Psalm 109:8. The word is \tl episkopē\tl*, an office of oversight — the post, not the man. Peter's argument is that the position itself must be refilled.\f*
\v 21 So of the men who went with us all the time the Lord Jesus went in and out among us,
\v 22 beginning from John's immersion until the day he was taken up from us — one of these must become a witness of his rising along with us."\f + \fr 1:22 \ft These two conditions define what an apostle is in this book: present for the whole of it, from the Jordan to the ascension, and able to testify to the resurrection from having seen it. The pool of men who qualify is fixed and cannot be added to once that generation is gone.\f*
\v 23 And they put forward two: Joseph called Barsabbas, who was surnamed Justus, and Matthias.
\v 24 And they prayed and said, "You, Lord, who know the hearts of all, show which one of these two you have chosen
\v 25 to take the place of this service and apostleship, from which Judas turned aside to go to his own place."
\v 26 And they gave them lots\f + \fr 1:26 \ft The lot was Israel's ordinary means of putting a decision in God's hands: dividing the land (Joshua 14:2), choosing the scapegoat (Leviticus 16:8), identifying Achan (Joshua 7:14-18), naming Saul king (1 Samuel 10:20-21), assigning the priestly courses (1 Chronicles 24:5). "The lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD" (Proverbs 16:33). This is the last time it is used in this book. After the next chapter the Spirit speaks directly, and the lot is not needed again.\f*, and the lot fell on Matthias, and he was counted together with the eleven apostles \add [i.e. the twelve stand complete again, one for each tribe]\add*.

\c 2
\s1 The Spirit Arrives at Pentecost
\p
\v 1 And when the day of Pentecost\f + \fr 2:1 \ft Greek \tl pentēkostē\tl*, "fiftieth" — the Feast of Weeks, Hebrew \tl Shavuot\tl*, kept fifty days after Passover (Leviticus 23:15-21). It was the wheat harvest festival, and one of the three feasts that brought Jews from everywhere to Jerusalem, which is why the crowd in this chapter is drawn from across the world. By this period it had also come to be associated with the giving of the law at Sinai, where God came down in fire and sound.\f* was being filled full, they were all together in one place.
\v 2 And suddenly there came out of the sky a sound like a violent rushing wind, and it filled the whole house where they were sitting.
\v 3 And there appeared to them tongues as of fire, dividing up, and one rested on each of them \add [i.e. on every person there, not on the leaders only]\add*,
\v 4 and they were all filled with the Holy Spirit and began to speak in other tongues, as the Spirit gave them to speak out.
\s1 The Crowd Hears Its Own Languages
\p
\v 5 Now there were Jews living in Jerusalem, devout men from every nation under heaven.
\v 6 And when this sound came, the crowd came together and was thrown into confusion, because each one heard them speaking in his own language.
\v 7 And they were astounded and marveled, saying, "Look — are not all these who are speaking Galileans\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Galileans were known by their accent (Matthew 26:73) and were not taken for a cosmopolitan or multilingual people. That is the whole force of the question.\f*?
\v 8 Then how is it that each of us hears them in his own language, the one we were born into?
\v 9 Parthians and Medes and Elamites, and those living in Mesopotamia, Judea and Cappadocia, Pontus and Asia,
\v 10 Phrygia and Pamphylia, Egypt and the parts of Libya around Cyrene, and Romans staying here,
\v 11 both Jews and proselytes\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Greek \tl prosēlytoi\tl*, Gentiles who had come the whole way into Judaism — circumcised, keeping the law, counted as Israel. They are to be distinguished from the "God-fearers" met later in the book (10:2; 13:16, 26), who attached themselves to the synagogue without taking that final step.\f*, Cretans and Arabs — we hear them telling in our own tongues the great things of God."\f + \fr 2:11 \ft The list runs from the far east to the west, and it maps the Jewish dispersion: the lands of the Assyrian and Babylonian exiles, the provinces of Asia Minor, north Africa, Rome, the islands, Arabia. What is scattered is gathered here to hear one thing in every language at once — the reverse of what happened at Babel, where one language was broken into many and the people were scattered (Genesis 11:1-9).\f*
\v 12 And they were all astounded and at a loss, saying to one another, "What does this mean?"
\v 13 But others sneered and said, "They are full of sweet wine."
\s1 Peter: This Is What Joel Spoke Of
\p
\v 14 But Peter stood up with the eleven \add [i.e. the whole restored twelve standing together]\add* and lifted up his voice and spoke out to them: "Men of Judea, and all of you who live in Jerusalem, let this be known to you, and give ear to my words.
\v 15 These men are not drunk, as you suppose, for it is the third hour of the day\f + \fr 2:15 \ft About nine in the morning, the hour of the morning offering and prayer. Nobody had eaten or drunk yet.\f*;
\v 16 but this is what was spoken through the prophet Joel:
\v 17 'And it shall be in the last days\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Joel 2:28-32. Joel says "after this"; Peter says "in the last days," borrowing the phrase from Isaiah 2:2 and Micah 4:1. The change is the whole point of the citation: he is claiming that the final age has begun, and that what the crowd is watching is its first sign.\f*, says God, that I will pour out from my Spirit on all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and your young men shall see visions, and your old men shall dream dreams;
\v 18 and even on my slaves, both men and women, in those days I will pour out from my Spirit, and they shall prophesy \add [i.e. every rank of person, with no one left outside]\add*.
\v 19 And I will give wonders in the sky above and signs on the earth below — blood and fire and a cloud of smoke.
\v 20 The sun shall be turned to darkness and the moon to blood, before the great and shining day of the Lord comes.
\v 21 And it shall be that everyone who calls on the name of the Lord shall be rescued.'\f + \fr 2:21 \ft This last line is where the whole quotation has been going. In Joel the name is the LORD's own. By the end of this speech Peter will have said that God made Jesus Lord (verse 36), and the crowd will be told to be immersed on his name (verse 38).\f*
\p
\v 22 Men of Israel, hear these words. Jesus the Nazarene, a man marked out to you by God through works of power and wonders and signs, which God did through him in your midst, as you yourselves know —
\v 23 this man, handed over by the settled purpose and foreknowledge of God, you nailed up and killed by the hand of lawless men\f + \fr 2:23 \ft "Lawless" here means outside the law of Moses — the Romans, who did the actual nailing. The sentence holds two things at once and does not reconcile them: God had determined it, and the men listening did it. Both stand.\f*.
\v 24 But God raised him up, loosing the cords of death\f + \fr 2:24 \ft The Greek word is \tl ōdinas\tl*, "birth pangs," which is what the Greek Old Testament makes of the Hebrew \tl chevlei mavet\tl* — a phrase that means "cords of death" (Psalm 18:4-5; 116:3). "Loosing" fits cords, not pangs, so the Hebrew image is kept here. Most English versions read "the pains of death."\f*, because it was not possible for him to be held fast by it.
\v 25 For David says about him, 'I kept the Lord before me always, because he is at my right hand, so that I will not be shaken.
\v 26 Therefore my heart was glad and my tongue rejoiced; and my flesh also will pitch its tent in hope,
\v 27 because you will not abandon my soul to Hades\f + \fr 2:27 \ft Greek \tl Hadēs\tl*, the standard rendering of Hebrew \tl Sheol\tl*, the place of the dead. Not a place of punishment here; simply where the dead are.\f*, nor will you give your holy one to see decay.
\v 28 You have made known to me the ways of life; you will fill me full of gladness with your face.'\f + \fr 2:28 \ft Psalm 16:8-11, quoted from the Greek.\f*
\p
\v 29 Men, brothers, it is permitted to say to you plainly about the patriarch David that he both died and was buried, and his tomb is among us to this day \add [i.e. and you can walk to it]\add*.
\v 30 So being a prophet, and knowing that God had sworn to him with an oath that he would seat one from the fruit of his loins on his throne\f + \fr 2:30 \ft 2 Samuel 7:12-13; Psalm 132:11. NA28 stops here. The majority text adds "according to the flesh he would raise up the Christ," which is why older English versions run longer at this point.\f*,
\v 31 he saw ahead and spoke about the rising of the Christ\f + \fr 2:31 \ft Greek \tl Christos\tl*, translating Hebrew \tl mashiach\tl*, anointed one. Anointing with oil was how a man was put into office — kings, priests. It is a title of office, not a name: the one God has installed to rule.\f* — that he was neither abandoned to Hades nor did his flesh see decay.
\v 32 This Jesus God raised up, and of that we all are witnesses.
\v 33 So having been lifted high to the right hand of God, and having received from the Father the promise of the Holy Spirit, he has poured out this which you both see and hear \add [i.e. what Joel said God would pour out, this man has poured out]\add*.
\v 34 For David did not go up into the heavens, but he himself says, 'The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand
\v 35 until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.'\f + \fr 2:35 \ft Psalm 110:1, the most quoted passage of Israel's scriptures in the New Testament. In Hebrew it reads "The LORD said to my lord" — David, the king, naming someone above himself. The argument runs the same way as the one from Psalm 16: David did not ascend and does not sit there, so he was speaking of another.\f*
\p
\v 36 So let all the house of Israel know for certain that God has made him both Lord and Christ — this Jesus whom you crucified."
\s1 What the Crowd Must Do
\p
\v 37 And when they heard this they were pierced to the heart, and they said to Peter and the rest of the apostles, "Men, brothers, what shall we do?"
\v 38 And Peter said to them, "Turn back\f + \fr 2:38 \ft Greek \tl metanoeō\tl*, rendered by the Hebrew idea behind it, \tl shub\tl* — to turn around and go back the way you came. Not regret; a change of direction. Here it is entirely concrete: they had passed a verdict on this man, and they are to reverse it.\f*, and let each of you be immersed on the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins\f + \fr 2:38 \ft Greek \tl eis aphesin\tl*, which is "for forgiveness" or "with a view to forgiveness." Whether the immersion obtains the forgiveness or points to it is a question the Greek does not settle, and the English is left as open as the Greek.\f*, and you will receive the gift of the Holy Spirit.
\v 39 For the promise is for you and for your children and for all who are far off\f + \fr 2:39 \ft "Those far off" is Isaiah 57:19, "Peace to the one far off and to the one near." For this audience it means Jews scattered across the world. As this book goes on, the phrase turns out to reach further than the audience could have heard in it.\f*, as many as the Lord our God calls to himself."
\v 40 And with many other words he bore witness and kept urging them, saying, "Be rescued from this twisted generation."\f + \fr 2:40 \ft "A crooked and twisted generation" is Moses's description of unfaithful Israel in the Song of Moses (Deuteronomy 32:5).\f*
\v 41 So those who welcomed his word were immersed, and on that day about three thousand souls were added.
\s1 The Life of the New Community
\p
\v 42 And they were holding fast to the teaching of the apostles and to the sharing together\f + \fr 2:42 \ft Greek \tl koinōnia\tl*, which most versions render "the fellowship." The word means holding a thing in common, and the next three verses say what was held: property, food, need. It is not a word for friendliness.\f*, to the breaking of the bread and to the prayers.
\v 43 And fear came on every soul, and many wonders and signs were happening through the apostles.
\v 44 And all who trusted were together and had everything in common,
\v 45 and they would sell their properties and belongings and distribute them to all, as anyone had need \add [i.e. the measure was need, not equality]\add*.
\v 46 And day by day, holding fast together in the temple and breaking bread from house to house \add [i.e. still inside Israel's worship, and also in each other's houses]\add*, they shared their food in gladness and singleness of heart,
\v 47 praising God and having favor with all the people. And the Lord kept adding those being rescued day by day to the same gathering.\f + \fr 2:47 \ft The verse ends in NA28 with \tl epi to auto\tl*, which is both "to the same gathering" and simply "together." Later manuscripts and most older English versions add "to the church."\f*

\c 3
\s1 A Man Lame from Birth at the Temple Gate
\p
\v 1 Now Peter and John were going up to the temple at the hour of prayer, the ninth\f + \fr 3:1 \ft About three in the afternoon, the hour of the evening offering, when the people gathered in the courts to pray while the incense was burned (Luke 1:10). It is also the hour at which Jesus died (Luke 23:44-46).\f*.
\v 2 And a certain man, lame from his mother's womb, was being carried along; they would set him down every day at the temple gate called Beautiful, to ask for alms\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Giving to the poor was a standing obligation of Jewish piety, and thought to carry weight before God: "almsgiving delivers from death" (Tobit 4:10); "as water puts out a blazing fire, so almsgiving atones for sin" (Sirach 3:30). A beggar at the gate of the temple was placed where the traffic was most disposed to give.\f* from those going into the temple.
\v 3 When he saw Peter and John about to go into the temple, he asked to receive alms.
\v 4 And Peter looked straight at him, with John, and said, "Look at us."
\v 5 And he fixed his attention on them, expecting to receive something from them \add [i.e. expecting a coin, which is all he had ever received there]\add*.
\v 6 But Peter said, "Silver and gold I do not have, but what I do have I give you: in the name of Jesus Christ the Nazarene, walk."
\v 7 And taking him by the right hand he raised him up, and at once his feet and ankles were made firm,
\v 8 and leaping up he stood and walked, and went into the temple with them \add [i.e. through the gate he had only ever sat outside]\add*, walking and leaping and praising God.\f + \fr 3:8 \ft "Then the lame will leap like a deer, and the tongue of the mute will shout for joy" (Isaiah 35:6). The words the author chooses here — made firm, leaping — are the words of that passage, which describes the age when God comes to set everything right.\f*
\v 9 And all the people saw him walking and praising God,
\v 10 and they knew him — that this was the one who used to sit for alms at the Beautiful Gate of the temple — and they were filled with astonishment and amazement at what had happened to him.
\s1 Peter in Solomon's Colonnade
\p
\v 11 And while he held on to Peter and John, all the people ran together to them in the colonnade called Solomon's\f + \fr 3:11 \ft A covered portico running along the east side of the outer court of the temple, and a standard place for public teaching. Jesus had taught there (John 10:23), and the community meets there again at 5:12.\f*, utterly astonished.
\v 12 And when Peter saw it, he answered the people: "Men of Israel, why do you marvel at this? And why do you stare at us as though by our own power or godliness we had made him walk?
\v 13 The God of Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, the God of our fathers\f + \fr 3:13 \ft God's own self-identification at the burning bush (Exodus 3:6, 15). Peter begins where Israel's story begins: nothing here is a foreign god or a new religion.\f*, has glorified his servant\f + \fr 3:13 \ft Greek \tl pais\tl*, which is both "servant" and "child." It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the servant of Isaiah, and the phrase here — God glorifying his servant — is Isaiah 52:13, the opening of the passage that runs on into Isaiah 53. Most older English versions render it "Son" or "child." The same word is used of Jesus again at 3:26, 4:27, and 4:30.\f* Jesus, whom you handed over and denied before Pilate's face when he had decided to release him.
\v 14 But you denied the holy and righteous one\f + \fr 3:14 \ft "Your holy one" is Psalm 16:10, the psalm Peter had quoted at Pentecost; "the righteous one, my servant" is Isaiah 53:11.\f* and asked for a murderer to be granted to you \add [i.e. you asked for Barabbas, who had killed a man in the uprising]\add*,
\v 15 and you killed the leader of life\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Greek \tl archēgos\tl*, the one who goes first and the rest follow — a founder, a chief, the one who opens the way. Rendered "leader" here to match Hebrews 2:10 and 12:2, where the same word is used of Jesus. Most versions have "Author" or "Prince."\f*, whom God raised out of the dead — and of that we are witnesses.
\v 16 And on trust in his name, this man whom you see and know — his name has made him strong; and the trust that is through him has given him this soundness in front of you all.\f + \fr 3:16 \ft The Greek of this sentence is tangled and doubles back on itself. It has been left tangled rather than tidied up. What it says plainly enough is that the name did it, and the trust that came through him did it.\f*
\s1 Turn Back, and the Seasons of Refreshing
\p
\v 17 And now, brothers, I know that you acted in ignorance, as did your rulers.\f + \fr 3:17 \ft This is not a dismissal of the guilt but a classification of it. The law of Moses distinguishes the sin done unwittingly, for which an offering is provided (Leviticus 4; Numbers 15:27-29), from the sin done with a high hand, for which none is (Numbers 15:30-31). Peter is putting his hearers in the first category — which is to say, atonement is available to them. Compare Jesus at the cross: "Father, forgive them; they do not know what they are doing" (Luke 23:34).\f*
\v 18 But what God foretold through the mouth of all the prophets — that his Christ would suffer — he has filled full in this way.
\v 19 Turn back, then, and return\f + \fr 3:19 \ft Two verbs where English wants one: change your mind, and turn round. The second is the standard Greek for the Hebrew \tl shub\tl*, the prophets' word — "Return, faithless Israel" (Jeremiah 3:12); "Return, O Israel, to the LORD your God" (Hosea 14:1).\f*, so that your sins may be wiped away\f + \fr 3:19 \ft The verb is used of erasing writing. "Blot out my transgressions" (Psalm 51:1); "I am the one who wipes out your transgressions for my own sake" (Isaiah 43:25). Not merely pardoned — erased.\f*,
\v 20 so that seasons of refreshing may come from the face of the Lord and he may send the Christ appointed for you, Jesus\f + \fr 3:20 \ft NA28 reads \tl prokecheirismenon\tl*, "appointed beforehand for you." Most older English versions follow a different reading and have "who was preached to you before."\f*,
\v 21 whom heaven must welcome until the times when everything is restored\f + \fr 3:21 \ft Greek \tl apokatastasis\tl*, from the same verb the disciples used in 1:6 when they asked whether Jesus was now restoring the kingdom to Israel. Peter does not deny that restoration; he places it. It is coming, it is what the prophets have always been describing, and heaven holds Jesus until it arrives.\f* that God spoke through the mouth of his holy prophets from of old.
\v 22 Moses said, 'The Lord your God will raise up for you a prophet like me from among your brothers; you shall listen to him in everything he says to you.\f + \fr 3:22 \ft Deuteronomy 18:15-19. To be a prophet "like Moses" is the highest thing that can be said of a man in Israel — "no prophet has arisen since in Israel like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face" (Deuteronomy 34:10). The voice from the cloud had already given the same command about Jesus: "This is my Son, my chosen one; listen to him" (Luke 9:35).\f*
\v 23 And it shall be that every soul that does not listen to that prophet shall be uprooted from the people.'\f + \fr 3:23 \ft The last clause is not from Deuteronomy 18 but from the formula of Leviticus 23:29, "cut off from his people." That is the covenant penalty — expulsion from Israel. Peter is speaking to insiders, and the warning is about losing standing among the people of God.\f*
\v 24 And all the prophets from Samuel and those after him, as many as spoke, also announced these days.
\v 25 You are the sons of the prophets and of the covenant that God cut with your fathers, saying to Abraham, 'And in your seed all the families of the earth shall be blessed.'\f + \fr 3:25 \ft Genesis 22:18 with Genesis 12:3. The promise to Abraham was never fenced off inside his own descendants; the blessing was always aimed at everyone. That is the ground on which this book will later justify going to the nations.\f*
\v 26 It was to you first \add [i.e. first, and so not to you only]\add* that God raised up his servant\f + \fr 3:26 \ft "Jesus" after "his servant" is not in NA28, though most older English versions have it.\f* and sent him to bless you, in turning each of you away from your wickedness."\f + \fr 3:26 \ft Notice what the blessing turns out to be. Not land, not wealth, not a throne — being turned away from wickedness. The gift Abraham's seed brings is the ability to repent.\f*

\c 4
\s1 Peter and John Arrested
\p
\v 1 And while they were speaking to the people, the priests and the captain of the temple\f + \fr 4:1 \ft The \tl stratēgos\tl*, the officer second only to the high priest, who commanded the temple police and answered for order inside the precinct. His presence makes this a security matter, not a debate.\f* and the Sadducees came upon them,
\v 2 worn out that they were teaching the people and announcing in Jesus the rising out of the dead\f + \fr 4:2 \ft The Sadducees held that there is no resurrection (Luke 20:27; Acts 23:8). It is not merely that the apostles are teaching a doctrine the Sadducees reject; they are producing a case — a particular man, raised — and a healed cripple standing in the court as the evidence. If they are right, the party that controls the temple is wrong about the thing it is most publicly wrong about.\f*.
\v 3 And they laid hands on them and put them under guard until the next day, for it was already evening \add [i.e. too late in the day for a hearing to be lawfully held]\add*.
\v 4 But many of those who heard the word trusted, and the number of the men came to about five thousand.
\s1 Before the Council
\p
\v 5 And it happened on the next day that their rulers and elders and scribes were gathered in Jerusalem\f + \fr 4:5 \ft These three groups together are the council — the Sanhedrin, the nation's supreme court, and the same body that had condemned Jesus (Luke 22:66).\f*,
\v 6 with Annas the high priest and Caiaphas and John and Alexander, and as many as were of the high-priestly family;\f + \fr 4:6 \ft Annas had been deposed from the office by Rome some twenty years earlier, and Caiaphas his son-in-law held it. The author gives the title to Annas anyway, because that is where the power sat: five of his sons also held the office in turn. Luke 3:2 names the pair the same way.\f*
\v 7 and standing them in the middle they asked, "By what power or by what name did you do this?"\f + \fr 4:7 \ft A legal question, not idle curiosity. The law requires that a man who works signs be examined as to whose authority he works them under, and prescribes death for one who works them and turns Israel to another god (Deuteronomy 13:1-5).\f*
\v 8 Then Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit\f + \fr 4:8 \ft Filled again, though he was filled at Pentecost. In this book the filling is not a possession held once but a supply given for the occasion — see 4:31, where the same people are filled a second time within the same chapter. Jesus had promised exactly this for exactly this situation: "do not worry about how or what you will say in your defense... the Holy Spirit will teach you in that very hour" (Luke 12:11-12).\f*, said to them, "Rulers of the people and elders,
\v 9 if we are being examined today over a good deed done to a sick man, by what means this man has been made whole \add [i.e. if a healing is the crime before this court]\add*,
\v 10 let it be known to all of you and to all the people of Israel that it is in the name of Jesus Christ the Nazarene, whom you crucified, whom God raised out of the dead — in this name this man stands before you whole.
\v 11 This is the stone that was counted as nothing by you the builders, which has become the head of the corner.\f + \fr 4:11 \ft Psalm 118:22. Peter adds two words the psalm does not have — "by you" — and so turns a proverb into a charge. The builders are the men responsible for building up Israel; the stone they threw out is the one God set at the corner, where the whole structure is aligned. Jesus had quoted the same verse to these same men (Luke 20:17).\f*
\v 12 And there is rescue in no one else, for neither is there any other name under heaven, given among men, by which we must be rescued."
\s1 Ordered Not to Speak the Name
\p
\v 13 And seeing the boldness of Peter and John, and grasping that they were unlettered and ordinary men\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Not illiterate — untrained. They had not studied under a recognized teacher and held no standing in the scribal system. "Ordinary men," Greek \tl idiōtai\tl*, means laymen, men with no office.\f*, they marveled; and they recognized them, that they had been with Jesus \add [i.e. that this was where their schooling had come from]\add*.
\v 14 And seeing the man who had been healed standing with them, they had nothing to say against it.
\v 15 So they ordered them to go outside the council, and they conferred with one another,
\v 16 saying, "What shall we do with these men? For that a plain sign has come about through them is clear to everyone living in Jerusalem, and we cannot deny it \add [i.e. the problem is not whether it happened but what follows if it did]\add*.
\v 17 But so that it spreads no further among the people, let us threaten them into speaking no more on this name to anyone at all."
\v 18 And calling them in, they charged them not to speak out or teach at all on the name of Jesus.
\v 19 But Peter and John answered them, "Whether it is right in God's sight to listen to you rather than to God, judge for yourselves;\f + \fr 4:19 \ft The principle is old and well attested in Israel's own story: the midwives who would not kill the infants because they feared God (Exodus 1:17), the three who would not bow to the image (Daniel 3:16-18), Daniel praying at his open window against the king's edict (Daniel 6:10).\f*
\v 20 for we cannot help speaking of what we saw and heard."
\v 21 And after further threats they let them go, finding no way to punish them, because of the people, since they were all glorifying God over what had happened;
\v 22 for the man on whom this sign of healing had come about was more than forty years old \add [i.e. a condition everyone in the city had watched for a lifetime]\add*.
\s1 The Community Prays for Boldness
\p
\v 23 And when they were released they went to their own people and reported all that the chief priests and the elders had said to them.
\v 24 And when they heard it, with one accord they lifted up their voice to God and said, "Master\f + \fr 4:24 \ft Greek \tl despotēs\tl*, the word for an owner with absolute authority over his household — not the usual word for "Lord." They are addressing the one authority that outranks the court that has just threatened them. NA28 has only "Master"; the majority text and older English versions read "Lord, you are God."\f*, you who made the heaven and the earth and the sea and everything in them,
\v 25 who said through the Holy Spirit by the mouth of our father David, your servant\f + \fr 4:25 \ft The Greek of this clause is rough and overloaded, and it has been left as it stands rather than smoothed out.\f*, 'Why did nations rage and peoples plot empty things?
\v 26 The kings of the earth took their stand, and the rulers were gathered together against the Lord and against his Anointed.'\f + \fr 4:26 \ft Psalm 2:1-2, a psalm for the crowning of a king of David's line, in which the nations conspire against God's anointed and get nowhere.\f*
\v 27 For truly in this city there were gathered against your holy servant Jesus, whom you anointed, both Herod and Pontius Pilate, with nations and peoples of Israel \add [i.e. the kings of the psalm and its rulers, its nations and its peoples, each with a name and a face]\add*,
\v 28 to do whatever your hand and your purpose marked out beforehand to happen.
\v 29 And now, Lord, look on their threats, and give your slaves to speak your word with all boldness \add [i.e. not that the threats be lifted, but that we not be quiet under them]\add*,
\v 30 while you stretch out your hand for healing, and signs and wonders come about through the name of your holy servant Jesus."
\v 31 And when they had prayed, the place where they were gathered was shaken\f + \fr 4:31 \ft Shaking is how the ground answers when God arrives: Sinai "quaked greatly" (Exodus 19:18), the doorposts shook at the seraph's cry (Isaiah 6:4), the earth trembled (Judges 5:4; Psalm 68:8).\f*, and they were all filled with the Holy Spirit and were speaking the word of God with boldness \add [i.e. the exact thing they had asked for, and nothing else]\add*.
\s1 Everything Held in Common
\p
\v 32 And the full number of those who trusted were of one heart and soul, and not one of them said that anything of his belongings was his own, but they had everything in common.
\v 33 And with great power the apostles were giving their witness of the rising of the Lord Jesus, and great favor was on them all.
\v 34 For there was no one needy among them\f + \fr 4:34 \ft "There will be no one in need among you" is Deuteronomy 15:4, the condition Moses said Israel would live in if it kept the commands about debt release and open-handedness. The author is saying that among these people it has actually happened.\f*; for as many as were owners of lands or houses would sell them and bring the proceeds of what was sold
\v 35 and lay it at the apostles' feet, and it was distributed to each as anyone had need \add [i.e. the measure of distribution was need, and the selling was nobody's rule but their own]\add*.
\v 36 And Joseph\f + \fr 4:36 \ft NA28 reads "Joseph"; most older English versions read "Joses."\f*, who was surnamed Barnabas by the apostles — which translated is "son of encouragement" — a Levite, a Cypriot by birth\f + \fr 4:36 \ft A Levite owning land is worth noticing: the tribe of Levi was given no territory in the land, because "the LORD is their inheritance" (Deuteronomy 18:2). Diaspora practice had loosened that long since, but the man from the landless tribe selling his field still reads as a return to type. He will be central to what follows — vouching for Saul when no one else will (9:27), sent to Antioch (11:22), and Paul's partner on the first journey out (13:2).\f*,
\v 37 owned a field, and he sold it and brought the money and laid it at the apostles' feet.

\c 5
\s1 Ananias and Sapphira
\p
\v 1 But a certain man named Ananias, with Sapphira his wife, sold a piece of property,
\v 2 and he held back part of the price\f + \fr 5:2 \ft The verb is the one the Greek Old Testament uses of Achan at Jericho, who "held back" some of what had been devoted to God and hid it in his tent (Joshua 7:1). That story stands at the beginning of Israel's life in the land, and it ends in sudden death. The reader is meant to hear it here.\f*, his wife knowing it too, and bringing a certain part he laid it at the apostles' feet \add [i.e. doing exactly what Barnabas had done, and meaning it to look like the same thing]\add*.
\v 3 But Peter said, "Ananias, why has Satan filled your heart\f + \fr 5:3 \ft The same verb used of the Spirit filling the community (2:4; 4:31). One heart or the other gets filled, and Peter's question — "why" — assumes Ananias could have refused.\f* to lie to the Holy Spirit and hold back part of the price of the land?
\v 4 While it stayed unsold, did it not stay yours? And when it was sold, was it not in your own authority? \add [i.e. no one required you to sell it, and no one required you to give it]\add* Why is it that you put this deed in your heart? You did not lie to men but to God."
\v 5 And when Ananias heard these words he fell down and breathed his last; and great fear came on all who heard.
\v 6 And the younger men rose and wrapped him up, and carried him out and buried him \add [i.e. at once, as the day required]\add*.
\p
\v 7 And there was an interval of about three hours, and his wife came in, not knowing what had happened.
\v 8 And Peter said to her, "Tell me whether you sold the land for so much." And she said, "Yes, for so much." \add [i.e. she was handed the chance to break with the lie, and she did not take it]\add*
\v 9 And Peter said to her, "Why is it that you agreed together to put the Spirit of the Lord to the test?\f + \fr 5:9 \ft The word for testing God is the word used of Israel at Massah, where they demanded proof that the LORD was among them or not (Exodus 17:2, 7). "You shall not put the LORD your God to the test, as you tested him at Massah" (Deuteronomy 6:16). To test God is to act on the assumption that he may not really be there.\f* Look — the feet of those who buried your husband are at the door, and they will carry you out."
\v 10 And at once she fell at his feet and breathed her last; and the young men came in and found her dead, and they carried her out and buried her beside her husband.
\v 11 And great fear came on the whole assembly\f + \fr 5:11 \ft Greek \tl ekklēsia\tl*, the first time the word is used of this community in the book. It is not a new coinage: in the Greek Old Testament it is the standard word for the assembly of Israel — the people gathered at Sinai (Deuteronomy 4:10; 9:10), the congregation in worship (Psalm 22:22). It is rendered "assembly" throughout, and the same word is used of Israel in the wilderness at 7:38.\f* and on all who heard these things.
\s1 Signs, and Crowds from the Towns Around
\p
\v 12 And through the hands of the apostles many signs and wonders were happening among the people. And they were all together with one accord in Solomon's colonnade;
\v 13 but of the rest no one dared to attach himself to them — and yet the people spoke well of them \add [i.e. admired at a distance, after what had happened to Ananias]\add*.
\v 14 And more than ever those who trusted were being added to the Lord, crowds of both men and women,
\v 15 so that they would even carry the sick out into the streets and lay them on cots and mats, so that as Peter came by at least his shadow might fall on one of them\f + \fr 5:15 \ft The text does not say the shadow healed anyone. It says what the people hoped. Compare the crowds reaching for the edge of Jesus's cloak (Luke 6:19; 8:44) — the same expectation, now attached to his witnesses.\f*.
\v 16 And the crowd from the towns around Jerusalem also came together, bringing sick people and those troubled by unclean spirits, and they were all healed.
\s1 Arrested Again, and Let Out by Night
\p
\v 17 But the high priest rose up, and all who were with him — that is, the party of the Sadducees — and they were filled with jealousy,
\v 18 and they laid hands on the apostles \add [i.e. not two of them this time, but all of them]\add* and put them in the public prison.
\v 19 But during the night an angel of the Lord opened the doors of the prison and led them out and said,
\v 20 "Go and stand in the temple and speak to the people all the words of this Life."\f + \fr 5:20 \ft The instruction is not "escape" or "lie low." It sends them back to the exact spot they were taken from, to do the exact thing they were taken for. The council's order and the angel's order cannot both be obeyed, which is the point Peter makes in verse 29.\f*
\v 21 And when they heard it they went into the temple at dawn and were teaching. And the high priest came, and those with him, and they called the council together — the whole senate of the sons of Israel — and sent to the jail to have them brought.
\v 22 But the officers who came did not find them in the prison; and they went back and reported,
\v 23 saying, "We found the jail locked in complete safety, and the guards standing at the doors\f + \fr 5:23 \ft "Outside" before "the doors" is in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*; but when we opened it we found no one inside."
\v 24 And when the captain of the temple and the chief priests heard these words, they were at a loss about them, wondering what this could come to.
\v 25 But someone came and reported to them, "Look — the men you put in the prison are standing in the temple and teaching the people."
\v 26 Then the captain went off with the officers and brought them, but without force, for they were afraid of being stoned by the people \add [i.e. the crowd now stood between the council and the men it had jailed]\add*.
\s1 We Must Obey God Rather Than Men
\p
\v 27 And they brought them and stood them before the council, and the high priest questioned them,
\v 28 saying, "We gave you strict orders not to teach on this name, and look — you have filled Jerusalem with your teaching, and you intend to bring this man's blood on us."\f + \fr 5:28 \ft He will not say the name, even while forbidding it — "this man." And "to bring blood upon" is the legal idiom for fixing the guilt of a killing on someone (Genesis 42:22; 2 Samuel 1:16). His objection is not that the man did not die; it is that the council is being held to account for it.\f*
\v 29 But Peter and the apostles answered, "One must obey God rather than men.
\v 30 The God of our fathers raised Jesus, whom you laid hands on and hanged on a tree\f + \fr 5:30 \ft Greek \tl xylon\tl*, which is plainly "wood" and is the word of Deuteronomy 21:22-23 — "anyone hung on a tree is under God's curse." Peter chooses the Torah's own word for a cursed death and then says in the same breath that God lifted this man to his right hand. The same word is used again at 10:39 and 13:29.\f*.
\v 31 This one God lifted high at his right hand as leader and rescuer, to give turning back to Israel and forgiveness of sins \add [i.e. the turning itself is the gift, and not the price of it]\add*.
\v 32 And we are witnesses of these words, and so is the Holy Spirit, whom God gave to those who obey him."
\s1 Gamaliel's Counsel
\p
\v 33 And when they heard this they were sawn through\f + \fr 5:33 \ft The verb means literally to be sawn in two. It describes rage, not remorse — a different word from the one at 2:37, where the crowd was pierced to the heart and asked what to do. Most versions have "they were cut to the heart." The same word returns at 7:54, with the same result.\f*, and they were set on doing away with them.
\v 34 But a certain Pharisee named Gamaliel stood up in the council, a teacher of the law honored by all the people\f + \fr 5:34 \ft Gamaliel the Elder, grandson of Hillel, the most respected legal authority of his generation; Paul says he was trained at his feet (22:3). That a Pharisee should restrain a Sadducean council on this matter is unsurprising — the Pharisees held that the dead are raised, and it is the preaching of a resurrection that the Sadducees cannot tolerate (4:2; 23:6-8).\f*, and gave orders to put the men outside for a little while.
\v 35 And he said to them, "Men of Israel, take care what you are about to do with these men.
\v 36 For before these days Theudas rose up, saying that he was somebody, and a number of men, about four hundred, attached themselves to him; he was done away with, and all who were persuaded by him were scattered and came to nothing.
\v 37 After him Judas the Galilean rose up in the days of the registration\f + \fr 5:37 \ft The census under Quirinius, about the year 6, which triggered a revolt in Galilee over paying tribute to Rome. Josephus treats Judas as the founder of the movement that ends in the war of 66-70.\f* and drew people away after him; he too perished, and all who were persuaded by him were scattered.
\v 38 And in the present case I tell you, keep away from these men and let them alone; for if this plan or this work is from men, it will be pulled down;
\v 39 but if it is from God, you will not be able to pull them down — you might even be found fighting against God."\f + \fr 5:39 \ft Gamaliel is not endorsing the movement. He is proposing that time will sort it out, and that the council risks nothing by waiting and everything by acting. The reader of this book has already been shown the answer to his question.\f* And they were persuaded by him;
\v 40 and calling in the apostles they beat them\f + \fr 5:40 \ft The synagogue flogging, thirty-nine strokes — the punishment Paul says he received five times (2 Corinthians 11:24). In the previous chapter the council could only threaten; now it has moved to the whip.\f* and charged them not to speak on the name of Jesus, and released them.
\v 41 So they went out from the council's presence, rejoicing that they had been counted worthy to be dishonored for the Name\f + \fr 5:41 \ft NA28 has simply "for the Name," with nothing added; most English versions supply "of Jesus" or "of him." The bare phrase is the point — by now there is only one Name that needs no saying. Compare Luke 6:22-23: "Blessed are you when men hate you and cast out your name as evil... leap for joy in that day."\f*.
\v 42 And every day, in the temple and from house to house, they did not stop teaching and announcing the good news that the Christ is Jesus \add [i.e. two orders to stop, a jail, and a flogging, and not one day's pause]\add*.

\c 6
\s1 The Hellenists' Complaint and the Seven
\p
\v 1 Now in those days, as the disciples were multiplying, there arose a grumbling\f + \fr 6:1 \ft The Greek word is the one the Greek Old Testament uses for Israel's murmuring in the wilderness — over water at Marah and Rephidim, over bread and meat in the desert of Sin (Exodus 15:24; 16:2; 17:3; Numbers 14:2). It is always about provision, and always aimed at the leadership.\f* of the Hellenists against the Hebrews\f + \fr 6:1 \ft Both groups are Jews and both are believers. The Hellenists are Greek-speaking, most of them from the dispersion — Alexandria, Cyrene, the cities of Asia; the Hebrews speak Aramaic and are native to the land. The line is language and culture, not doctrine.\f*, because their widows were being overlooked in the daily service \add [i.e. the very people the law puts first were being passed over]\add*.
\v 2 So the twelve called the full number of the disciples together and said, "It is not pleasing that we should leave the word of God to wait on tables.
\v 3 So look out, brothers, for seven men from among you who are well spoken of, full of the Spirit\f + \fr 6:3 \ft NA28 has "full of the Spirit and of wisdom"; many English versions read "the Holy Spirit."\f* and of wisdom, whom we will set over this need;
\v 4 but we will hold fast to prayer and to the service of the word."\f + \fr 6:4 \ft The same word — service — is used of waiting on tables in verse 1 and of the ministry of the word here. The apostles are not ranking the two tasks; they are saying that one pair of hands cannot do both without dropping one. Moses was told the same thing by Jethro (Exodus 18:17-23) and by God (Numbers 11:14-17).\f*
\v 5 And the word pleased the whole crowd, and they chose Stephen, a man full of trust and of the Holy Spirit, and Philip and Prochorus and Nicanor and Timon and Parmenas and Nicolaus, a proselyte of Antioch\f + \fr 6:5 \ft Every one of the seven names is Greek, and the last man is a Gentile who had converted to Judaism. The complaint came from the Hellenists, and the whole administration of the fund is handed to Hellenists. Antioch, named here in passing, will later become the base of the mission to the nations (11:19-26; 13:1-3).\f*,
\v 6 whom they set before the apostles; and they prayed and laid their hands on them\f + \fr 6:6 \ft The gesture of commissioning in Israel: Moses laid hands on Joshua and gave him his charge (Numbers 27:23), and Israel laid hands on the Levites when they were set apart (Numbers 8:10).\f*.
\v 7 And the word of God kept growing, and the number of the disciples in Jerusalem was multiplying greatly, and a great crowd of the priests were obeying the trust \add [i.e. the temple's own servants, joining the movement that is about to be charged with attacking the temple]\add*.
\s1 Stephen Seized
\p
\v 8 And Stephen, full of favor and power\f + \fr 6:8 \ft NA28 reads "full of favor and power"; the majority text, and most older English versions, have "full of faith and power."\f*, was doing great wonders and signs among the people.
\v 9 But some of those from the synagogue called the Synagogue of the Freedmen\f + \fr 6:9 \ft Greek \tl Libertinoi\tl*, the Latin word for freed slaves. These are Jews taken captive — most likely in Pompey's conquest of Jerusalem in 63 BC — and later freed, or their descendants, who had their own congregation in the city. Note where they come from: Cyrene, Alexandria, Cilicia, Asia. They are Greek-speaking men of the dispersion, exactly like Stephen. The fight is inside the Hellenist community, not across it.\f*, and Cyrenians and Alexandrians, and those from Cilicia and Asia, rose up and argued with Stephen,
\v 10 and they were not strong enough to stand against the wisdom and the Spirit by which he spoke.\f + \fr 6:10 \ft "I will give you a mouth and wisdom which none of your adversaries will be able to contradict or resist" (Luke 21:15). This is the first time in the book that promise is kept for someone outside the twelve.\f*
\v 11 Then they put men up to say\f + \fr 6:11 \ft The verb means to plant, to suborn — the witnesses are coached. The author tells the reader the charges are manufactured before he tells him what they are.\f*, "We have heard him speaking words\f + \fr 6:11 \ft "Blasphemous" before "words" is in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f* against Moses and God."
\v 12 And they stirred up the people and the elders and the scribes, and they came upon him and seized him and brought him to the council \add [i.e. and the crowd, which had shielded the apostles until now, was turned]\add*,
\v 13 and they set up false witnesses who said, "This man does not stop speaking words against this holy place and the law;
\v 14 for we have heard him saying that this Jesus the Nazarene will tear down this place and change the customs that Moses handed on to us."\f + \fr 6:14 \ft Jesus did say the temple would come down, not one stone left on another (Luke 21:5-6). The distortion is in who does it and why: a prophet's warning of judgment is recast as a man's threat to demolish God's house.\f*
\v 15 And all who sat in the council fixed their eyes on him and saw his face like the face of an angel.\f + \fr 6:15 \ft When Moses came down from the mountain with the law, "the skin of his face shone," and the people were afraid to come near him (Exodus 34:29-30). The man on trial for speaking against Moses is marked in front of the court the way Moses was marked.\f*

\c 7
\s1 Stephen: A God Who Was Never Tied to a Place
\p
\v 1 And the high priest said, "Is this so?"
\v 2 And he said, "Men, brothers and fathers, listen. The God of glory\f + \fr 7:2 \ft "The God of glory thunders" (Psalm 29:3) — a psalm about a voice that shakes the wilderness and strips the forests. Stephen names God at his least containable and then puts him in Mesopotamia.\f* appeared to our father Abraham while he was in Mesopotamia, before he settled in Haran \add [i.e. in pagan country, long before there was a holy place anywhere]\add*,
\v 3 and said to him, 'Go out from your land and from your kindred, and come into the land that I will show you.'
\v 4 Then he went out from the land of the Chaldeans and settled in Haran. And from there, after his father died, God moved him into this land in which you now live.
\v 5 And he gave him no inheritance in it, not even a foot's length\f + \fr 7:5 \ft The phrase is from Deuteronomy 2:5, where God tells Israel it will get none of Esau's territory, not so much as a footprint. The man who received the promise of this land owned none of it. He held a word, not a deed.\f*; but he promised to give it to him as a holding, and to his seed after him — and he had no child.
\v 6 And God spoke like this: that his seed would be a sojourner in a land not their own, and they would enslave it and mistreat it four hundred years.
\v 7 'And the nation to which they will be slaves I will judge,' said God, 'and after that they will come out and serve me in this place.'\f + \fr 7:7 \ft Genesis 15:13-14, with the closing phrase from Exodus 3:12 — where "this place" is the mountain in the wilderness, not Jerusalem.\f*
\v 8 And he gave him a covenant of circumcision; and so Abraham fathered Isaac and circumcised him on the eighth day, and Isaac fathered Jacob, and Jacob the twelve patriarchs \add [i.e. a mark carried in the body, which goes wherever the man goes]\add*.
\s1 Joseph, Sold by His Brothers
\p
\v 9 And the patriarchs, being jealous of Joseph, sold him into Egypt; and God was with him\f + \fr 7:9 \ft Four words that carry the whole speech. The covenant family threw him out; God went with him. Presence follows the rejected one, not the ones doing the rejecting.\f*,
\v 10 and rescued him out of all his troubles, and gave him favor and wisdom before Pharaoh king of Egypt, and he appointed him ruler over Egypt and over all his household.
\v 11 And a famine came over all Egypt and Canaan, and great trouble, and our fathers could not find food \add [i.e. the promised land fed no one that year either]\add*.
\v 12 But when Jacob heard that there was grain in Egypt, he sent out our fathers the first time.
\v 13 And on the second time Joseph was made known to his brothers, and Joseph's family became plain to Pharaoh \add [i.e. the first time they stood in front of him and did not know him]\add*.
\v 14 And Joseph sent and called for Jacob his father and all his kindred, seventy-five souls.\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Seventy-five is the figure in the Greek Old Testament at Genesis 46:27 and Exodus 1:5, which counts Joseph's grandsons born in Egypt; the Hebrew text has seventy. Stephen quotes the Greek, the Bible of the dispersion.\f*
\v 15 And Jacob went down into Egypt, and he died, he and our fathers,
\v 16 and they were carried back to Shechem and laid in the tomb that Abraham had bought for a price in silver from the sons of Hamor in Shechem.\f + \fr 7:16 \ft Genesis has two purchases: Abraham bought the cave at Hebron from Ephron the Hittite (Genesis 23:16-20), and Jacob bought the plot at Shechem from the sons of Hamor (Genesis 33:19), where Joseph's bones were later buried (Joshua 24:32). The speech as it stands has Abraham buying at Shechem, and it has been rendered as it stands rather than corrected.\f*
\s1 Moses, Refused the First Time
\p
\v 17 But as the time of the promise drew near, which God had agreed with Abraham, the people grew and multiplied in Egypt,
\v 18 until another king rose over Egypt who did not know Joseph.
\v 19 This man dealt shrewdly with our kindred and mistreated our fathers, making them expose their infants so that they would not stay alive.
\v 20 At that time Moses was born, and he was beautiful to God\f + \fr 7:20 \ft Greek \tl asteios tō theō\tl*, "beautiful to God." It is a Hebrew way of saying exceedingly beautiful — as one might say "a city great to God" for a very great city (Jonah 3:3). Kept literal here.\f*. He was brought up three months in his father's house,
\v 21 and when he was exposed, Pharaoh's daughter took him up and brought him up as her own son \add [i.e. the man who would break Egypt was reared inside Pharaoh's own house]\add*.
\v 22 And Moses was trained in all the wisdom of the Egyptians, and he was powerful in his words and works.\f + \fr 7:22 \ft The same phrase the two on the Emmaus road used of Jesus — "a prophet powerful in deed and word" (Luke 24:19).\f*
\p
\v 23 And when the time of his fortieth year was filled full, it came up into his heart to visit his brothers, the sons of Israel.
\v 24 And seeing one of them being wronged, he defended him and got justice for the one who was being crushed, striking down the Egyptian.
\v 25 And he supposed his brothers would understand that God was giving them rescue through his hand, but they did not understand \add [i.e. the deliverer was standing in front of them and they could not see it]\add*.
\v 26 And on the next day he appeared to them while they were fighting, and he tried to bring them together in peace, saying, 'Men, you are brothers. Why do you wrong each other?'
\v 27 But the one who was wronging his neighbor pushed him away and said, 'Who appointed you a ruler and a judge over us?
\v 28 Do you want to do away with me the way you did away with the Egyptian yesterday?'
\v 29 And at this word Moses fled and became a sojourner in the land of Midian, where he fathered two sons.
\p
\v 30 And when forty years were filled full, an angel\f + \fr 7:30 \ft "Of the Lord" after "an angel" is in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f* appeared to him in the wilderness of Mount Sinai in the flame of a burning bush.
\v 31 And when Moses saw it he marveled at the sight; and as he came near to look, the voice of the Lord came:
\v 32 'I am the God of your fathers, the God of Abraham and Isaac and Jacob.'\f + \fr 7:32 \ft NA28 has the compressed form given here. The majority text repeats "the God of" before each name, as most older English versions do.\f* And Moses began to tremble and did not dare to look.
\v 33 And the Lord said to him, 'Untie the sandal from your feet, for the place on which you stand is holy ground.\f + \fr 7:33 \ft This is the pivot of the whole speech. A patch of empty desert in Midian is holy — not because anything was built there, not because a priest consecrated it, but because God is standing on it. Holiness comes from the presence, and the presence goes where it likes.\f*
\v 34 I have surely seen the mistreatment of my people in Egypt, and I have heard their groaning, and I have come down to take them out. And now come, I will send you into Egypt.'
\s1 The One They Refused, God Sent
\p
\v 35 This Moses, whom they denied, saying, 'Who appointed you a ruler and a judge?' — this man God has sent as both ruler and redeemer, with the hand of the angel who appeared to him in the bush \add [i.e. the question they asked to disqualify him, God answered by commissioning him]\add*.
\v 36 This man led them out, doing wonders and signs in the land of Egypt and at the Red Sea and in the wilderness for forty years.
\v 37 This is the Moses who said to the sons of Israel, 'God will raise up for you a prophet like me from among your brothers.'\f + \fr 7:37 \ft Deuteronomy 18:15, the same verse Peter quoted at 3:22. "Him you shall hear" is added at the end of the verse in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*
\v 38 This is the one who was in the assembly in the wilderness\f + \fr 7:38 \ft Greek \tl ekklēsia\tl*, the same word used of the community of Christ throughout this book (5:11; 8:1; 9:31; 20:28). It is rendered "assembly" in both cases, because it is one word: Israel gathered in the desert, and this gathering now.\f* with the angel who spoke to him on Mount Sinai and with our fathers, who received living words to give to us \add [i.e. the law, which I am charged with despising, and which he calls living]\add*,
\v 39 to whom our fathers would not become obedient; but they pushed him away and turned back in their hearts to Egypt \add [i.e. their feet had come out; their hearts had not]\add*,
\v 40 saying to Aaron, 'Make us gods who will go in front of us; for this Moses, who led us out of the land of Egypt — we do not know what has happened to him.'
\v 41 And in those days they made a calf and brought a sacrifice up to the idol, and were glad in the works of their hands \add [i.e. delighted with a thing they had manufactured]\add*.
\v 42 But God turned away and gave them over to serve the host of heaven, as it is written in the book of the prophets: 'Did you bring me slain beasts and sacrifices forty years in the wilderness, house of Israel?
\v 43 And you took up the tent of Moloch and the star of the god Rephan, the figures you made to bow down to them. And I will move you out beyond Babylon.'\f + \fr 7:43 \ft Amos 5:25-27, quoted from the Greek, which reads "beyond Babylon" where the Hebrew has "beyond Damascus." Note that Israel carried two tents through the wilderness: God's, and Moloch's. Moloch was the god whose worship took children (Leviticus 18:21; 2 Kings 23:10).\f*
\s1 The Tent, the House, and the Most High
\p
\v 44 Our fathers had the tent of the testimony in the wilderness, just as the one who spoke to Moses ordered him to make it according to the pattern he had seen \add [i.e. God's own design, and built to be carried]\add*.
\v 45 And our fathers, receiving it in turn, brought it in with Joshua when they took over the land from the nations whom God drove out from before the face of our fathers, until the days of David \add [i.e. four hundred years, and God still living in a tent]\add*,
\v 46 who found favor before God and asked to find a dwelling for the house of Jacob\f + \fr 7:46 \ft NA28 reads "a dwelling for the house of Jacob"; most English versions read "for the God of Jacob."\f*.
\v 47 But it was Solomon who built him a house.
\v 48 Yet the Most High does not live in things made by hands\f + \fr 7:48 \ft Greek \tl cheiropoiētos\tl*, "handmade," which is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for idols (Isaiah 46:6). Stephen has just used the same idea of the golden calf — "the works of their hands" (verse 41). He is not saying the temple was a sin; Solomon said the same thing when he dedicated it: "will God really dwell on the earth? Heaven and the highest heaven cannot contain you, much less this house I have built" (1 Kings 8:27). He is saying the building never contained God, and treating it as though it did is the old error in a new form.\f*, as the prophet says:
\v 49 'Heaven is my throne, and the earth a footstool for my feet. What kind of house will you build me, says the Lord, or what is the place of my rest?
\v 50 Did not my hand make all these things?'\f + \fr 7:50 \ft Isaiah 66:1-2.\f*
\s1 You Are Doing What Your Fathers Did
\p
\v 51 You stiff-necked people, uncircumcised in hearts and ears!\f + \fr 7:51 \ft Both phrases are God's own words about Israel. "A stiff-necked people" is what he calls them after the golden calf (Exodus 33:3, 5; Deuteronomy 9:6). "Circumcise your heart and stiffen your neck no longer" is Deuteronomy 10:16; "their ears are uncircumcised and they cannot listen" is Jeremiah 6:10. Stephen is not inventing an insult; he is quoting the indictment already on the record.\f* You always fall against the Holy Spirit\f + \fr 7:51 \ft Greek \tl antipiptete\tl*, "you fall against, you throw yourselves at." Most versions have "you resist."\f* — as your fathers did, so do you.
\v 52 Which of the prophets did your fathers not persecute? And they killed those who announced beforehand the coming of the Righteous One, whom you have now handed over and murdered —
\v 53 you who received the law by the arrangements of angels, and did not keep it."\f + \fr 7:53 \ft That the law was given through angels is the reading of Deuteronomy 33:2 in Greek and a standing tradition of the period (compare Galatians 3:19; Hebrews 2:2). The charge against Stephen was that he spoke against the law. His last sentence hands the charge back: you are the ones who did not keep it.\f*
\s1 The Stoning of Stephen
\p
\v 54 And when they heard this they were sawn through in their hearts, and they ground their teeth at him.
\v 55 But he, full of the Holy Spirit, gazed into heaven and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at the right hand of God\f + \fr 7:55 \ft Everywhere else he is seated there (Psalm 110:1; Acts 2:34; Hebrews 1:3). Here he is on his feet. In a courtroom a man stands to testify or to give the verdict. While the court below condemns Stephen, the one they killed is standing up.\f*,
\v 56 and he said, "Look — I see the heavens opened up, and the son of man standing at the right hand of God."\f + \fr 7:56 \ft "Son of man" is Jesus's own name for himself in the Gospels, and this is the one place in the New Testament where anyone else uses it. It comes from Daniel 7:13-14, where one like a son of man comes with the clouds to the Ancient of Days and is given dominion over all peoples and nations.\f*
\v 57 But they cried out with a loud voice and covered their ears \add [i.e. doing with their hands what he had just accused their hearts of]\add*, and rushed at him with one accord,
\v 58 and throwing him outside the city\f + \fr 7:58 \ft The law puts a blasphemer outside the camp to be stoned, and requires that the witnesses throw first (Leviticus 24:14; Deuteronomy 17:7). That is why they take their coats off.\f* they stoned him. And the witnesses laid their clothes at the feet of a young man called Saul.
\v 59 And they stoned Stephen while he called out and said, "Lord Jesus, receive my spirit."\f + \fr 7:59 \ft The Greek says only that he was "calling out" and does not name whom he called on; the object is not supplied here, as most versions supply it. The prayer itself is Jesus's own dying prayer with the address changed: "Father, into your hands I entrust my spirit" (Luke 23:46, quoting Psalm 31:5).\f*
\v 60 And falling on his knees he cried out with a loud voice, "Lord, do not set this sin against them."\f + \fr 7:60 \ft As Jesus prayed from the cross, "Father, forgive them; they do not know what they are doing" (Luke 23:34). The first man to die for this message dies praying the prayer his master prayed.\f* And when he had said this he fell asleep.

\c 8
\s1 Persecution Scatters the Church
\p
\v 1 And Saul was in full agreement with his killing. And on that day there arose a great persecution against the assembly in Jerusalem, and they were all scattered\f + \fr 8:1 \ft The verb is the root of the word "dispersion" — what happened to Israel in exile. It is also the ordinary word for sowing seed by throwing it wide. The persecutors are doing the first thing; the book is about to show the second.\f* through the regions of Judea and Samaria, except the apostles \add [i.e. into exactly the two regions named in the commission, and by force rather than by obedience]\add*.
\v 2 And devout men buried Stephen and made a great lament over him.
\v 3 But Saul was ravaging the assembly\f + \fr 8:3 \ft The verb is used in the Greek Old Testament of the wild boar that tears up the vineyard (Psalm 80:13). Paul describes the same period himself: "I persecuted this Way to the death, binding and handing over to prisons both men and women" (22:4); "I was persecuting the assembly of God beyond measure and trying to destroy it" (Galatians 1:13).\f*, going into house after house and dragging out both men and women and handing them over to prison.
\s1 Philip in Samaria
\p
\v 4 So those who had been scattered went through the land announcing the good news of the word \add [i.e. the silencing produced more speakers, not fewer]\add*.
\v 5 And Philip\f + \fr 8:5 \ft Not the apostle of that name but the Philip appointed with Stephen among the seven (6:5). The men set over the food distribution turn out to be the ones who carry the message out of the city.\f* went down to the city of Samaria\f + \fr 8:5 \ft NA28 reads "the city of Samaria"; some witnesses have "a city of Samaria." — Samaria was the capital of the old northern kingdom. After Assyria deported its people in 722 BC and settled foreigners in their place, the population that emerged kept the five books of Moses, rejected the prophets, and worshiped on Mount Gerizim instead of in Jerusalem. Jews and Samaritans had despised each other for centuries; "Jews have no dealings with Samaritans" (John 4:9). This is the first boundary the message crosses.\f* and proclaimed the Christ to them.\f + \fr 8:5 \ft The Samaritans, who held only the law of Moses, expected the prophet like Moses of Deuteronomy 18:15-18. Proclaiming "the Christ" to them lands on the one messianic expectation they had.\f*
\v 6 And the crowds with one accord gave attention to what Philip said, as they heard and saw the signs he was doing.
\v 7 For many of those who had unclean spirits — they came out crying with a loud voice — and many who were paralyzed and lame were healed;
\v 8 and there was much joy in that city.
\s1 Simon the Magician
\p
\v 9 But a certain man named Simon had been in the city before this, practicing magic\f + \fr 8:9 \ft The law forbids it outright — "there shall not be found among you anyone who practices divination, or a soothsayer, or a sorcerer" (Deuteronomy 18:10). The author keeps a hard line between Simon's \tl mageia\tl* and Philip's \tl sēmeia\tl*, sorcery and signs. Both draw crowds; they do not come from the same place.\f* and astounding the nation of Samaria, saying he himself was somebody great.
\v 10 To him they all gave attention, from small to great, saying, "This man is the power of God that is called Great."\f + \fr 8:10 \ft NA28 has the title in this form; the majority text has only "the great power of God." Whatever the exact wording, the people are assigning him a divine title, not merely admiring him.\f*
\v 11 And they gave attention to him because for a long time he had astounded them with his magic.
\v 12 But when they trusted Philip as he announced the good news about the kingdom of God and the name of Jesus Christ, they were immersed, both men and women.
\v 13 And Simon himself also trusted, and after being immersed he held close to Philip; and seeing signs and great works of power happening, he was astounded \add [i.e. the man who had astounded everyone was now the one astounded]\add*.
\s1 Peter and John Come Down
\p
\v 14 And when the apostles in Jerusalem heard that Samaria had welcomed the word of God, they sent Peter and John to them,
\v 15 who came down and prayed for them that they might receive the Holy Spirit;
\v 16 for he had not yet fallen on any of them, but they had only been immersed in the name of the Lord Jesus.\f + \fr 8:16 \ft This is the one place in the book where belief and immersion happen and the Spirit does not come with them (contrast 2:38; 10:44). The author gives no reason. What the delay accomplishes is visible in the narrative: the first community outside Judaism receives the Spirit through the hands of the same men who received it at Pentecost, so that there is one movement and not two — a Jewish one and a Samaritan one with nothing between them.\f*
\v 17 Then they laid their hands on them, and they were receiving the Holy Spirit.
\v 18 And when Simon saw that the Spirit\f + \fr 8:18 \ft NA28 reads "the Spirit"; most older English versions read "the Holy Spirit."\f* was given through the laying on of the apostles' hands, he brought them money,
\v 19 saying, "Give me this authority too, so that whoever I lay my hands on may receive the Holy Spirit."\f + \fr 8:19 \ft Notice what he asks for. Not the Spirit — the authority to hand it out. He is asking to be put back where Philip found him, the man everyone attends to. And he asks for it in the only terms his old trade knew: spells and techniques were bought and sold among practitioners for a price.\f*
\v 20 But Peter said to him, "May your silver go to destruction with you, because you thought you could get the gift of God with money \add [i.e. a thing stops being a gift the moment it has a price]\add*.
\v 21 You have no part or share in this matter\f + \fr 8:21 \ft "Part or share" is the phrase used in the law for a tribe's allotment in the land — the Levites have "no part or inheritance" among their brothers (Deuteronomy 12:12; 14:27). Peter is using the language of being left out of the inheritance.\f*, for your heart is not straight before God.
\v 22 So turn back from this wickedness of yours, and beg the Lord — if perhaps the intention of your heart may be forgiven you\f + \fr 8:22 \ft "If perhaps" is in the Greek. Everywhere else in this book forgiveness is held out flatly to those who turn (2:38; 3:19). Here it is held out with a question mark over it, and the text does not remove the question mark.\f*;
\v 23 for I see you are in the gall of bitterness and the bond of unrighteousness."\f + \fr 8:23 \ft "A root bearing gall and bitterness" is Deuteronomy 29:18, describing a man inside the covenant community who hears the terms and privately decides he will do as he likes. Such a man is not merely lost himself; he poisons what he grows in.\f*
\v 24 And Simon answered, "You beg the Lord for me, so that nothing you have said may come on me."\f + \fr 8:24 \ft He does not say he repents. He asks that the consequences be kept off him. The author leaves him there and never mentions him again.\f*
\v 25 So when they had borne witness and spoken the word of the Lord, they went back to Jerusalem, announcing good news to many villages of the Samaritans \add [i.e. the two who had once wanted fire called down on a Samaritan village, preaching their way through them]\add*.
\s1 The Ethiopian on the Gaza Road
\p
\v 26 But an angel of the Lord spoke to Philip: "Get up and go south, to the road that goes down from Jerusalem to Gaza." (This is a desert road.) \add [i.e. out of a city full of people who were listening, onto an empty road]\add*
\v 27 And he got up and went. And look — an Ethiopian man, a eunuch, a court official of Candace queen of the Ethiopians\f + \fr 8:27 \ft Ethiopia here is the kingdom of Meroe, up the Nile south of Egypt — for a Mediterranean reader, the southern edge of the world. "Candace" is not a name but the title of its queen mother, as "Pharaoh" was of Egypt's kings. The man runs the royal treasury.\f*, who was over all her treasure, had come to Jerusalem to bow down\f + \fr 8:27 \ft He has travelled well over a thousand miles to worship at a temple whose inner courts he could not enter. "No man who is emasculated may enter the assembly of the LORD" (Deuteronomy 23:1). He is a worshiper who is barred from worshiping, and he is going home.\f*,
\v 28 and was going back, sitting in his chariot and reading the prophet Isaiah\f + \fr 8:28 \ft The one prophet who addresses his case directly: "let not the eunuch say, I am a dry tree. For thus says the LORD: to the eunuchs who keep my sabbaths and hold fast my covenant, I will give in my house and within my walls a memorial and a name better than sons and daughters, an everlasting name that shall not be cut off" (Isaiah 56:3-5). A scroll of that size was a costly thing to own, and he is reading it aloud, as everyone did.\f*.
\v 29 And the Spirit said to Philip, "Go up and join this chariot."
\v 30 And Philip ran up and heard him reading the prophet Isaiah, and said, "Do you really know what you are reading?"\f + \fr 8:30 \ft There is a play on words in the Greek that English cannot keep: \tl ginōskeis ha anaginōskeis\tl* — do you know what you are knowing-again, that is, reading.\f*
\v 31 And he said, "How could I, unless someone guides me?" And he called Philip to come up and sit with him.
\v 32 And the passage of scripture he was reading was this: "Like a sheep he was led to slaughter, and like a lamb silent before the one who shears it, so he does not open his mouth.
\v 33 In his humiliation his justice was taken away. Who will tell of his generation? For his life is taken from the earth."\f + \fr 8:33 \ft Isaiah 53:7-8, in the Greek. The passage he has stopped at is the one that had divided Jewish readers for centuries: an unnamed sufferer, killed without justice, whose death somehow does something for others. It is the question in verse 34 that every reader of this chapter asked.\f*
\v 34 And the eunuch answered Philip and said, "I ask you, about whom does the prophet say this — about himself, or about someone else?"
\v 35 And Philip opened his mouth, and beginning from this scripture he announced to him the good news of Jesus.
\v 36 And as they went along the road they came to some water, and the eunuch said, "Look — water. What keeps me from being immersed?"\f + \fr 8:36 \ft The question of a man who has spent his life being kept from things. The next verse in most older English Bibles — "If you believe with all your heart, you may," and the eunuch's confession in reply — is not in NA28, and the numbering here runs from 36 to 38.\f*
\v 38 And he ordered the chariot to stop, and they both went down into the water, Philip and the eunuch, and he immersed him \add [i.e. no delay, no apostle sent for, and nothing standing in the way]\add*.
\v 39 And when they came up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord snatched Philip away\f + \fr 8:39 \ft Some Western witnesses expand this to read "the Holy Spirit fell on the eunuch, and an angel of the Lord snatched Philip away." That is not in NA28. — The verb is the one used of Elijah, whom men expected the Spirit to carry off to some unknown place (1 Kings 18:12; 2 Kings 2:16).\f*, and the eunuch did not see him again; and he went on his way rejoicing.
\v 40 But Philip was found at Azotus, and passing through he announced the good news to all the towns until he came to Caesarea\f + \fr 8:40 \ft Azotus is Ashdod, one of the old Philistine cities on the coast. From there the road runs north through Jamnia, Joppa, and Apollonia to Caesarea, the Roman administrative capital. Philip settles there; we find him still in that house twenty years later (21:8).\f*.

\c 9
\s1 Saul on the Road to Damascus
\p
\v 1 But Saul, still breathing threat and murder against the disciples of the Lord, went to the high priest
\v 2 and asked him for letters to Damascus, to the synagogues\f + \fr 9:2 \ft Damascus held a large Jewish population, and its synagogues functioned as community courts with power to discipline their members. Letters from the high priest would ask them to hand over the men he wanted. Whether Rome recognized such extradition is another question; the letters are an appeal to Jewish communal authority, not a warrant.\f*, so that if he found any who were of the Way\f + \fr 9:2 \ft The community's own name for itself, used without explanation here and again at 19:9, 23; 22:4; 24:14, 22. It comes out of Israel's scriptures, where "the way of the LORD" is the manner of life God's people are to walk in (Genesis 18:19; Psalm 1:6; Isaiah 40:3). It names a movement by a road, not by a doctrine.\f*, both men and women, he might bring them bound to Jerusalem.
\v 3 And as he went, it happened that he drew near to Damascus, and suddenly a light out of the sky flashed all around him,
\v 4 and falling to the ground he heard a voice saying to him, "Saul, Saul\f + \fr 9:4 \ft The doubled name is how God calls a man at a turning point: "Abraham, Abraham" at the mountain (Genesis 22:11), "Moses, Moses" at the bush (Exodus 3:4), "Samuel, Samuel" in the night (1 Samuel 3:10). And the form is the Hebrew one, \tl Saoul\tl*, not the Greek — Paul says as much when he tells the story himself (26:14).\f*, why are you persecuting me?"\f + \fr 9:4 \ft Not "my followers." Me. He has been arresting people in Jerusalem and is on the road to arrest more in Damascus, and the voice from heaven counts every one of those arrests as laid on itself.\f*
\v 5 And he said, "Who are you, lord?"\f + \fr 9:5 \ft The Greek \tl kyrie\tl* is both "Lord" and, in ordinary speech, simply "sir." Coming from a man who has just said he does not know who is speaking, it can only be the second, and it is given a small letter here — and again at 10:4 and 22:8 — and a capital once the speaker gives his name.\f* And he said, "I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting.
\v 6 But get up and go into the city, and you will be told what you must do."\f + \fr 9:6 \ft Two familiar lines are missing here in NA28: "It is hard for you to kick against the goads" at the end of verse 5, and "And trembling and astonished he said, Lord, what do you want me to do?" at the start of verse 6. The goads do appear in Paul's own telling of the story at 26:14. — Note also how little he is told. Enough to stop him and move him; nothing about the work itself. That comes through a man in the city.\f*
\v 7 And the men traveling with him stood speechless, hearing the voice but seeing no one.
\v 8 And Saul got up from the ground; and though his eyes were open he could see nothing, so they led him by the hand and brought him into Damascus \add [i.e. he entered the city he had come to raid, led like a child]\add*.
\v 9 And for three days he did not see, and he did not eat or drink.
\s1 Ananias Is Sent
\p
\v 10 Now there was a certain disciple in Damascus named Ananias, and the Lord said to him in a vision, "Ananias." And he said, "Look — here I am, Lord."\f + \fr 9:10 \ft The answer of everyone in Israel's scriptures who is called: Abraham (Genesis 22:1), Moses (Exodus 3:4), Samuel (1 Samuel 3:4), Isaiah (Isaiah 6:8). And the man giving it is not an apostle or an elder — just "a certain disciple."\f*
\v 11 And the Lord said to him, "Get up and go to the street called Straight\f + \fr 9:11 \ft The main east-west avenue of Damascus, colonnaded and running the width of the city. The instructions are as exact as an address: this street, this house, this man, this town he comes from.\f*, and look in the house of Judas for a man from Tarsus named Saul; for look — he is praying \add [i.e. the man who came breathing threats has spent three days praying]\add*,
\v 12 and he has seen in a vision a man named Ananias coming in and laying his hands on him so that he may see again."\f + \fr 9:12 \ft Two visions, one from each side, arranged to meet. The author uses the same device with Cornelius and Peter in the next chapter but one (10:3-6, 10-16). It rules out coincidence: God has spoken to both parties before either moved.\f*
\v 13 But Ananias answered, "Lord, I have heard from many about this man, how much harm he did to your holy ones\f + \fr 9:13 \ft Greek \tl hagioi\tl*, "holy ones, set-apart ones" — the first time the believers are called this in the book. In Israel's scriptures it is a term for God's consecrated people (Psalm 34:9; Daniel 7:18).\f* in Jerusalem;
\v 14 and here he has authority from the chief priests to bind everyone who calls on your name."
\v 15 But the Lord said to him, "Go, because this man is a chosen vessel to me\f + \fr 9:15 \ft A vessel is a thing used, chosen by whoever intends to use it. "As the clay in the potter's hand, so are you in my hand, house of Israel" (Jeremiah 18:6). The choosing here is not for standing; it is for a job, and the next verse names its cost.\f*, to carry my name before nations and kings and the sons of Israel;
\v 16 for I will show him how much he must suffer for my name."\f + \fr 9:16 \ft Every speech in this book so far has been addressed to "men of Israel." This commission puts the nations at the head of the list, and it puts suffering into the assignment itself rather than treating it as a hazard of the job. The man who made others suffer for the Name will suffer for it.\f*
\v 17 So Ananias went and went into the house, and laying his hands on him he said, "Saul, brother \add [i.e. brother, said to the man who arrived carrying warrants for his arrest]\add*, the Lord has sent me — Jesus, who appeared to you on the road you were coming by — so that you may see again and be filled with the Holy Spirit."
\v 18 And at once something like scales fell from his eyes and he saw again; and he got up and was immersed,
\v 19 and taking food he was strengthened.
\s1 Saul Preaches in Damascus
\p
\v 20 And he was with the disciples in Damascus for some days, and at once he was proclaiming Jesus\f + \fr 9:20 \ft NA28 reads "proclaiming Jesus"; the majority text has "Christ," which is how most older English versions read.\f* in the synagogues — that this one is the Son of God\f + \fr 9:20 \ft The one place in this book where "Son of God" is the content of the preaching. The title comes from Psalm 2:7, spoken over the king of David's line at his crowning, in a psalm where God gives that king the nations for an inheritance. Paul will quote the psalm outright at 13:33.\f*.
\v 21 And all who heard were beside themselves and said, "Is this not the man who in Jerusalem ravaged those who call on this name? And had he not come here for this, to bring them bound to the chief priests?"
\v 22 But Saul grew all the stronger and threw the Jews living in Damascus into confusion, proving that this one is the Christ.
\p
\v 23 And when a good many days had been filled full\f + \fr 9:23 \ft Paul's own account fills in what the phrase covers: he went off into Arabia, came back to Damascus, and only after three years went up to Jerusalem (Galatians 1:17-18).\f*, the Jews took counsel together to do away with him;
\v 24 but their plot became known to Saul. And they were watching the gates day and night as well, to do away with him,
\v 25 but his disciples took him by night and let him down through the wall, lowering him in a basket.\f + \fr 9:25 \ft Paul tells the same story and adds that the governor under King Aretas had the city watched (2 Corinthians 11:32-33). Rahab let the spies down through her window in the wall of Jericho the same way (Joshua 2:15).\f*
\s1 Saul in Jerusalem
\p
\v 26 And when he came to Jerusalem he tried to attach himself to the disciples, and they were all afraid of him, not trusting that he was a disciple \add [i.e. they had watched him drag their people out of their houses]\add*.
\v 27 But Barnabas took him and brought him to the apostles\f + \fr 9:27 \ft The same Barnabas the apostles had nicknamed "son of encouragement" (4:36). He does here what the name says: he takes the man nobody will stand next to and stands next to him.\f*, and told them how he had seen the Lord on the road, and that he had spoken to him, and how in Damascus he had spoken boldly in the name of Jesus.
\v 28 And he was with them, going in and out in Jerusalem, speaking boldly in the name of the Lord,
\v 29 and he was speaking and arguing with the Hellenists; but they set out to do away with him \add [i.e. the same men, the same argument, and the same outcome they had arranged for Stephen]\add*.
\v 30 And when the brothers learned it, they brought him down to Caesarea and sent him off to Tarsus.
\v 31 So the assembly throughout all Judea and Galilee and Samaria had peace and was being built up; and going on in the fear of the Lord and in the encouragement of the Holy Spirit, it was multiplying.\f + \fr 9:31 \ft NA28 has the singular throughout — one assembly, spread over three regions, growing. Most older English versions have the plural, "the churches." This is also the first time Galilee is named in the book.\f*
\s1 Peter at Lydda
\p
\v 32 Now it happened that as Peter went through them all, he came down also to the holy ones living in Lydda\f + \fr 9:32 \ft Lod, in the coastal plain some twenty-five miles northwest of Jerusalem, on the road to the sea. From here on, the author tracks Peter's position westward with care; where he is standing matters to what happens in chapter 10.\f*.
\v 33 And there he found a man named Aeneas, who had been lying on a mat for eight years, for he was paralyzed.
\v 34 And Peter said to him, "Aeneas, Jesus Christ heals you \add [i.e. he does it, not I]\add*. Get up and make your bed." And at once he got up.
\v 35 And all who lived in Lydda and Sharon saw him, and they turned to the Lord.
\s1 Tabitha Raised at Joppa
\p
\v 36 Now in Joppa\f + \fr 9:36 \ft The port from which Jonah sailed when he was running from a commission to a Gentile city (Jonah 1:3). Peter will be summoned from this same town to the house of a Roman officer.\f* there was a certain disciple named Tabitha, which translated is Dorcas\f + \fr 9:36 \ft Both names mean "gazelle," one in Aramaic and one in Greek — she was known by both, in a community that spoke both. The Greek word for "disciple" is feminine here, and this is the only place in the New Testament where that form occurs.\f*. She was full of good works and acts of mercy that she did.
\v 37 And it happened in those days that she fell sick and died; and they washed her and laid her in an upper room \add [i.e. not in a tomb, but in the room where the community met]\add*.
\v 38 And since Lydda was near Joppa, the disciples heard that Peter was there and sent two men to him, urging him, "Do not delay in coming through to us."
\v 39 So Peter got up and went with them. And when he arrived they brought him up to the upper room, and all the widows stood beside him weeping and showing the tunics and clothes that Dorcas made while she was with them \add [i.e. holding up the evidence of what the community had lost]\add*.
\v 40 But Peter put them all outside, and kneeling down he prayed; and turning to the body he said, "Tabitha, get up."\f + \fr 9:40 \ft Elijah and Elisha both shut the door before raising a dead child (1 Kings 17:19-22; 2 Kings 4:33), and Peter does the same. The words are within one consonant of the words Jesus used over Jairus's daughter — \tl talitha koum\tl*, "little girl, get up" (Mark 5:41). But Jesus did not pray first. Peter does.\f* And she opened her eyes, and seeing Peter she sat up.
\v 41 And he gave her his hand and raised her up, and calling the holy ones and the widows he presented her alive \add [i.e. gave her back to the people who depended on her]\add*.
\v 42 And it became known throughout all Joppa, and many trusted on the Lord.
\v 43 And it happened that he stayed many days in Joppa with a certain Simon, a tanner.\f + \fr 9:43 \ft A quiet detail doing a great deal of work. A tanner handles the skins of dead animals all day, which leaves him perpetually unclean and, on account of the smell, living at the edge of town. The leading apostle is lodging in that house when the voice tells him in the next chapter to stop calling things unclean.\f*

\c 10
\s1 Cornelius, a Centurion in Caesarea
\p
\v 1 Now there was a certain man in Caesarea\f + \fr 10:1 \ft Caesarea Maritima, Herod's harbour city and the Roman administrative capital of Judea — a Gentile city on Jewish soil, where the governor lived and the garrison was housed.\f* named Cornelius, a centurion of the cohort called Italian\f + \fr 10:1 \ft A centurion commanded about eighty men. An Italian cohort of Roman citizens is attested in Syria in this period by inscription. He is a professional officer of the occupying army.\f*,
\v 2 devout and fearing God\f + \fr 10:2 \ft "God-fearer" is close to a technical term. It describes a Gentile who attached himself to the synagogue — worshiping Israel's God, keeping the moral commands, giving to the community — but who had not been circumcised and so had not become a proselyte. He stands as near the covenant as a Gentile could stand without crossing into it. The book meets several such men (13:16, 26; 16:14; 17:4, 17; 18:7).\f* with all his household, doing many acts of mercy for the people and begging God always.
\v 3 About the ninth hour of the day\f + \fr 10:3 \ft Three in the afternoon, the hour of the evening offering in the temple and of the prayers that went with it — which is what makes the angel's next words what they are.\f* he saw plainly in a vision an angel of God coming in to him and saying to him, "Cornelius."
\v 4 And staring at him and becoming afraid he said, "What is it, lord?" And he said to him, "Your prayers and your acts of mercy have gone up as a memorial before God.\f + \fr 10:4 \ft Every word here is out of the sacrificial law. The "memorial" is the handful of the grain offering that the priest burned on the altar so that it would "go up" to God (Leviticus 2:2, 9, 16). This man has no temple, no priest, and no covenant standing — and his prayers and his charity have been received on the altar as an offering. "Let my prayer be set before you as incense, the lifting of my hands as the evening offering" (Psalm 141:2).\f*
\v 5 And now send men to Joppa and send for a certain Simon who is surnamed Peter.
\v 6 He is lodging with a certain Simon, a tanner, whose house is by the sea."\f + \fr 10:6 \ft "He will tell you what you must do" is added at the end of this verse in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — Note that the angel does not preach. He gives an address. The message itself has to come through a man.\f*
\v 7 And when the angel who spoke to him went away, he called two of his household slaves and a devout soldier from those who waited on him,
\v 8 and after telling them everything he sent them off to Joppa.
\s1 Peter's Vision on the Roof
\p
\v 9 And on the next day, as they were traveling and drawing near to the city, Peter went up on the housetop to pray, about the sixth hour \add [i.e. at midday, when the meal was being got ready]\add*.
\v 10 And he became hungry and wanted to eat; and while they were getting it ready, a trance fell on him,
\v 11 and he saw the sky opened up and something like a great sheet coming down, being let down by four corners onto the earth,
\v 12 in which were all the four-footed animals and creeping things of the earth and birds of the sky \add [i.e. the clean and the unclean together in one cloth, with nothing sorted]\add*.
\v 13 And a voice came to him: "Get up, Peter, kill and eat."
\v 14 But Peter said, "By no means, Lord, for I have never eaten anything common and unclean."\f + \fr 10:14 \ft Two different words. "Unclean" is what the law itself forbids; "common" is what has been defiled by contact. Peter is claiming a perfect record on both counts. Ezekiel made the same protest when God told him to cook over dung — "I have never defiled myself... no unclean flesh has come into my mouth" (Ezekiel 4:14). God gave way to Ezekiel. He does not give way here.\f*
\v 15 And a voice came to him again, a second time: "What God has made clean, do not you make common."\f + \fr 10:15 \ft The cleansing is spoken of as already done. Peter is not being asked to judge; he is being told that the judgment has been made and that he must stop contradicting it. And this is not a small adjustment: the food laws were the daily rehearsal of Israel's separateness. "I am the LORD your God, who has separated you from the peoples. You shall therefore separate the clean beast from the unclean" (Leviticus 20:24-26). To eat was to declare a boundary. Verse 28 will show what Peter takes the vision to be about.\f*
\v 16 And this happened three times, and at once the object was taken up into the sky \add [i.e. three times, and then taken back where it came from with nothing thrown out]\add*.
\s1 The Spirit Sends Peter with the Messengers
\p
\v 17 Now while Peter was thoroughly at a loss within himself as to what the vision he had seen might mean \add [i.e. he did not know — the meaning was not obvious even to him]\add*, look — the men sent by Cornelius, having asked out the way to Simon's house, stood at the gate,
\v 18 and calling out they asked whether Simon who is surnamed Peter was lodging there.
\v 19 And while Peter was turning the vision over in his mind, the Spirit said to him, "Look — three men are looking for you.
\v 20 So get up and go down and go with them, doubting nothing\f + \fr 10:20 \ft The Greek verb covers a range — to separate, to make a distinction, to judge between, to waver. Here it is in the middle voice and is rendered "doubting nothing"; at 11:12, where Peter retells this and the form is active, it is rendered "making no distinction." It is the same word, and either sense is available in both places. It runs through the whole episode and comes back at 15:9, where God "made no distinction between us and them."\f*, because I have sent them."
\v 21 And Peter went down to the men and said, "Look — I am the one you are looking for. What is the reason you have come?"\f + \fr 10:21 \ft "Who had been sent to him from Cornelius" is added after "the men" in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*
\v 22 And they said, "Cornelius, a centurion, a righteous man who fears God and is well spoken of by the whole nation of the Jews, was told by a holy angel to send for you to come to his house and to hear words from you."
\v 23 So he called them in and gave them lodging \add [i.e. Gentiles under the same roof, eating at the same table — the first line crossed]\add*. And on the next day he got up and went out with them, and some of the brothers from Joppa went with him \add [i.e. six of them, as he later tells it, and every one a witness he will need]\add*.
\s1 Peter Enters a Gentile House
\p
\v 24 And on the following day he came into Caesarea. And Cornelius was waiting for them, having called together his relatives and close friends \add [i.e. he had assembled a household before he knew what he would hear]\add*.
\v 25 And when Peter came in, Cornelius met him and fell at his feet and bowed down.
\v 26 But Peter raised him up, saying, "Get up — I myself am also a man."
\v 27 And talking with him he went in and found many gathered together,
\v 28 and he said to them, "You yourselves know that it is out of bounds for a Jewish man to join himself to a foreigner or come near him; and to me God has shown not to call any man common or unclean.\f + \fr 10:28 \ft Here is what Peter took the vision to mean, and it is not about menus. The two words he used of food in verse 14 — common, unclean — he now uses of people. The rule he cites is not written in the law of Moses; nowhere does the Torah forbid entering a Gentile house. It is what grew up around the food laws: if you cannot eat with a man you cannot live with him, and a wall made of diet became a wall made of custom, and the custom came to be treated as the command.\f*
\v 29 So when I was sent for I came without objection. So I ask, for what reason did you send for me?"
\v 30 And Cornelius said, "Four days ago, at this hour, I was praying the ninth-hour prayer in my house\f + \fr 10:30 \ft "Fasting" is added here in the majority text and in most older English versions; NA28 has only praying.\f*, and look — a man in shining clothing stood before me
\v 31 and said, 'Cornelius, your prayer has been heard and your acts of mercy have been remembered before God.
\v 32 So send to Joppa and call for Simon who is surnamed Peter; he is lodging in the house of Simon a tanner by the sea.'\f + \fr 10:32 \ft "Who when he comes will speak to you" is added at the end of the verse in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*
\v 33 So I sent to you at once, and you did well to come. So now we are all here before God, to hear everything that has been ordered you by the Lord."\f + \fr 10:33 \ft "Before God" is the language of Israel standing in the assembly. A Gentile officer says it of his own front room, and he says it without being taught to.\f*
\s1 God Does Not Receive the Face
\p
\v 34 And Peter opened his mouth and said, "In truth I grasp that God does not receive the face\f + \fr 10:34 \ft Greek \tl prosōpolēmptēs\tl*, literally "a face-receiver" — the Hebrew idiom for a judge who decides by who is standing in front of him. It is kept literal here. And it is not a new idea: "the LORD your God is God of gods and Lord of lords, who is not partial and takes no bribe" (Deuteronomy 10:17); "there is no injustice with the LORD our God, nor partiality" (2 Chronicles 19:7). Peter is not discovering a doctrine; he is discovering that he had stopped believing one.\f*,
\v 35 but in every nation the one who fears him and works righteousness is welcome to him.
\v 36 The word he sent to the sons of Israel, announcing the good news of peace through Jesus Christ — this one is Lord of all —\f + \fr 10:36 \ft The parenthesis breaks the sentence open. The message went to Israel; the one it is about is Lord of everybody. If that is true, the message cannot be kept where it started.\f*
\v 37 you yourselves know what happened throughout all Judea, beginning from Galilee after the immersion that John proclaimed:
\v 38 Jesus of Nazareth, how God anointed him with the Holy Spirit and with power; who went through the land doing good and healing all who were being crushed by the devil, because God was with him \add [i.e. the same thing said of Joseph in Egypt, and for the same reason]\add*.
\v 39 And we are witnesses of everything he did both in the country of the Jews and in Jerusalem — whom they also did away with by hanging him on a tree\f + \fr 10:39 \ft Again the Torah's word for a cursed death (Deuteronomy 21:22-23); see the note at 5:30. Peter does not soften it in a Gentile house any more than he softened it before the council.\f*.
\v 40 This one God raised on the third day and gave him to become plainly seen,
\v 41 not to all the people but to witnesses chosen beforehand by God, to us who ate and drank with him after he rose out of the dead \add [i.e. we proved he was no ghost by sharing his table]\add*.
\v 42 And he charged us to proclaim to the people and to bear full witness that this is the one marked out by God as judge of living and dead.
\v 43 To him all the prophets bear witness, that everyone who trusts in him receives forgiveness of sins through his name."\f + \fr 10:43 \ft "Everyone." Not everyone in Israel, not everyone circumcised — everyone. It is the last word of the sermon, and the Spirit does not wait for the next one.\f*
\s1 The Spirit Falls on the Nations
\p
\v 44 While Peter was still speaking these words, the Holy Spirit fell on all who were hearing the word \add [i.e. before the sermon was finished, before any call to turn, before any water]\add*.
\v 45 And the trusting ones from the circumcision, as many as had come with Peter, were amazed, because the gift of the Holy Spirit had been poured out on the nations too;
\v 46 for they heard them speaking in tongues and making God great.\f + \fr 10:46 \ft The same two things that happened in the upper room at Pentecost, described in the same words (2:4, 11). Peter says as much when he reports it: "the Holy Spirit fell on them just as on us at the beginning" (11:15). Everywhere else in this book the water comes first and the Spirit follows (2:38; 8:14-17). Here the order is reversed, and the reversal is the argument: no apostle laid a hand on them, no one prayed for it, nothing human stood between God and these people.\f* Then Peter answered,
\v 47 "Can anyone withhold the water, so that these should not be immersed, who have received the Holy Spirit just as we did?"\f + \fr 10:47 \ft He does not ask whether they may be baptized. He asks who could possibly stop it. God has already settled the matter; the only question left is whether a man will put up a barrier where God has taken one down.\f*
\v 48 And he ordered them to be immersed in the name of Jesus Christ. Then they asked him to stay on for some days \add [i.e. and he stayed, and ate at their table, which is what he will have to answer for in Jerusalem]\add*.

\c 11
\s1 Peter Answers for Eating with Gentiles
\p
\v 1 Now the apostles and the brothers who were throughout Judea heard that the nations too had welcomed the word of God.
\v 2 And when Peter went up to Jerusalem, those of the circumcision took issue with him,
\v 3 saying, "You went in to men who are uncircumcised and ate with them."\f + \fr 11:3 \ft Note the charge. Not "you preached to Gentiles" and not "you baptized the uncircumcised" — you ate with them. A shared table was the most complete form of belonging there was, and the food laws had made it impossible across this line. The objection goes straight to the thing that had held the wall up.\f*
\v 4 But Peter began and set it out to them in order, saying,\f + \fr 11:4 \ft He does not argue. He tells them what happened, in sequence. The author thinks the account important enough to write out twice, and it will be summarized a third time at the council in chapter 15.\f*
\v 5 "I was in the city of Joppa praying, and in a trance I saw a vision: something like a great sheet coming down, let down out of the sky by four corners, and it came all the way to me.
\v 6 Staring into it I looked closely and saw the four-footed animals of the earth and the wild beasts and the creeping things and the birds of the sky.
\v 7 And I also heard a voice saying to me, 'Get up, Peter, kill and eat.'
\v 8 But I said, 'By no means, Lord, for nothing common or unclean has ever gone into my mouth.'
\v 9 But a voice answered a second time out of the sky, 'What God has made clean, do not you make common.'
\v 10 And this happened three times, and it was all drawn back up into the sky.
\v 11 And look — at once three men stood at the house where we were, sent to me from Caesarea \add [i.e. arriving at the very moment the vision ended]\add*.
\v 12 And the Spirit told me to go with them, making no distinction\f + \fr 11:12 \ft The same Greek word rendered "doubting nothing" at 10:20, here in the active voice and rendered by its other sense. Either reading is available in both places — and it is the very thing his accusers in verse 2 are doing to him.\f*. And these six brothers went with me too \add [i.e. six of them, standing here, who saw all of it]\add*, and we went into the man's house.
\v 13 And he reported to us how he had seen the angel standing in his house and saying, 'Send to Joppa and send for Simon who is surnamed Peter,
\v 14 who will speak words to you by which you will be rescued, you and your whole household.'\f + \fr 11:14 \ft This clause is not in the angel's speech as chapter 10 reported it. Peter's retelling names the whole event for what it was: a rescue, and one that came through words a man had to travel and speak.\f*
\v 15 And as I began to speak, the Holy Spirit fell on them just as on us at the beginning \add [i.e. at Pentecost — the same gift, in the same way]\add*.
\v 16 And I remembered the word of the Lord, how he said, 'John immersed with water, but you will be immersed in the Holy Spirit.'\f + \fr 11:16 \ft Jesus's words at 1:5. Peter had heard them, and had watched them come true on himself, without ever grasping how far the "you" reached. It takes seeing it happen to Gentiles to make him remember what he was told.\f*
\v 17 So if God gave them the same gift he gave us when we trusted on the Lord Jesus Christ, who was I, that I could stand in God's way?"
\v 18 And when they heard this they were quiet, and they glorified God, saying, "So then God has given to the nations too the turning back that leads to life."\f + \fr 11:18 \ft At 5:31 Peter had said the exalted Jesus was giving "turning back to Israel." The sentence here is the same one with a word added: to the nations too. And the phrase "turning back that leads to life" is Ezekiel's — "turn, and live" (Ezekiel 18:32) — the covenant choice Moses set before Israel (Deuteronomy 30:15-20) now set before everyone.\f*
\s1 The Church at Antioch
\p
\v 19 Now those who had been scattered by the trouble that came about over Stephen went as far as Phoenicia and Cyprus and Antioch\f + \fr 11:19 \ft Antioch on the Orontes, capital of the province of Syria and the third city of the empire after Rome and Alexandria. From here on it is the base from which the mission to the nations runs.\f*, speaking the word to no one except Jews only \add [i.e. the old boundary still held, even among the scattered]\add*.
\v 20 But there were some of them, men of Cyprus and Cyrene, who came to Antioch and spoke also to the Hellenists\f + \fr 11:20 \ft NA28 reads "Hellenists"; many English versions read "Greeks." The contrast with the previous verse — "to no one except Jews only, but also to these" — requires that the people in view here are not Jews, whatever the exact word. — And note who does it: unnamed men, holding no office, sent by nobody. Peter's crossing came by angel, vision, and direct command; this one came by ordinary men deciding to speak.\f*, announcing the good news of the Lord Jesus.
\v 21 And the hand of the Lord was with them\f + \fr 11:21 \ft "The hand of the LORD" is the standing phrase for God's power at work in an undertaking — on Elijah (1 Kings 18:46), on Ezra (Ezra 7:6, 9). What the unauthorized men were doing, God was doing.\f*, and a great number who trusted turned to the Lord.
\v 22 And the word about them was heard in the ears of the assembly in Jerusalem, and they sent out Barnabas as far as Antioch \add [i.e. as they had sent Peter and John when Samaria believed]\add*.
\v 23 And when he came and saw the favor of God he rejoiced, and he urged them all to hold to the Lord with the purpose of their heart\f + \fr 11:23 \ft The verb means to stay with, to remain beside. It is what the law asks of Israel — "hold fast to him" (Deuteronomy 10:20; 11:22; Joshua 23:8) — and one does not urge a man to keep doing what is happening to him automatically. He has just called what he sees the favor of God, and in the same breath he tells them to hold on to it.\f*,
\v 24 because he was a good man and full of the Holy Spirit and of trust. And a large crowd was added to the Lord.
\v 25 And he went off to Tarsus to hunt for Saul\f + \fr 11:25 \ft The verb means to search out, and it implies effort. Saul had been packed off to Tarsus years before (9:30) and nothing has been heard of him since. Barnabas leaves a thriving community to go find one man — the man he had already vouched for once (9:27), and the man the Lord had named as the one who would carry the name to the nations (9:15).\f*,
\v 26 and when he found him he brought him to Antioch. And it happened that for a whole year they were gathered together in the assembly and taught a large crowd; and it was in Antioch that the disciples were first called Christians\f + \fr 11:26 \ft The Greek verb means "were styled, came to be called," and it does not say by whom. The ending is the ordinary Greek and Latin way of naming a man's partisans — \tl Hērōdianoi\tl*, Herod's people; \tl Christianoi\tl*, Christ's people. In Jerusalem the movement could still pass for a party within Judaism. In a city where uncircumcised Gentiles sat at the same table, it could not, and the outside world had to find a word for it.\f*.
\s1 Famine Relief
\p
\v 27 Now in those days prophets came down from Jerusalem to Antioch.
\v 28 And one of them named Agabus stood up and signaled through the Spirit that a great famine was about to come over the whole inhabited world — which happened under Claudius.\f + \fr 11:28 \ft Claudius reigned from 41 to 54, and grain shortages under him are recorded by several Roman writers; Josephus describes a severe famine in Judea about 46-47. Agabus appears again at 21:10-11.\f*
\v 29 And the disciples determined, each of them as he had the means, to send something for service to the brothers living in Judea \add [i.e. each man judging his own share, and no one setting a figure]\add*;
\v 30 which they also did, sending it to the elders by the hand of Barnabas and Saul.\f + \fr 11:30 \ft The direction of the money is worth noticing. The prophets had always pictured the nations bringing their wealth up to Zion. Here a mixed Gentile congregation sends relief down to Judea — not as tribute, and not upward to a superior, but sideways, to family that is hungry. This is also the first mention of "elders" at Jerusalem.\f*

\c 12
\s1 Herod Kills James and Arrests Peter
\p
\v 1 Now about that time Herod the king\f + \fr 12:1 \ft Herod Agrippa I, grandson of Herod the Great, whom Claudius had made king over almost the whole of his grandfather's territory. The family runs through this story: Herod the Great killed the children of Bethlehem, Herod Antipas beheaded John and sat in on Jesus's trial, and now a third Herod takes hold of the assembly. The community's own prayer had already read them into Psalm 2 — "the kings of the earth took their stand... against the Lord and against his Anointed" (4:26-27).\f* laid hands on some from the assembly to mistreat them.
\v 2 And he did away with James the brother of John with the sword.\f + \fr 12:2 \ft Nine words in the Greek, and no more. One of the three who were closest to Jesus is executed, and the book gives him no speech, no farewell, and no explanation of why he was not delivered as Peter is about to be. The silence is not an oversight. Jesus had said plainly, "they will put some of you to death" (Luke 21:16), and the text lets the difference between the two men stand without accounting for it.\f*
\v 3 And seeing that it pleased the Jews, he went on to arrest Peter as well — and those were the days of unleavened bread\f + \fr 12:3 \ft The week that begins with Passover (Exodus 12:15-20; Leviticus 23:5-6). The author stops the sentence to tell us the season, and everything that follows is shaped by it: a king who oppresses God's people, an angel who comes in the night, a people led out, and the king struck down.\f*.
\v 4 And having seized him he put him in prison, handing him over to four squads of four soldiers to guard him\f + \fr 12:4 \ft Sixteen men for one prisoner, four on duty at each watch of the night. The author heaps up the precautions — the squads, the two chains, the men sleeping either side of him, the guards on the door — so that when they all come to nothing there will be nothing left to credit but God.\f*, meaning to bring him up to the people after the Passover.
\v 5 So Peter was being kept in the prison; but prayer was being made intently to God for him by the assembly \add [i.e. the chains on one side of the "but," and the praying on the other]\add*.
\s1 Led Out in the Night
\p
\v 6 And when Herod was about to bring him out, that night Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains, and guards in front of the door were keeping the prison \add [i.e. the last night he had, and he slept through most of it]\add*.
\v 7 And look — an angel of the Lord stood there, and a light shone in the cell. And striking Peter's side\f + \fr 12:7 \ft The verb is the one the Greek Old Testament uses of God's blows — "I will strike every firstborn in the land of Egypt" (Exodus 12:12); "the angel of the LORD went out and struck the camp of the Assyrians" (2 Kings 19:35). Here it is a tap to wake a sleeping man. The same word comes back in verse 23 and kills a king.\f* he woke him, saying, "Get up quickly." And the chains fell off his hands.
\v 8 And the angel said to him, "Belt yourself and tie on your sandals." And he did so. And he said to him, "Throw your cloak around you and follow me."\f + \fr 12:8 \ft These are the Passover instructions, almost word for word: "you shall eat it with your belt fastened, your sandals on your feet... and you shall eat it in haste; it is the LORD's Passover" (Exodus 12:11). Peter is being dressed for the exodus.\f*
\v 9 And he went out and followed, and he did not know that what was happening through the angel was real, but he thought he was seeing a vision.
\v 10 And passing the first guard and the second they came to the iron gate that leads into the city, which opened for them of its own accord; and they went out and went on one street, and at once the angel left him \add [i.e. exactly as far as was needed, and not one step further]\add*.
\v 11 And Peter, coming to himself, said, "Now I truly know that the Lord sent out his angel and took me out of Herod's hand and from everything the people of the Jews were expecting."\f + \fr 12:11 \ft "Sent his angel" is Daniel's line from the lions' den (Daniel 6:22), and "out of the hand of" is the standing phrase for God's rescues — out of the hand of the Egyptians, of Pharaoh, of the lion and the bear (Exodus 3:8; 18:10; 1 Samuel 17:37). Peter reaches for the old words without thinking.\f*
\s1 At the House of Mary
\p
\v 12 And seeing where he was, he came to the house of Mary the mother of John who was surnamed Mark\f + \fr 12:12 \ft John Mark will go with Barnabas and Saul back to Antioch (12:25), set out with them on the first journey and quit at Perga (13:5, 13), and become the reason those two men part company (15:37-39). His mother's house is a meeting place of the Jerusalem community.\f*, where a good many were gathered and praying \add [i.e. still praying, in the middle of the night]\add*.
\v 13 And when he knocked at the door of the gateway, a servant girl named Rhoda came to answer,
\v 14 and recognizing Peter's voice, for joy she did not open the gate but ran in and reported that Peter was standing at the gateway \add [i.e. an iron gate had opened by itself, and this one stayed shut]\add*.
\v 15 But they said to her, "You are out of your mind." But she kept insisting it was so. And they said, "It is his angel."\f + \fr 12:15 \ft A belief attested in the period: that a person has a heavenly counterpart who may appear in his likeness (compare Matthew 18:10). They find it easier to believe Peter is dead and his angel has come than that the thing they have been praying for all night has happened.\f*
\v 16 But Peter kept on knocking, and when they opened they saw him and were beside themselves.
\v 17 But he motioned to them with his hand to be quiet, and told them how the Lord had brought him out of the prison, and he said, "Report these things to James\f + \fr 12:17 \ft Not the James of verse 2, who is dead, but the brother of Jesus, named here for the first time and named without introduction, as though the reader already knows him. He will preside at the council in chapter 15, and Paul calls him "the Lord's brother" (Galatians 1:19). Peter is handing Jerusalem over.\f* and the brothers." And he went out and went to another place \add [i.e. and the book does not say where, because his part in it is nearly done]\add*.
\p
\v 18 And when day came there was no small stir among the soldiers over what had become of Peter.
\v 19 And when Herod searched for him and did not find him, he examined the guards and ordered them led away\f + \fr 12:19 \ft Roman military practice held a guard answerable with his own life for a prisoner who escaped — which is why the jailer at Philippi reaches for his sword (16:27). The men die for a failure that was not theirs.\f*; and he went down from Judea to Caesarea and stayed there.
\s1 The Death of Herod
\p
\v 20 Now he was in a fighting mood with the people of Tyre and Sidon; but with one accord they came to him, and after winning over Blastus, the king's chamberlain, they asked for peace, because their country was fed from the king's \add [i.e. he controlled their grain, and so they had to come to him]\add*.
\v 21 And on a set day Herod put on royal clothing and sat on the platform and made a public speech to them.\f + \fr 12:21 \ft Josephus tells the same story: a festival at Caesarea, a robe woven of silver that blazed when the early sun struck it, flatterers crying out that he was more than mortal, and Agrippa dying of abdominal pain five days later (Antiquities 19.343-350).\f*
\v 22 And the people shouted out, "The voice of a god, and not of a man!"
\v 23 And at once an angel of the Lord struck him, because he did not give the glory to God; and he was eaten by worms and breathed his last.\f + \fr 12:23 \ft The author names the charge exactly, and it is not the murder of James or the jailing of Peter: he took what belongs to God. Compare Peter two chapters earlier, pulling Cornelius up off the floor — "Get up; I myself am also a man" (10:26). One man refused the honor and was used; the other took it and was struck. And the fate is the one Isaiah assigned to the king of Babylon who said "I will make myself like the Most High": worms for a covering (Isaiah 14:11-14; compare Ezekiel 28:2 of the king of Tyre).\f*
\v 24 But the word of God kept growing and multiplying \add [i.e. the king is dead, and the word is not]\add*.
\v 25 And Barnabas and Saul went back, having filled full their service to Jerusalem, taking along John who was surnamed Mark.\f + \fr 12:25 \ft NA28 reads "returned to Jerusalem," which the story cannot carry — they had gone to Jerusalem and are now going home. The phrase is taken here with what precedes it: their service was to Jerusalem, and having completed it they went back. Most English versions read "returned from Jerusalem."\f*

\c 13
\s1 The Spirit Sets Barnabas and Saul Apart
\p
\v 1 Now there were in Antioch, in the assembly there, prophets and teachers: Barnabas and Simeon who was called Niger, and Lucius the Cyrenian, and Manaen, who had been brought up with Herod the tetrarch, and Saul.\f + \fr 13:1 \ft Look at the list before reading on. A Levite from Cyprus. A man whose Latin nickname means "black." A North African from Cyrene, where the men who first preached to Gentiles in this city came from (11:20). A man raised in the household of the Herod who beheaded John and mocked Jesus at his trial. And a Pharisee from Cilicia who used to hunt down believers. The mission to the nations is not launched by a uniform community deciding to reach the unlike; it comes out of a leadership that has already crossed every line it will be asked to cross.\f*
\v 2 And while they were serving the Lord and fasting, the Holy Spirit said, "Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them."\f + \fr 13:2 \ft Nobody volunteers and nobody proposes a plan. And the verb "set apart" is the one used in the law of Israel's own separation from the nations — "I have separated you from the peoples" (Leviticus 20:26). The word that fenced Israel off is now the word that sends two men out.\f*
\v 3 Then, after fasting and praying and laying their hands on them, they let them go \add [i.e. the call was already God's; the assembly only released them for it]\add*.
\s1 Cyprus, and the Magician at Paphos
\p
\v 4 So these, sent out by the Holy Spirit, went down to Seleucia\f + \fr 13:4 \ft The port of Antioch, sixteen miles down the Orontes. Cyprus, the first stop, is Barnabas's own island (4:36).\f*, and from there they sailed off to Cyprus.
\v 5 And when they came to Salamis they announced the word of God in the synagogues of the Jews \add [i.e. the synagogue first, as it will be in every city from here to Rome]\add*; and they had John as their assistant as well.
\v 6 And going through the whole island as far as Paphos, they found a certain man, a magician, a false prophet, a Jew whose name was Bar-Jesus\f + \fr 13:6 \ft The name means "son of Joshua" or "son of Jesus." He is a Jew who is everything the law of Israel forbids: a sorcerer (Deuteronomy 18:10-12) claiming a prophet's authority (Deuteronomy 13:1-5).\f*,
\v 7 who was with the proconsul Sergius Paulus, a man of understanding. He called for Barnabas and Saul and looked to hear the word of God \add [i.e. the Roman governor is the one seeking, and the Jewish prophet is the one obstructing]\add*.
\v 8 But Elymas the magician — for that is how his name is translated — stood against them, seeking to turn the proconsul away from the trust.
\v 9 But Saul, who is also Paul\f + \fr 13:9 \ft The author has called him Saul for four chapters and will call him Paul for the rest of the book. It is not a conversion name; a Jew of the dispersion commonly carried a Hebrew name and a Roman one, and Paulus was probably his from birth. What changes here is the story: the mission has turned toward the Roman world, and the Roman name is the one that goes with it. Watch the word order too — up to now it has been "Barnabas and Saul"; from verse 13 it is "Paul and Barnabas."\f*, filled with the Holy Spirit, looked straight at him
\v 10 and said, "You who are full of every deceit and every fraud, son of the devil \add [i.e. son of the devil, whatever your name says]\add*, enemy of all righteousness — will you not stop twisting the straight ways of the Lord?\f + \fr 13:10 \ft "Make straight the paths of the Lord" is Isaiah 40:3, the verse that defined John the Baptist's work (Luke 3:4). John straightened the road; this man bends it.\f*
\v 11 And now, look — the hand of the Lord is on you, and you will be blind, not seeing the sun for a time." And at once mist and darkness fell on him, and he went around looking for someone to lead him by the hand.\f + \fr 13:11 \ft Paul knows what he is pronouncing. He was struck blind on the Damascus road and had to be led by the hand into the city (9:8) — the same words. And it is "for a time," which is to say the door is left open, as it was left open for him.\f*
\v 12 Then the proconsul, seeing what had happened, trusted, astounded at the teaching of the Lord.
\s1 Into the Synagogue at Pisidian Antioch
\p
\v 13 And putting out from Paphos, Paul and those with him came to Perga in Pamphylia; and John left them and went back to Jerusalem.\f + \fr 13:13 \ft The author records it and gives no reason. Paul will call it desertion two chapters later, and it will split him from Barnabas (15:37-39). The road ahead of them ran up over the Taurus mountains through country notorious for bandits.\f*
\v 14 But they went on from Perga and came to Antioch in Pisidia\f + \fr 13:14 \ft A different Antioch from the one they were sent from — a Roman colony in the highlands of central Asia Minor, a hundred miles inland and three and a half thousand feet up.\f*, and going into the synagogue on the day of the Sabbath they sat down.
\v 15 And after the reading of the law and the prophets, the synagogue rulers sent to them, saying, "Men, brothers, if there is any word of encouragement for the people among you, speak."\f + \fr 13:15 \ft The order of the service: a reading from the law, a reading from the prophets, then an address, for which a visiting teacher might be invited. Jesus was given the same opening at Nazareth (Luke 4:16-21).\f*
\s1 Paul: From Egypt to David
\p
\v 16 So Paul stood up, and motioning with his hand he said: "Men of Israel, and you who fear God, listen \add [i.e. two audiences in one room, and he is addressing both]\add*.
\v 17 The God of this people Israel chose our fathers and lifted the people high while they were sojourners in the land of Egypt, and with a raised arm he led them out of it\f + \fr 13:17 \ft "With a mighty hand and an outstretched arm" is the fixed liturgical phrase for the exodus (Deuteronomy 5:15; Psalm 136:12). Note that from here to verse 23 God is the subject of nearly every verb: he chose, he raised up, he led out, he put up with, he gave, he removed, he brought.\f*;
\v 18 and for about forty years he put up with them in the wilderness\f + \fr 13:18 \ft One letter's difference in the Greek gives a very different picture. NA28 reads \tl etropophorēsen\tl*, "he put up with their ways"; the variant \tl etrophophorēsen\tl* means "he carried them as a nurse carries a child," which some versions follow.\f*;
\v 19 and after tearing down seven nations in the land of Canaan, he gave them their land as an inheritance — about four hundred and fifty years.\f + \fr 13:19 \ft The seven nations are the standard list of Deuteronomy 7:1. NA28 attaches the four hundred and fifty years to what precedes — the period from the patriarchs to the giving of the land. The majority text attaches it to what follows, the era of the judges, which is how most older English versions read.\f*
\v 20 And after this he gave them judges, until Samuel the prophet.
\v 21 And from then on they asked for a king\f + \fr 13:21 \ft "They asked" — the initiative here is Israel's, not God's, and the request displeased Samuel (1 Samuel 8:5-6). God gave them what they asked for anyway. The man speaking bears that king's name and comes from that king's tribe (Philippians 3:5).\f*, and God gave them Saul son of Kish, a man of the tribe of Benjamin, for forty years.
\v 22 And after removing him he raised up David for them as king, of whom he also said, bearing witness, 'I have found David the son of Jesse, a man after my own heart, who will do all my wishes.'\f + \fr 13:22 \ft Two texts woven together: "a man after his own heart" from 1 Samuel 13:14, and "I have found David my servant" from Psalm 89:20.\f*
\v 23 From this man's seed, according to promise, God brought to Israel a rescuer, Jesus\f + \fr 13:23 \ft NA28 reads "a rescuer, Jesus"; the majority text has "a Savior, Jesus" in the form of an act — salvation. The word itself is the one used in Israel's scriptures for the deliverers God raised up in the time of the judges (Judges 3:9, 15).\f*,
\v 24 after John had proclaimed beforehand, in front of the face of his coming, an immersion of turning back to all the people of Israel.
\v 25 And as John was filling full his course, he said, 'What do you suppose me to be? I am not he. But look — one is coming after me, the sandal of whose feet I am not worthy to untie.' \add [i.e. not fit for the one job in the house too low for anyone but the meanest slave]\add*
\p
\v 26 Men, brothers, sons of Abraham's family, and those among you who fear God — to us the word of this rescue has been sent out.
\v 27 For those living in Jerusalem and their rulers, not recognizing this one, and not recognizing the voices of the prophets that are read every Sabbath, filled them full by judging him\f + \fr 13:27 \ft The sting is in "read every Sabbath" — the very practice this congregation has just performed. The words were in their ears week after week, and in condemning him they carried out what the words said. They accomplished the prophecy by refusing it.\f*;
\v 28 and finding no cause for death, they asked Pilate to have him done away with.
\v 29 And when they had finished everything written about him, they took him down from the tree and laid him in a tomb \add [i.e. and there the story of the promised son of David ends, in a borrowed grave]\add*.
\v 30 But God raised him out of the dead;
\v 31 and for many days he appeared to those who had come up with him from Galilee to Jerusalem, who are now his witnesses to the people.
\s1 What the Psalms Said Would Happen
\p
\v 32 And we announce good news to you: the promise made to the fathers —
\v 33 that God has filled this full for us their children by raising Jesus, as it is also written in the second psalm\f + \fr 13:33 \ft A few witnesses read "the first psalm," which reflects an old way of counting in which what we call Psalms 1 and 2 stood as one. — Psalm 2:7 is a coronation text. "Today I have begotten you" was said over a king of David's line on the day he was installed, not the day he was born. Paul applies it neither to Jesus's birth nor to his baptism but to the resurrection: that is the day he was enthroned.\f*: 'You are my son; today I have fathered you.'
\v 34 And that he raised him out of the dead, no longer to go back to decay, he has said in this way: 'I will give you the holy and sure things of David.'\f + \fr 13:34 \ft Isaiah 55:3 in Greek. The argument turns on a shared word: the "holy things" pledged to David here, and "your holy one" in the psalm quoted next. Blessings sworn to endure need a living heir; a descendant in the grave cannot hold them.\f*
\v 35 So he also says in another place: 'You will not give your holy one to see decay.'\f + \fr 13:35 \ft Psalm 16:10 — the same psalm, and the same argument from it, that Peter used at Pentecost (2:25-31).\f*
\v 36 For David, after serving his own generation by the purpose of God, fell asleep and was added to his fathers and saw decay \add [i.e. his service was real, and it was for his own lifetime, and his body rotted]\add*;
\v 37 but the one God raised did not see decay.
\v 38 So let it be known to you, men, brothers, that through this one forgiveness of sins is announced to you; and from all the things you could not be set right from by the law of Moses,
\v 39 in this one everyone who trusts is set right.\f + \fr 13:38-39 \ft The law provided offerings for sin committed unwittingly (Leviticus 4-5) and a yearly cleansing for the nation (Leviticus 16). What it provided no remedy for was the sin done "with a high hand," deliberately: that man "shall be cut off from among his people" (Numbers 15:30-31). Paul is not saying the law failed at its work. He is saying there was work it never undertook, and that this is where it is now being done.\f*
\v 40 So watch out that what is spoken in the prophets does not come upon you:
\v 41 'Look, you scoffers, and marvel and vanish, because I am doing a work in your days, a work you would never trust even if someone told you the whole of it.'"\f + \fr 13:41 \ft Habakkuk 1:5, quoted from the Greek. Know what the unbelievable work was in Habakkuk's day: God announced that he was raising up the Babylonians — Gentiles — as his instrument toward Israel, and the prophet could hardly credit it (Habakkuk 1:6). Paul chooses the one text in the prophets where the scandal is that God is working through the nations. He is about to do it again in this room.\f*
\p
\v 42 And as they were going out, the people urged them to speak about these things on the next Sabbath.\f + \fr 13:42 \ft NA28 reads as here. The majority text, followed by most older English versions, has "when the Jews had gone out of the synagogue, the Gentiles begged that these words be preached to them."\f*
\v 43 And when the synagogue had broken up, many of the Jews and of the devout proselytes followed Paul and Barnabas, who talked with them and urged them to hold on to the favor of God \add [i.e. to stay in it, having entered it]\add*.
\s1 Almost the Whole City
\p
\v 44 And on the coming Sabbath nearly the whole city was gathered to hear the word of the Lord \add [i.e. no longer a synagogue congregation but a Roman colony, most of it Gentile]\add*.
\v 45 But when the Jews saw the crowds they were filled with jealousy, and they contradicted what Paul said and blasphemed \add [i.e. the same words they had asked to hear again a week before]\add*.
\v 46 And speaking boldly, Paul and Barnabas said, "It was necessary that the word of God be spoken to you first. Since you push it away from you and judge yourselves not worthy of eternal life — look, we are turning to the nations.\f + \fr 13:46 \ft Two things to notice. He says nothing about God rejecting them; the verbs are theirs — you push it away, you judge yourselves. And "we are turning" is a present tense about this city on this day, not a decree. The same thing happens again at Corinth (18:6) and again at Rome (28:28), and in the next city he goes back into the synagogue first (14:1).\f*
\v 47 For this is what the Lord has ordered us: 'I have set you as a light of nations, for you to be for rescue to the end of the earth.'"\f + \fr 13:47 \ft Isaiah 49:6, spoken to the servant of the LORD: raising up the tribes of Jacob is "too small a thing" — the servant is also to be light for the nations. It is the same verse standing behind "to the end of the earth" in 1:8. The turn to the Gentiles is not improvised out of frustration; the command was on the books before the rejection.\f*
\v 48 And when the nations heard this they were glad and glorified the word of the Lord, and as many as were appointed to eternal life trusted.\f + \fr 13:48 \ft Greek \tl tetagmenoi\tl*, a perfect participle from a verb used of assigning a soldier his post or enrolling a man on a register. The form can be read as passive, "as many as had been appointed," or as middle, "as many as had set themselves." English cannot hold both, and the passive is taken here. Set the verse beside verse 46: there the verbs are active and reflexive — they push away, they judge themselves; here the verb is not.\f*
\v 49 And the word of the Lord was carried through the whole region.
\v 50 But the Jews stirred up the devout women of high standing and the leading men of the city, and they raised a persecution against Paul and Barnabas and threw them out of their district \add [i.e. worked through the city's own authorities rather than a mob]\add*.
\v 51 But they shook the dust off their feet against them\f + \fr 13:51 \ft What Jesus told them to do where they were not received (Luke 9:5; 10:11). The gesture says the message was delivered and the refusal is not on the messenger.\f* and came to Iconium.
\v 52 And the disciples were being filled with joy and with the Holy Spirit \add [i.e. the ones left behind, after the men who taught them had been driven out]\add*.

\c 14
\s1 Iconium
\p
\v 1 Now it happened in Iconium that they went together into the synagogue of the Jews and spoke in such a way that a great crowd of both Jews and Greeks trusted \add [i.e. the synagogue first again, in the next city as in the last]\add*.
\v 2 But the Jews who would not obey stirred up the souls of the nations and made them bitter against the brothers.
\v 3 So they spent a long time speaking boldly on the Lord\f + \fr 14:3 \ft "So" — because of the opposition, not in spite of it. The threat lengthens the stay.\f*, who bore witness to the word of his favor, granting signs and wonders to happen through their hands \add [i.e. God himself testifying to what they were saying]\add*.
\v 4 But the mass of the city was split, and some were with the Jews and some with the apostles.\f + \fr 14:4 \ft Up to this point the author has kept the word "apostles" for the twelve. Here, and again at verse 14, he uses it of Barnabas and Paul — without argument, in the passage where the two are doing what apostles do: sent by the Spirit, preaching, working signs, planting communities.\f*
\v 5 And when a rush was made by both the nations and the Jews, with their rulers, to mistreat them and stone them\f + \fr 14:5 \ft Stoning is not a Roman penalty. It is the punishment the law of Israel prescribes for blasphemy and for leading the people after other gods (Leviticus 24:14-16; Deuteronomy 13:6-10), which tells you how the charge was being framed.\f*,
\v 6 they learned of it and fled to the cities of Lycaonia, Lystra and Derbe and the surrounding country\f + \fr 14:6 \ft Lycaonia is a different district with a different language from Iconium, which reckoned itself Phrygian. The distinction matters in a few verses, when the crowd shouts in a tongue the visitors do not follow.\f*,
\v 7 and there they were announcing the good news.
\s1 Lystra: Taken for Zeus and Hermes
\p
\v 8 And in Lystra a certain man sat who was powerless in his feet, lame from his mother's womb, who had never walked \add [i.e. said three ways, so that no one can call it a partial cure]\add*.
\v 9 This man heard Paul speaking; and Paul looked straight at him and saw that he had trust to be rescued\f + \fr 14:9 \ft The Greek word covers both being healed and being saved, and the author does not separate them. Compare the healing at the temple gate in chapter 3, which this scene mirrors almost line for line: the same congenital lameness, the same fixed gaze, the same leaping up. What God did through Peter in Jerusalem he does through Paul in a Lycaonian town.\f*,
\v 10 and said with a loud voice, "Stand up straight on your feet." And he leaped up and was walking.\f + \fr 14:10 \ft "Then the lame will leap like a deer" (Isaiah 35:6) — the promise of the age of restoration, happening now in a town with no synagogue in it.\f*
\v 11 And the crowds, seeing what Paul had done, lifted up their voice in Lycaonian, saying, "The gods have come down to us in the likeness of men!"\f + \fr 14:11 \ft They speak their own language, not the Greek Paul was preaching in, which is why he does not grasp what is happening until the animals appear. And what they say is not random superstition. There was a story attached to this very region: Zeus and Hermes came through the Phrygian hill country disguised as travellers, were turned away at every door but one, and drowned the villages that refused them (Ovid, \tl Metamorphoses\tl* 8.618-724). These people are not going to make that mistake.\f*
\v 12 And they called Barnabas Zeus, and Paul Hermes, since he was the one who led the word.\f + \fr 14:12 \ft Hermes was the herald and spokesman of the gods, so the man doing the talking must be him; the one standing with him in silence must be the chief god.\f*
\v 13 And the priest of the Zeus who was in front of the city brought bulls and garlands to the gates and wanted to make sacrifice along with the crowds \add [i.e. the temple's own priest, with bulls, at the city gate — a full public sacrifice]\add*.
\v 14 But when the apostles Barnabas and Paul heard it, they tore their clothes\f + \fr 14:14 \ft The Jewish response to hearing blasphemy (Matthew 26:65; compare 2 Kings 19:1). They are not embarrassed; they are horrified. Two chapters ago a king accepted exactly this and was struck dead for it (12:22-23).\f* and rushed out into the crowd, crying out
\v 15 and saying, "Men, why are you doing these things? We too are men of like feelings with you, announcing good news to you, that you should turn from these empty things\f + \fr 14:15 \ft "Empty things" is the standing word of Israel's prophets for idols — "they went after emptiness and became empty" (Jeremiah 2:5). His hearers would not catch the allusion; to them it means simply "things that do nothing."\f* to the living God\f + \fr 14:15 \ft "The living God" is the whole contrast in two words. Idols are made of wood and stone and cannot act; this God is alive and does things (Joshua 3:10; 1 Samuel 17:26; 2 Kings 19:4). Note how differently Paul must begin here than in a synagogue. There is no Abraham, no Moses, no scripture to quote — no shared book at all. He starts at creation.\f*, who made the heaven and the earth and the sea and everything in them.\f + \fr 14:15 \ft Almost word for word from Exodus 20:11 and Psalm 146:6. Against a world of gods who each own a department — the sky, the sea, the grain — he sets one God who made all the departments.\f*
\v 16 In the generations gone by he let all the nations go their own ways \add [i.e. allowed it, which is not the same as approving it, and that time is now over]\add*,
\v 17 and yet he did not leave himself without witness, doing good — giving you rains from heaven and fruitful seasons, filling your hearts full of food and gladness."\f + \fr 14:17 \ft The nations were never sent prophets, but they were sent rain. "Are there any among the idols of the nations that can bring rain?... Is it not you, O LORD our God?" (Jeremiah 14:22). Every harvest and every full table was evidence, and it went unread.\f*
\v 18 And saying these things they barely stopped the crowds from sacrificing to them \add [i.e. barely — even the gods' own denial almost failed to stop it]\add*.
\s1 Paul Stoned
\p
\v 19 But Jews came from Antioch and Iconium, and having won the crowds over and stoned Paul, they dragged him outside the city, supposing him to be dead.\f + \fr 14:19 \ft A hundred miles from Antioch and thirty from Iconium, to reach this town. And it is the same crowd: the people who were bringing bulls to sacrifice to him a few days ago pick up stones. Paul counts this among his sufferings years later — "once I was stoned" (2 Corinthians 11:25; compare 2 Timothy 3:11).\f*
\v 20 But when the disciples circled around him he got up and went into the city. And on the next day he went out with Barnabas to Derbe \add [i.e. the next day, on the road again]\add*.
\v 21 And after announcing the good news to that city and making a good many disciples, they went back to Lystra and to Iconium and to Antioch\f + \fr 14:21 \ft From Derbe the easy road home ran east through the Cilician Gates to Tarsus. Instead they turn round and walk back through the town where Paul was stoned, the town that plotted to stone him, and the town that expelled them. The point of the journey was never only to preach in new places.\f*,
\v 22 strengthening the souls of the disciples, urging them to hold on in the trust, and saying that we must come into the kingdom of God through many troubles.\f + \fr 14:22 \ft "Must" is the word of divine necessity that this book uses of the Messiah's own suffering — it had to be so (Luke 24:26; Acts 17:3). Trouble is not a risk that better planning avoids; it is the road. The man saying it has been stoned within the month, and the marks are on him. And note the other verb: he urges them to \tl hold on\tl* — one does not urge a man to keep doing what cannot be stopped.\f*
\v 23 And when they had appointed elders for them in each assembly\f + \fr 14:23 \ft The Greek verb is literally "having stretched out the hand," which is what voters did in a Greek assembly. It came to mean simply "appoint," and either sense — appointed by the apostles, or elected by a show of hands — is available here. "Appointed" is taken. Either way, these are not loose gatherings; every community is left with named leaders, on the pattern of the synagogue and of Jerusalem.\f*, praying with fasting, they handed them over to the Lord in whom they had trusted \add [i.e. they could not stay, so they put them in other hands]\add*.
\s1 Back to Antioch
\p
\v 24 And going through Pisidia they came into Pamphylia,
\v 25 and when they had spoken the word in Perga they went down to Attalia,
\v 26 and from there they sailed off to Antioch, where they had been handed over to the favor of God for the work they had filled full \add [i.e. back to the place they had been given away from, the assignment finished]\add*.
\v 27 And when they arrived and gathered the assembly together, they reported all that God had done with them, and that he had opened a door of trust for the nations.\f + \fr 14:27 \ft Note how the whole journey is summed up. Not "we preached in four cities and were run out of three." God did it, and God opened the door. The men walked through a door they had not forced.\f*
\v 28 And they spent no little time with the disciples.

\c 15
\s1 The Question: Must Gentiles Be Circumcised?
\p
\v 1 And some came down from Judea and were teaching the brothers, "Unless you are circumcised by the custom of Moses, you cannot be rescued."\f + \fr 15:1 \ft The demand is not invented. God gave circumcision to Abraham as the sign of the covenant, and said of the man who refuses it that he "shall be cut off from his people; he has broken my covenant" (Genesis 17:9-14). What is new is the word "rescued." Circumcision has been turned from the mark of the covenant into the condition of salvation, and that is what is being argued about.\f*
\v 2 And when Paul and Barnabas had no small standing-off and argument with them, they arranged for Paul and Barnabas and some others of them to go up to the apostles and elders in Jerusalem about this question \add [i.e. the question was too large for one congregation to answer]\add*.
\v 3 So they, sent on their way by the assembly, went through both Phoenicia and Samaria, telling in full of the turning of the nations, and they gave great joy to all the brothers \add [i.e. joy everywhere along the road — the objection is coming from one party, not from the churches]\add*.
\v 4 And when they arrived in Jerusalem they were welcomed by the assembly and the apostles and the elders, and they reported all that God had done with them.
\v 5 But some of those from the party of the Pharisees who had trusted rose up and said, "They must be circumcised, and ordered to keep the law of Moses."\f + \fr 15:5 \ft These are believers. They are not opponents of the movement; they are Pharisees inside it, and their position is consistent on its own terms: if Jesus is Israel's Messiah, then belonging to him means belonging to Israel, and belonging to Israel means the covenant sign and the covenant law. Note also that their demand has grown past the one in verse 1 — not circumcision only, but the whole law.\f*
\s1 Peter: God Made No Distinction
\p
\v 6 And the apostles and the elders were gathered together to see about this matter.
\v 7 And after there had been much arguing, Peter stood up and said to them, "Men, brothers, you know that from early days God chose among you that through my mouth the nations should hear the word of the good news and trust \add [i.e. this was settled years ago, and not by me]\add*.
\v 8 And God, who knows the heart\f + \fr 15:8 \ft One Greek word, "heart-knower," used in the New Testament only here and at 1:24. It is doing work in the argument: circumcision is a mark in the flesh, and the one who reads hearts has already given his verdict — by giving his Spirit.\f*, bore witness to them by giving them the Holy Spirit just as he did to us,
\v 9 and he made no distinction between us and them, cleansing their hearts by trust.\f + \fr 15:9 \ft "Made no distinction" is the same verb that has run through this whole story — the Spirit telling Peter to go with no distinction (10:20; 11:12), and the circumcision party distinguishing against him when he got back (11:2). And the cleansing is by trust, in the heart, not in the flesh. It is the same verb as the voice on the roof: "what God has made clean" (10:15).\f*
\v 10 So now why do you put God to the test\f + \fr 15:10 \ft A heavy charge. Testing God is what Israel did at Massah, and the law forbids it by name (Exodus 17:2, 7; Deuteronomy 6:16). To demand circumcision now is to challenge a verdict God has already handed down.\f* by putting a yoke on the neck of the disciples that neither our fathers nor we were strong enough to carry?\f + \fr 15:10 \ft He is not disparaging the law. He is saying what Israel's own prophets said about Israel: "my covenant that they broke" (Jeremiah 31:32); "they did not walk in my statutes" (Ezekiel 20:13); the long confession of Nehemiah 9. To lay on Gentiles the yoke that broke every generation of the covenant people is to require of them what has never once produced what it is now being said to produce.\f*
\v 11 But we trust that we are rescued through the favor of the Lord Jesus, in the same way as they."\f + \fr 15:11 \ft "Christ" after "the Lord Jesus" is in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — And watch which way round the comparison runs. The demand was that they be saved as we are. Peter says that we are saved as they are. Grace is not the concession made to Gentiles; grace is the ground everyone is standing on.\f*
\v 12 And the whole crowd fell silent, and they were listening to Barnabas and Paul telling all the signs and wonders God had done through them among the nations.
\s1 James Gives the Judgment
\p
\v 13 And after they were quiet, James\f + \fr 15:13 \ft The brother of Jesus (Galatians 1:19), who emerged as the head of the Jerusalem community at 12:17. He speaks last, and his ruling becomes the decision.\f* answered and said, "Men, brothers, listen to me.
\v 14 Simeon\f + \fr 15:14 \ft Peter's name in its Hebrew form, used of him nowhere else in this book. It suits the speaker and the room.\f* has told how God first looked in on the nations, to take from them a people for his name.\f + \fr 15:14 \ft Two words are set against each other on purpose. "Nations" is the word for everyone who is not Israel; "a people" is the word for Israel, God's own. Out of the not-people God is taking a people — which is what Hosea said: "I will say to Not-my-people, You are my people" (Hosea 2:23). And "looked in on" is the verb used of God's saving visits to Israel in Egypt and in the days of the judges (Exodus 4:31; Ruth 1:6; Luke 1:68).\f*
\v 15 And with this the words of the prophets agree, as it is written:
\v 16 'After these things I will come back and rebuild the tent of David that has fallen down, and I will rebuild its ruins and set it straight again,
\v 17 so that the rest of mankind may seek out the Lord, and all the nations on whom my name has been called, says the Lord who does these things
\v 18 known from of old.'\f + \fr 15:16-18 \ft Amos 9:11-12, quoted from the Greek, and the Greek is not saying what the Hebrew says. The Hebrew has Israel possessing "the remnant of Edom"; the Greek, reading slightly different words, has "the rest of mankind" seeking the Lord. James's whole case rests on the Greek text his hearers used, where the rebuilt house of David exists so that the nations may seek God — and where those nations bear God's name without any word about circumcision. — Where "known from of old" attaches is open: it can close the quotation, as here, or begin James's own comment. NA28 has only those three words; the majority text runs on, "known to God are all his works from the beginning of the world."\f*
\v 19 So my judgment is not to trouble those from the nations who are turning to God,
\v 20 but to write to them to keep away from the pollutions of idols and from sexual immorality and from what is strangled and from blood.\f + \fr 15:20 \ft NA28 gives the four in this order; the list is repeated in a different order at verse 29. — These are not four items chosen at random. They are close to what the law already required of the foreigner living in Israel, who was never asked to be circumcised: not to sacrifice elsewhere, not to eat blood, to drain the blood from what is killed, and to keep clear of the sexual practices listed in Leviticus 18 (Leviticus 17:8-16; 18:26). The Torah itself distinguished what it asked of the resident foreigner from what it asked of the native-born, and James applies that distinction here. They are also, in practical terms, exactly the four things that have to hold if a law-keeping Jew and a Gentile believer are going to eat at the same table.\f*
\v 21 For from generations long past Moses has had those who proclaim him in every city, being read in the synagogues every Sabbath."
\s1 The Letter to Antioch
\p
\v 22 Then it seemed good to the apostles and the elders, with the whole assembly, to choose men from among themselves and send them to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas: Judas called Barsabbas and Silas, leading men among the brothers\f + \fr 15:22 \ft Silas — Silvanus in its Latin form — becomes Paul's companion for the next three chapters and is named as co-sender of both letters to Thessalonica. Jerusalem sends its own men so that Antioch hears the verdict from Jerusalem's mouth and not only from Paul's.\f*,
\v 23 writing by their hand: "The apostles and the elders, brothers, to the brothers throughout Antioch and Syria and Cilicia who are from the nations: greetings.\f + \fr 15:23 \ft The Greek can be read either as "the apostles and the elders, brothers" — naming the writers, then addressing the readers — or as a single title, "the elder brothers." The first is taken here.\f*
\v 24 Since we have heard that some who went out from us have troubled you with words, unsettling your souls, though we gave them no orders\f + \fr 15:24 \ft The disavowal is flat: those men had no authorization from Jerusalem. "Unsettling" translates a word used of dismantling a building or pulling down fortifications — they were not merely confusing people; they were taking apart what had been built. — "Saying, You must be circumcised and keep the law" is added here in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*,
\v 25 it seemed good to us, having come to one mind, to choose men and send them to you with our beloved Barnabas and Paul,
\v 26 men who have handed over their lives for the name of our Lord Jesus Christ \add [i.e. and the marks of it are on one of them]\add*.
\v 27 So we have sent Judas and Silas, and they will also report the same things by mouth.
\v 28 For it seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us to lay no further burden on you than these necessary things:\f + \fr 15:28 \ft The boldest sentence in the letter. The assembly does not merely announce what it has decided; it names the Holy Spirit as party to the decision. Everything in this book has prepared for the claim — the Spirit fell on Cornelius, the Spirit sent Barnabas and Saul, the Spirit told Peter to go and not distinguish — and the council is saying that the same Spirit has been in the room.\f*
\v 29 to keep away from what is sacrificed to idols and from blood and from what is strangled and from sexual immorality. If you keep yourselves from these, you will do well. Farewell."
\s1 The Letter Read at Antioch
\p
\v 30 So they were sent off and came down to Antioch, and gathering the crowd together they handed over the letter.
\v 31 And when they read it they rejoiced at the encouragement \add [i.e. the men from Judea had unsettled them; the letter settles them]\add*.
\v 32 And Judas and Silas, being prophets themselves, encouraged the brothers with many words and strengthened them.
\v 33 And after spending some time they were sent off in peace from the brothers to those who had sent them.\f + \fr 15:33 \ft "But it seemed good to Silas to remain there" is added as verse 34 in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28, and the numbering here runs from 33 to 35.\f*
\v 35 But Paul and Barnabas stayed on in Antioch, teaching and announcing the good news of the word of the Lord, with many others as well.
\s1 Paul and Barnabas Part
\p
\v 36 And after some days Paul said to Barnabas, "Let us go back and look in on the brothers in every city where we announced the word of the Lord, to see how they are." \add [i.e. not a new mission but a check on the old one, left behind in cities that had rioted]\add*
\v 37 Now Barnabas wanted to take along John, who was called Mark, as well;
\v 38 but Paul thought it right not to take along the one who had left them in Pamphylia and had not gone with them to the work.\f + \fr 15:38 \ft "The work" is the Spirit's own word for the assignment at 13:2. Paul is not weighing a personality; he is saying the man walked out on a commission. Barnabas is doing for Mark what he had once done for Paul when nobody in Jerusalem would touch him (9:27), and Mark is his cousin (Colossians 4:10).\f*
\v 39 And there was a sharpness between them, so that they separated from each other, and Barnabas took Mark and sailed off to Cyprus;\f + \fr 15:39 \ft The Greek word is strong — a flare of temper, not a polite disagreement. The author reports it and passes no verdict on either man. What he does report is the result: one journey becomes two, and the word goes further because the two men could not travel together.\f*
\v 40 but Paul chose Silas and went out, handed over to the favor of the Lord by the brothers.
\v 41 And he went through Syria and Cilicia, strengthening the assemblies.

\c 16
\s1 Timothy Joins Them
\p
\v 1 And he came down also to Derbe and to Lystra. And look — a certain disciple was there named Timothy, the son of a trusting Jewish woman but of a Greek father,
\v 2 who was well spoken of by the brothers in Lystra and Iconium.
\v 3 Paul wanted this man to go out with him, and he took him and circumcised him because of the Jews who were in those places, for they all knew that his father was a Greek.\f + \fr 16:3 \ft This is not a reversal of the decision just reached in Jerusalem, and the difference is worth being clear about. The council ruled that Gentiles need not become Jews. Timothy already is a Jew — Jewish descent ran through the mother — but his Greek father had left him uncircumcised, and everyone in the district knew it. He is not being made a Jew; an irregularity in a Jewish man's standing is being repaired, so that he can walk into synagogues at Paul's side without the question swallowing every conversation.\f*
\v 4 And as they went through the cities they handed over to them the decisions to keep that had been reached by the apostles and elders in Jerusalem \add [i.e. the letter of chapter fifteen, delivered city by city]\add*.
\v 5 So the assemblies were being made firm in the trust and were growing in number day by day.
\s1 The Spirit Redirects Them to Macedonia
\p
\v 6 And they went through the Phrygian and Galatian country, having been kept by the Holy Spirit from speaking the word in Asia \add [i.e. barred from the obvious province, with Ephesus in it]\add*.
\v 7 And coming to Mysia they tried to go into Bithynia, and the Spirit of Jesus did not let them;\f + \fr 16:7 \ft NA28 reads "the Spirit of Jesus," a phrase that occurs nowhere else in this book; the majority text has only "the Spirit." It says plainly what the book has assumed throughout: the Spirit directing this mission is the Spirit of the risen Jesus, who poured him out (2:33).\f*
\v 8 so passing by Mysia they came down to Troas \add [i.e. two doors shut, and the road running out at the coast]\add*.
\v 9 And a vision appeared to Paul during the night: a certain Macedonian man was standing and calling him and saying, "Cross over to Macedonia and help us."\f + \fr 16:9 \ft He asks for help, not for instruction — the verb is the one the Psalms use when a man cries to God to come to his aid. The Spirit had said only where they could not go; the direction of the mission arrives as a plea from the place that needs it.\f*
\v 10 And when he had seen the vision, at once we sought to go out to Macedonia, putting it together that God had called us to announce the good news to them.\f + \fr 16:10 \ft The narrative changes person here without a word of explanation: "they" becomes "we," and stays "we" until Paul leaves Philippi (verse 40). It happens again at 20:5, 21:1, and 27:1. This is as close as the writer comes to telling us he was there. — Note also "putting it together." No one is told to go to Macedonia. They reason it out from the doors that shut and the door that opened.\f*
\s1 Lydia at Philippi
\p
\v 11 So putting out from Troas we ran a straight course to Samothrace, and on the next day to Neapolis,
\v 12 and from there to Philippi, which is a leading city of that district of Macedonia, a colony\f + \fr 16:12 \ft The Greek here is unsettled, and "a leading city of that district" and "the first city of the district" are both defensible; the first is taken. — "Colony" is the Latin word carried straight over. Augustus refounded Philippi after the battle fought there in 42 BC and settled it with veterans. Its people held Roman citizenship, its officials were Roman, its language of record was Latin, and it was proud of all three. Hold on to that; it decides how this chapter ends.\f*. And we spent some days in this city.
\v 13 And on the day of the Sabbath we went outside the gate beside a river, where we supposed there was a place of prayer\f + \fr 16:13 \ft NA28 reads "where we supposed there was a place of prayer"; the majority text has "where prayer was customarily made." — A synagogue needed ten Jewish men. They go looking outside the gate instead, and find women, which suggests the Jewish community in this Roman colony was very small and largely female. The gospel arrives in Europe at a riverside, in front of a handful of women, and the author reports it without remark.\f*, and we sat down and spoke to the women who had gathered.
\v 14 And a certain woman named Lydia was listening, a seller of purple from the city of Thyatira\f + \fr 16:14 \ft Thyatira was known for its dyers; purple cloth was a luxury good, and a woman trading in it across the Aegean from her home city is a woman of means running her own business.\f*, who feared God; and the Lord opened her heart to give attention to what Paul said.\f + \fr 16:14 \ft The verb is the one used at the end of Luke's Gospel for what the risen Jesus did on the Emmaus road: he opened the scriptures, their eyes were opened, he opened their minds (Luke 24:31, 32, 45). Two things happen in this sentence and neither is redundant. Paul speaks, and the Lord opens. What the opening produces is not a trance but attention.\f*
\v 15 And when she was immersed, and her household, she begged us, saying, "If you have judged me to be trusting in the Lord, come into my house and stay." And she pressed us.\f + \fr 16:15 \ft The verb is a forceful one — she overrode them. It is the same verb the two on the Emmaus road used on Jesus: "they urged him strongly, Stay with us" (Luke 24:29). Her house becomes the meeting place of the Philippian church (verse 40).\f*
\s1 The Slave Girl with the Python Spirit
\p
\v 16 And it happened, as we were going to the place of prayer, that a certain servant girl met us who had a spirit, a python\f + \fr 16:16 \ft Greek \tl pneuma pythōna\tl*, kept as it stands rather than turned into "a spirit of divination." The Python was the serpent that guarded the oracle at Delphi before Apollo killed it, and the priestess there prophesied as one possessed. The Greek Old Testament uses a related term for the medium the law forbids Israel to consult (Leviticus 19:31; Deuteronomy 18:11) — the sort of woman Saul went to at Endor. The word names a famous Greek institution and a forbidden Hebrew practice at the same time.\f*, and she brought her masters much profit by fortune-telling \add [i.e. her masters, plural — she was owned by more than one man, as an investment is]\add*.
\v 17 She followed after Paul and us and cried out, saying, "These men are slaves of the Most High God, who announce to you a way of rescue."\f + \fr 16:17 \ft There is no article in the Greek: "a way of rescue," not "the way." And "Most High God" is a title Israel used of the LORD (Genesis 14:18-20) and Greeks used of Zeus. Every word she says is true, and in that city, out of that mouth, all of it can be heard as an endorsement of one more god among the others. That is the problem.\f*
\v 18 And she did this for many days. But Paul, worn out, turned and said to the spirit, "I order you in the name of Jesus Christ to come out of her." And it came out that very hour.\f + \fr 16:18 \ft He addresses the spirit, not the girl. And note that he is not pleased by the free advertising — the verb says he was worn down by it. True words from that source do not help; they file the gospel among the things the city already worships.\f*
\s1 Beaten and Jailed
\p
\v 19 But when her masters saw that their hope of profit had gone out, they seized Paul and Silas and dragged them into the marketplace to the rulers \add [i.e. what they had lost was income, and that is what the whole prosecution is about]\add*,
\v 20 and bringing them to the magistrates they said, "These men are throwing our city into turmoil — they are Jews —
\v 21 and they announce customs it is not lawful for us to take up or to do, being Romans."\f + \fr 16:21 \ft Not one word about the girl, the spirit, or the money. The charge is built for the room: outsiders, Jews, unsettling a Roman colony with foreign practices. Nothing specific is alleged, and nothing needs to be. Remember the wording — in sixteen verses it will come back on the men who accept it.\f*
\v 22 And the crowd rose up together against them, and the magistrates tore their clothes off them and ordered them to be beaten with rods\f + \fr 16:22 \ft The Roman punishment, administered by the lictors who walked in front of the magistrates carrying the rods. Paul counts it up years later: "three times I was beaten with rods" (2 Corinthians 11:25). There has been no trial, no evidence, and no defense.\f*.
\v 23 And after laying many blows on them they threw them into prison, ordering the jailer to keep them securely.
\v 24 Having received such an order, he threw them into the inner prison and fastened their feet in the stocks \add [i.e. the deepest cell, and the legs forced apart in the wood]\add*.
\s1 Midnight, and an Earthquake
\p
\v 25 And about midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them \add [i.e. a congregation of thieves and debtors, with no choice but to hear]\add*.
\v 26 And suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken; and at once all the doors were opened and everyone's bonds were loosed.\f + \fr 16:26 \ft The shaking is the same word used of the room where the community prayed in 4:31. And notice how precise it is: the doors open and the chains fall, and the building does not come down on the men inside. This is not damage; it is a release.\f*
\v 27 And the jailer woke up, and seeing the doors of the prison standing open, he drew his sword and was about to do away with himself, supposing the prisoners had escaped.\f + \fr 16:27 \ft A guard answered with his own life for a prisoner who got away — as Herod's soldiers had discovered (12:19). He is not despairing; he is doing the arithmetic.\f*
\v 28 But Paul called out with a loud voice, "Do yourself no harm — we are all here."\f + \fr 16:28 \ft The doors are open and the chains are off, and nobody has gone. The author does not explain why the other prisoners stayed. The effect is that the jailer's life is the first thing saved in this story.\f*
\v 29 And calling for lights he rushed in, and trembling he fell down before Paul and Silas,
\v 30 and bringing them outside he said, "Sirs, what must I do to be rescued?"\f + \fr 16:30 \ft The bluntest form of the question anywhere in this book, and it comes from a pagan who has heard no sermon — only two men singing in the dark, an earthquake that let everyone go, and a shout that stopped his hand.\f*
\v 31 And they said, "Trust on the Lord Jesus, and you will be rescued, you and your household."\f + \fr 16:31 \ft "Christ" after "the Lord Jesus" is in the majority text and most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — The household is the unit here as it was with Lydia (verse 15) and will be with Crispus (18:8). It does not mean a man's faith is credited to his slaves and children; the next verse has the word spoken to every one of them, and verse 34 has the whole house rejoicing at its own believing.\f*
\v 32 And they spoke the word of the Lord to him, with all who were in his house.
\v 33 And taking them along that very hour of the night he washed their wounds, and he was immersed at once, he and all his people \add [i.e. he washes the marks his own prison put on them, and is washed himself the same hour]\add*.
\v 34 And bringing them up into his house he set a table before them, and he was glad with his whole household at having trusted God.
\s1 Roman Citizens
\p
\v 35 And when day came the magistrates sent the officers, saying, "Release those men."
\v 36 And the jailer reported these words to Paul: "The magistrates have sent word that you should be released. So come out now and go in peace."
\v 37 But Paul said to them, "They beat us in public without a trial, men who are Romans, and threw us into prison; and now they are throwing us out secretly? No indeed — let them come themselves and lead us out."\f + \fr 16:37 \ft He has been a Roman citizen the whole time and has said nothing until now. Every clause is a charge. Roman law forbade the beating of a citizen who had not been tried, and Cicero could make a whole prosecution out of it: "to bind a Roman citizen is a crime, to flog him an abomination, to kill him all but parricide." The charge against them was practising what is "not lawful for Romans"; it is the magistrates who have done that. — And the demand is not wounded pride. The beating was public; a quiet release would leave the city thinking they were guilty, and Lydia and the jailer have to go on living here.\f*
\v 38 And the officers reported these words to the magistrates; and they were afraid when they heard that they were Romans,
\v 39 and they came and begged them, and they led them out and asked them to leave the city \add [i.e. came in person, as he had required]\add*.
\v 40 So going out of the prison they went to Lydia, and when they saw the brothers they encouraged them and left.\f + \fr 16:40 \ft The narrative slips back out of "we" here and does not resume until 20:5, when Paul passes through Philippi again — which suggests the writer stayed behind with the congregation he had helped to start.\f*

\c 17
\s1 Thessalonica
\p
\v 1 And traveling through Amphipolis and Apollonia they came to Thessalonica, where there was a synagogue of the Jews\f + \fr 17:1 \ft A hundred miles down the Via Egnatia, and two considerable cities passed without a word about preaching in them. The clause explains the route: Thessalonica, the provincial capital, had what the towns along the way did not.\f*.
\v 2 And as was Paul's custom he went in to them, and for three Sabbaths he reasoned with them from the scriptures\f + \fr 17:2 \ft "As was his custom" is the phrase used of Jesus going into the synagogue at Nazareth (Luke 4:16). And the verb is the one from which "dialogue" comes: he argued back and forth with them, out of the book they both held.\f*,
\v 3 opening them up and setting out\f + \fr 17:3 \ft Two words worth pausing on. "Opening" is what the risen Jesus did to the scriptures and to the minds of the two on the Emmaus road (Luke 24:32, 45), and what the Lord did to Lydia's heart (16:14). "Setting out" is literally placing something in front of someone, as a host sets food before a guest — laying the evidence on the table for them to examine.\f* that the Christ had to suffer and rise out of the dead, and that "this is the Christ — the Jesus whom I announce to you."\f + \fr 17:3 \ft The argument runs in two steps: from the scriptures, that the Christ must suffer and rise; then, that this particular man is he. The first step is the harder one, and it is the one Jesus himself made on the Emmaus road — "was it not necessary for the Christ to suffer these things?" (Luke 24:26).\f*
\v 4 And some of them were persuaded and were allotted to Paul and Silas, and a great crowd of the devout Greeks, and not a few of the leading women \add [i.e. some Jews, and a great many of the God-fearing Gentiles who had been attached to that synagogue]\add*.
\v 5 But the Jews became jealous and took along some wicked men from the loafers of the marketplace, and forming a mob they set the city in an uproar; and coming to Jason's house they looked for them, to bring them out to the people's assembly \add [i.e. the men who would not touch such company hired it]\add*.
\v 6 But when they did not find them, they dragged Jason and some brothers before the city officials\f + \fr 17:6 \ft Greek \tl politarchai\tl*, a title that appears in no classical author — but it appears on inscriptions from Thessalonica, and nowhere else does it fit. It is the local word for the local magistrates, and the writer has it right.\f*, shouting, "These men who have upset the whole inhabited world have come here too,
\v 7 and Jason has taken them in. And all of these act against Caesar's decrees, saying there is another king, Jesus."\f + \fr 17:7 \ft This is the most dangerous charge brought in the book so far, and it is a translation. Paul has been arguing that Jesus is the Christ — and "Christ" means the anointed king of David's line. His accusers put that into the language of Roman law and hand the magistrates a case of treason. It is what was done to Jesus himself: "we found this man... saying that he himself is Christ, a king" (Luke 23:2).\f*
\v 8 And they stirred up the crowd and the city officials who heard this,
\v 9 and after taking security from Jason and the rest, they released them.\f + \fr 17:9 \ft A bond, not a beating — these magistrates handle it better than the ones at Philippi. But the bond is posted against further disturbance, which means Jason is financially liable if Paul comes back. It may be what Paul means when he writes to this church that he tried again and again to return and was blocked (1 Thessalonians 2:18).\f*
\s1 Berea
\p
\v 10 But the brothers at once sent Paul and Silas away by night to Berea; and when they arrived they went into the synagogue of the Jews \add [i.e. straight into the synagogue again, in the next town]\add*.
\v 11 Now these were of better birth than those in Thessalonica: they welcomed the word with all eagerness, examining the scriptures daily to see whether these things were so.\f + \fr 17:11 \ft Greek \tl eugenesteroi\tl*, "of better birth," used of character rather than pedigree; most versions have "more noble," and the concrete word is kept here. — What the author praises is a combination: they took the message in eagerly, and they also checked it, every day, against the text. The verb for their examining is a courtroom word — sifting evidence. This is the one place in the book where the narrator stops to rate an audience, and this is what he rates highly.\f*
\v 12 So many of them trusted, and not a few of the Greek women of high standing, and men as well.
\v 13 But when the Jews from Thessalonica learned that the word of God had been announced by Paul in Berea too, they came there as well, stirring up and troubling the crowds \add [i.e. fifty miles, to do it again]\add*.
\v 14 Then the brothers at once sent Paul off to go as far as the sea, but Silas and Timothy stayed on there.
\v 15 And those who were escorting Paul brought him as far as Athens; and after receiving an order for Silas and Timothy to come to him as quickly as they could, they left \add [i.e. leaving him in Athens on his own]\add*.
\s1 Paul in Athens
\p
\v 16 Now while Paul was waiting for them in Athens, his spirit was being stirred within him as he saw the city full of idols.\f + \fr 17:16 \ft The Greek is one compound word: idol-swamped. Ancient writers said the same thing about Athens — Petronius joked that it was easier to meet a god there than a man. And the verb for Paul's reaction is sharp; it is the root of the word used for his quarrel with Barnabas at 15:39. This is not distaste. It is what a man formed by Exodus 20 feels when he walks through such a city.\f*
\v 17 So he reasoned in the synagogue with the Jews and the devout, and in the marketplace every day with those who happened to be there\f + \fr 17:17 \ft Two rooms at once. The synagogue, as always. And the \tl agora\tl* — where Socrates had done the same thing four hundred years earlier, buttonholing whoever came past. Paul takes up an Athenian practice in the Athenian place for it.\f*.
\v 18 And some of the Epicurean and Stoic philosophers also fell in with him\f + \fr 17:18 \ft The two dominant schools, and they would object for opposite reasons. Epicureans held that the gods exist but do not interfere, that the soul dies with the body, and that there is no judgment. Stoics held that a divine reason runs through everything and that virtue is living according to it — but the divine they meant was impersonal, and it did not act in a particular man at a particular date.\f*, and some said, "What is this seed-picker trying to say?"\f + \fr 17:18 \ft Greek \tl spermologos\tl*, a bird that hops about picking up stray seeds. As Athenian slang it means a man who has scavenged scraps of other people's learning and retails them without understanding any of it. The image is kept rather than flattened to "babbler."\f* And others, "He seems to be an announcer of foreign divinities" — because he was announcing the good news of Jesus and the rising.\f + \fr 17:18 \ft "Introducing new divinities" is word for word the charge on which Athens tried and executed Socrates (Plato, \tl Apology\tl* 24b). — Some have wondered whether they heard two names, Jesus and Anastasis — "Rising" — and took them for a god and his consort.\f*
\v 19 And they took hold of him and brought him to the Areopagus\f + \fr 17:19 \ft The Hill of Ares, and the council that met there — an ancient body which by this period still had oversight of religion, education, and public teaching in the city. Whether this is an inquiry or merely a hearing, it is the institution that would decide whether a new god may be introduced.\f*, saying, "May we know what this new teaching is that you are speaking?
\v 20 For you are bringing certain strange things to our ears, so we want to know what these things mean."
\v 21 (Now all the Athenians and the foreigners staying there spent their time on nothing else than telling or hearing something newer.)\f + \fr 17:21 \ft A commonplace about the city, and an old one: Thucydides called the Athenians addicted to innovation, and Demosthenes complained that they went about asking "is there any news?" instead of acting on the news they had. The author is telling us why Paul is getting a hearing, and it is not because they think it matters.\f*
\s1 Paul at the Areopagus
\p
\v 22 So Paul stood in the middle of the Areopagus and said, "Men of Athens, I see that in every way you are very religious.\f + \fr 17:22 \ft The Greek word \tl deisidaimonesterous\tl* runs both ways: "very religious" and "rather superstitious." A man standing before this council would mean the first, and the second is equally available in the word. "Very religious" is taken.\f*
\v 23 For as I passed through and looked closely at the objects of your worship, I even found an altar on which was written, 'To an unknown god.' So what you worship without knowing it, this I announce to you.\f + \fr 17:23 \ft Such altars are attested: Pausanias saw "altars of gods called unknown" near the Athenian harbour, and Diogenes Laertius reports that Epimenides once had nameless altars raised in the city to whatever god had sent a plague, since nobody knew which one to appease. Anxious not to slight a deity, they hedged. — Note the strategy. In a synagogue Paul starts from the scriptures they share; here he starts from something he has seen on a walk. He does not tell them they are wrong to worship. He tells them they do not know who they are worshiping, and offers to say.\f*
\v 24 The God who made the world and everything in it — this one, being Lord of heaven and earth, does not live in temples made by hands\f + \fr 17:24 \ft "Made by hands" is the Greek Old Testament's word for idols (Isaiah 46:6), and Stephen used it of the temple itself (7:48, quoting Isaiah 66:1). A Stoic in the audience would nod at the sentiment — Seneca wrote that God is not to be shut up in shrines. A Jew would hear the prophets. The sentence works on both ears at once.\f*,
\v 25 nor is he waited on by human hands as though he needed anything, since he himself gives life and breath and everything to all.\f + \fr 17:25 \ft "Who gives breath to the people on the earth" (Isaiah 42:5). And compare Psalm 50:9-12: "if I were hungry I would not tell you, for the world and its fullness are mine." A god who needs feeding is a god with a deficiency.\f*
\v 26 And he made from one every nation of men, to live on all the face of the earth, marking out the seasons appointed and the boundaries of where they live\f + \fr 17:26 \ft NA28 reads "from one"; most older English versions have "of one blood." — This lands harder in Athens than anywhere else it could be said. The Athenians held that they alone had sprung from their own soil, a people apart from all others; it was the centre of their civic pride. Paul tells them, in their own council chamber, that every nation came out of one man. And the setting of boundaries is Deuteronomy 32:8 — the nations receive their times and their borders from God, which is neither Epicurean indifference nor Stoic fate.\f*,
\v 27 to seek God — if perhaps they might feel after him and find him\f + \fr 17:27 \ft The verb is groping, feeling one's way in the dark with the hands because the eyes are no use — the word Moses uses of a blind man groping at noon (Deuteronomy 28:29). The searching is real, and it is impaired, and the "if perhaps" is in the Greek: it may not succeed.\f* — and yet he is not far from each one of us.
\v 28 For in him we live and move and are; as some of your own poets have said, 'For we are also his offspring.'\f + \fr 17:28 \ft Two quotations from pagan verse. The first is usually credited to Epimenides of Crete, addressing Zeus. The second is the fifth line of the \tl Phaenomena\tl* of Aratus, a Stoic poet from Paul's own province of Cilicia, and it is also about Zeus. Paul is not endorsing their theology. He is taking two things they got right — that we depend wholly on the divine, and that we are of its kin — and correcting the name they attached them to.\f*
\v 29 So being the offspring of God, we ought not to think that the divine is like gold or silver or stone, an engraving of human craft and thought \add [i.e. if we came from him, he cannot be less alive than we are]\add*.
\v 30 So God has overlooked the times of ignorance, and now he orders all men everywhere to turn back\f + \fr 17:30 \ft "Overlooked" is not "approved." It is the same patience Paul described at Lystra: "in past generations he let all the nations go their own ways" (14:16). What has changed is that the message has now arrived, and with it a command — and the command is universal, to everyone, everywhere. Note also the word-play the Greek carries: they worship what is unknown (verse 23), in times of ignorance, and both words are built on the same root.\f*,
\v 31 because he has set a day on which he is about to judge the inhabited world in righteousness by a man he has marked out, having given trust to all by raising him out of the dead."\f + \fr 17:31 \ft The Greek \tl pistin paraschōn pasin\tl* is either "having given trust to all" or "having furnished proof to all"; the first keeps the author's usual word and is taken here. — Three claims land at once, and every one of them is hard for this room: a fixed day of judgment, a single man appointed to conduct it, and a body raised from the dead. "He will judge the world in righteousness" is Psalm 96:13. The man is not named. That is what the next question would have been.\f*
\p
\v 32 And when they heard of a rising of the dead, some sneered, but others said, "We will hear you about this again."\f + \fr 17:32 \ft It is the resurrection that splits the room, and it would. In the play performed in this city about the founding of this very court, Apollo declares over the first trial ever held here: "once the dust has drunk a man's blood, there is no rising" — and the word Aeschylus uses is the word Paul has just used (\tl Eumenides\tl* 647-648). For a Greek the body is what the soul escapes; raising it again is not a hope but an absurdity.\f*
\v 33 So Paul went out from their midst \add [i.e. no riot, no mass turning, no expulsion — he simply left]\add*.
\v 34 But some men joined him and trusted — among them Dionysius the Areopagite\f + \fr 17:34 \ft A member of the council that had just heard him. The argument reached inside the body that was weighing it.\f* and a woman named Damaris, and others with them.

\c 18
\s1 Corinth: Aquila and Priscilla
\p
\v 1 After these things he left Athens and came to Corinth\f + \fr 18:1 \ft Capital of the province of Achaia, and a city with a port on either side of its isthmus — one facing Italy, one facing Asia — so that cargo and even small vessels were hauled overland across the neck rather than risk the voyage round the Peloponnese. Rome levelled the old city in 146 BC and refounded it a century later, settling it largely with freed slaves. It was rich, crowded, transient, and had a name for vice that had passed into the language.\f*.
\v 2 And he found a certain Jew named Aquila, a Pontian by birth, who had recently come from Italy with Priscilla his wife, because Claudius had ordered all the Jews to leave Rome; and he went to them\f + \fr 18:2 \ft Suetonius records the expulsion: Claudius "drove the Jews out of Rome, since they were constantly making disturbances at the instigation of Chrestus" — almost certainly a garbled "Christus," which would mean the trouble was an argument inside the synagogues of Rome about whether Jesus is the Messiah. Note that the author never describes Paul converting this couple. Everywhere else he narrates a conversion when there is one. These two look to have arrived already believing.\f*,
\v 3 and because he was of the same trade he stayed with them, and they worked; for by trade they were tentmakers \add [i.e. he worked with his hands for his keep, until the money came from Macedonia]\add*.
\v 4 And he reasoned in the synagogue every Sabbath and persuaded both Jews and Greeks.
\s1 The Turn to the Gentiles at Corinth
\p
\v 5 And when Silas and Timothy came down from Macedonia, Paul was held fast to the word\f + \fr 18:5 \ft NA28 reads \tl syneicheto tō logō\tl*, "was gripped by, held fast to, the word"; the majority text has "was pressed in the spirit." — What changed is in Paul's own letters: the Macedonian churches sent money (2 Corinthians 11:9; Philippians 4:15), and he could leave the workshop.\f*, bearing witness to the Jews that the Christ is Jesus.
\v 6 But when they set themselves against him and blasphemed, he shook out his clothes and said to them, "Your blood be on your own head; I am clean. From now on I will go to the nations."\f + \fr 18:6 \ft The words come from Ezekiel's watchman: if the watchman sounds the trumpet and the people will not heed it, "their blood shall be on their own head," and the watchman has saved his life (Ezekiel 33:1-9). Paul has sounded it. — And "from now on" means in this city. Thirteen verses later he walks into the synagogue at Ephesus.\f*
\v 7 And moving on from there he went into the house of a certain man named Titius Justus, who worshiped God, whose house was next door to the synagogue \add [i.e. next door — near enough that anyone who wanted to hear him still could]\add*.
\v 8 And Crispus the synagogue ruler trusted the Lord with his whole household, and many of the Corinthians who heard trusted and were immersed.\f + \fr 18:8 \ft Not a member but the presiding officer of the synagogue — the man whose job was to run its worship and decide who spoke. Paul names him as one of the very few people he baptized personally at Corinth (1 Corinthians 1:14). The verbs in the second half are all continuous: they kept hearing, kept believing, kept being immersed, over the eighteen months that follow.\f*
\v 9 And the Lord said to Paul by night in a vision, "Do not be afraid, but speak and do not be silent,\f + \fr 18:9 \ft The command implies the fear was real, and he had earned it — beaten at Philippi, run out of Thessalonica, chased from Berea. "Do not be afraid, for I am with you" is the standing form of words when God commissions a man in Israel's scriptures: to Abraham, to Isaac, to Jacob, to Joshua, to Gideon, to Jeremiah (Genesis 15:1; 26:24; 46:3; Joshua 1:5-9; Judges 6:12, 23; Jeremiah 1:8).\f*
\v 10 because I am with you, and no one will lay hands on you to harm you, because I have many people in this city."\f + \fr 18:10 \ft "People" here is \tl laos\tl*, the word the Greek Old Testament keeps for Israel — God's own, by covenant. He says it of a pagan port city, of people who have not yet believed anything. They are already his, and the preaching is how they will be gathered rather than how they will be manufactured. James had used the same word at the council: God is taking "a people for his name" out of the nations (15:14).\f*
\v 11 And he stayed a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them \add [i.e. longer than he had stayed anywhere yet]\add*.
\s1 Before Gallio
\p
\v 12 But while Gallio was proconsul of Achaia\f + \fr 18:12 \ft Lucius Junius Gallio, elder brother of the philosopher Seneca. An inscription found at Delphi records a letter of Claudius naming him proconsul of Achaia, which fixes his year of office at about 51-52 and makes this the firmest date in the whole book. "Proconsul" is also the correct title: Achaia was a senatorial province, and the author gets the office right here as he does at Cyprus, Philippi, and Thessalonica.\f*, the Jews rose up with one accord against Paul and brought him to the judgment seat\f + \fr 18:12 \ft The \tl bēma\tl*, the raised stone platform in the forum from which the governor gave judgment. The one at Corinth has been excavated and can still be stood on. This is the highest court in the province, and a ruling here sets the precedent for all of it.\f*,
\v 13 saying, "This man is persuading people to worship God against the law."\f + \fr 18:13 \ft Whose law is left carefully unsaid. If Roman law, the charge is that he promotes a religion the state has not licensed; if Jewish law, it is a quarrel the governor might be induced to punish. The vagueness is the strategy, and Gallio sees straight through it.\f*
\v 14 And as Paul was about to open his mouth, Gallio said to the Jews, "If it were some wrongdoing or wicked crime, Jews, I would have reason to put up with you \add [i.e. Paul is acquitted before he has said a word]\add*;
\v 15 but if these are questions about a word and names and your own law, you will see to it yourselves. I do not want to be a judge of these things."\f + \fr 18:15 \ft This is the moment the political question raised at Philippi and Thessalonica gets an answer. Twice the charge has been that the message is an offence against Rome — unlawful customs, another king. Here the highest official in the province hears it out and rules that it is nothing of the kind: an internal dispute over words, names, and Jewish law, outside his jurisdiction. Preaching that Jesus is the Christ is not a crime against Caesar. The mission proceeds from here under that ruling.\f*
\v 16 And he drove them away from the judgment seat.
\v 17 And they all took hold of Sosthenes the synagogue ruler and beat him in front of the judgment seat; and none of this mattered to Gallio.\f + \fr 18:17 \ft NA28 reads "they all"; most older English versions have "all the Greeks," which makes it a crowd turning on a Jew once the governor has shown he will not intervene. Without that word it could as easily be the synagogue turning on the leader who lost its case. — Sosthenes is presumably Crispus's successor. A man of that name appears as Paul's co-sender at the head of 1 Corinthians, which if it is the same man means this synagogue lost two rulers in a row.\f*
\s1 Back to Antioch, by Way of Ephesus
\p
\v 18 And Paul, after staying on many more days, said goodbye to the brothers and sailed off for Syria, and with him Priscilla and Aquila; he had his head shaved in Cenchreae, for he had a vow.\f + \fr 18:18 \ft A Nazirite vow, or something modelled on it: no wine, no cutting of the hair, until the term is finished, when the hair is shaved and burned with the offering (Numbers 6:1-21). Set it beside verse 6. The man who said "from now on I will go to the nations" is keeping a Jewish vow twelve verses later. His turning to the Gentiles is a change of audience, not of religion.\f*
\v 19 And they came down to Ephesus, and he left them there \add [i.e. left them where he means to come back to]\add*; and he himself went into the synagogue and reasoned with the Jews.
\v 20 And when they asked him to stay a longer time he did not agree,
\v 21 but said goodbye and said, "I will come back to you again, if God wills," and he put out from Ephesus.\f + \fr 18:21 \ft "I must by all means keep this coming feast in Jerusalem" is added before this in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — Note that this is the city the Spirit had shut him out of (16:6). The refusal was a matter of timing, not of principle, and he now holds even his own intentions loosely.\f*
\v 22 And landing at Caesarea he went up and greeted the assembly, and went down to Antioch.\f + \fr 18:22 \ft One goes "up" to Jerusalem and "down" from it, whatever the compass says, and the unnamed city he goes up to from Caesarea can only be Jerusalem. In one verse the second journey closes: back to the mother community, and then back to the congregation that had sent him out.\f*
\v 23 And after spending some time there he went out, going through the Galatian country and Phrygia in order, strengthening all the disciples \add [i.e. the third journey begins with no ceremony at all]\add*.
\s1 Apollos
\p
\v 24 Now a certain Jew named Apollos, an Alexandrian by birth, an eloquent man, came down to Ephesus, and he was powerful in the scriptures.\f + \fr 18:24 \ft Alexandria was the intellectual capital of the Jewish dispersion — the city of Philo, and the city where the Greek Old Testament was made. A Jew trained there would have both the scriptures and the rhetorical schooling of the Greek world, which is exactly what the two descriptions here say.\f*
\v 25 This man had been instructed in the way of the Lord, and being fervent in spirit he was speaking and teaching accurately the things about Jesus, though he knew only the immersion of John.\f + \fr 18:25 \ft Everything he taught was right; it simply stopped early. He knows Jesus, and he knows John's baptism of turning back, and there he ends — which is to say his information reached Alexandria before any account of Pentecost did. News of a prophet in Judea travelled the trade routes; news of the Spirit travelled with the apostles.\f*
\v 26 And he began to speak boldly in the synagogue; and when Priscilla and Aquila heard him, they took him aside and set out the way of God to him more accurately.\f + \fr 18:26 \ft Three things worth noticing. They do not correct him in public. They do not treat him as a false teacher — "more accurately" concedes that he was accurate already, and adds what was missing. And the wife is named first, as she is again in Romans 16:3 and 2 Timothy 4:19, which was not the usual order for a married couple.\f*
\v 27 And when he wanted to cross over to Achaia, the brothers encouraged him and wrote to the disciples to welcome him\f + \fr 18:27 \ft A letter of recommendation, the early communities' means of vouching for a travelling teacher to people who had never met him (compare Romans 16:1-2; 2 Corinthians 3:1).\f*; and when he arrived he was a great help to those who had trusted through favor,
\v 28 for he vigorously refuted the Jews in public, showing through the scriptures that the Christ is Jesus \add [i.e. the same case Paul had made, now argued by a man trained to argue it]\add*.

\c 19
\s1 Disciples Who Knew Only John's Immersion
\p
\v 1 And it happened, while Apollos was in Corinth, that Paul went through the upper parts and came down to Ephesus and found some disciples,
\v 2 and he said to them, "Did you receive the Holy Spirit when you trusted?"\f + \fr 19:2 \ft The Greek is an aorist participle, which is either "when you trusted" or "after you had trusted"; the first is taken. Either way the question assumes the two belong together — that is how it has gone in every conversion in this book so far.\f* And they said to him, "But we have not even heard whether there is a Holy Spirit."\f + \fr 19:2 \ft They cannot mean they have never heard of the Spirit of God; any Jew reading the prophets knows of him. They mean they have not heard that he has been given. Their information stops before Pentecost. They are standing in the age of waiting, and nobody has told them it is over.\f*
\v 3 And he said, "Into what, then, were you immersed?" And they said, "Into John's immersion."
\v 4 And Paul said, "John immersed with an immersion of turning back, telling the people to trust in the one coming after him — that is, in Jesus."\f + \fr 19:4 \ft He does not call John's baptism false. He says what it was for. It pointed forward; these men are standing at the pointer without having looked where it points.\f*
\v 5 And when they heard this they were immersed into the name of the Lord Jesus,\f + \fr 19:5 \ft The one place in the book where anyone is immersed twice, and the reason is in what the first immersion was. John's belonged to the time before the death, the rising, and the outpouring; immersion into the name of the Lord Jesus belongs to the time after. They are not two versions of the same thing. Apollos, whose case was similar (18:25), is not said to have been immersed again.\f*
\v 6 and when Paul laid his hands on them the Holy Spirit came on them, and they were speaking in tongues and prophesying \add [i.e. the two signs of Pentecost, in a fourth city and on a fourth kind of people]\add*.
\v 7 And there were about twelve men in all.
\s1 Two Years in the Hall of Tyrannus
\p
\v 8 And going into the synagogue he spoke boldly for three months, reasoning and persuading about the kingdom of God \add [i.e. three months, where in other cities he had three Sabbaths]\add*.
\v 9 But when some were being hardened\f + \fr 19:9 \ft The verb is the one used of Pharaoh's heart in Exodus and of the wilderness generation in Psalm 95:8 — "do not harden your hearts." It is not failure to follow an argument; it is refusal to give way to one.\f* and would not obey, speaking evil of the Way in front of the crowd, he moved away from them and set the disciples apart, reasoning every day in the lecture hall of Tyrannus\f + \fr 19:9 \ft A \tl scholē\tl* is a hall used for lectures and debate. Some manuscripts add "from the fifth hour to the tenth" — roughly eleven to four, the hot part of the day when business stopped and such a hall stood empty. If that is right, Paul worked at his trade in the morning (20:34) and taught through the siesta. Either way the change matters: the message has moved out of a Jewish house of worship into a Greek lecture room, and off the Sabbath cycle onto every day of the week.\f*.
\v 10 And this went on for two years, so that all who lived in Asia heard the word of the Lord, both Jews and Greeks.\f + \fr 19:10 \ft Paul does not tour the province; he stays put in its capital and the province comes to him and goes home again. He had never met the Colossians face to face (Colossians 2:1), and Colossae is in Asia. The seven congregations addressed in Revelation 2-3 are all in this province, and this is most likely when they began.\f*
\s1 The Sons of Sceva, and the Burning of the Books
\p
\v 11 And God was doing works of power of no ordinary kind through Paul's hands \add [i.e. God doing them, and Paul's hands the instrument]\add*,
\v 12 so that even handkerchiefs and aprons were carried from his skin to those who were sick, and the diseases left them and the evil spirits went out.\f + \fr 19:12 \ft Sweat-cloths and workmen's aprons — objects off a tentmaker's bench. Compare Peter's shadow (5:15) and the woman who touched the edge of Jesus's cloak (Luke 8:44). The point of recording it here is the contrast with what comes next: this is what real power looks like, and the next five verses show what borrowing it looks like.\f*
\v 13 But some of the traveling Jewish exorcists also took it in hand to name the name of the Lord Jesus over those who had evil spirits, saying, "I bind you by the Jesus whom Paul proclaims."\f + \fr 19:13 \ft Jewish exorcism was a recognized trade; Josephus says he watched one performed before Vespasian using formulas credited to Solomon. Hear what these men actually say: not "by Jesus my Lord" but "by the Jesus whom Paul proclaims." They have no standing with him at all. They are treating the name as a spell — a word that works if you pronounce it correctly — which is exactly how the magical handbooks of the period treat every divine name they collect.\f*
\v 14 And there were seven sons of a certain Sceva, a Jewish chief priest, who were doing this.\f + \fr 19:14 \ft No high priest named Sceva is known from any source. The title is most likely their own advertising: a practitioner who can claim the high-priestly line can charge more. The author lets the credentials stand, because the point is what happens to them.\f*
\v 15 But the evil spirit answered them, "Jesus I know, and Paul I understand — but who are you?"
\v 16 And the man in whom the evil spirit was leaped on them, overpowered them all and was so strong against them that they fled out of that house naked and wounded.\f + \fr 19:16 \ft The Greek word strictly means "both," yet seven sons have just been named; it has been rendered "them all," which is what the sentence must mean, though the word itself says "both." — One man against seven. The name is not a tool that comes free with the pronunciation. It is the authority of a living person, and it works for those he knows.\f*
\v 17 And this became known to all who lived in Ephesus, both Jews and Greeks, and fear fell on them all, and the name of the Lord Jesus was made great.
\v 18 And many of those who had trusted came, confessing and reporting what they had been doing \add [i.e. believers already, who had never let go of what they were still doing on the side]\add*.
\v 19 And a good many of those who had practiced meddling arts brought their books together and burned them in front of everyone; and they counted up their value and found it fifty thousand pieces of silver.\f + \fr 19:19 \ft Ephesus was the byword for this: "Ephesian letters" was a common phrase for magical formulas. Such texts were trade secrets and worth money accordingly — fifty thousand drachmas is something like a hundred and thirty years of a labourer's wages. They burn them in public, where the ash can be seen and nothing can be quietly kept back. Note what the Sceva affair has done: it did not frighten pagans into believing; it forced believers to stop hedging.\f*
\v 20 So with power the word of the Lord was growing and growing strong.
\s1 Paul Sets His Face Toward Rome
\p
\v 21 Now when these things were filled full, Paul set it in his spirit to go through Macedonia and Achaia and travel to Jerusalem, saying, "After I have been there, I must also see Rome."\f + \fr 19:21 \ft "Must" is the book's word for what God has determined — the same word used of the Messiah's suffering. The whole remainder of the book is the working out of this sentence: the riot here, the journey through Greece, the arrest in Jerusalem, the hearings before governors and a king, the appeal to Caesar, the shipwreck, and a rented room in Rome. He gets there, and he gets there in chains.\f*
\v 22 And sending two of those who served him into Macedonia, Timothy and Erastus, he himself stayed on in Asia for a time.
\s1 The Silversmiths' Riot
\p
\v 23 And about that time there arose no small trouble about the Way.
\v 24 For a certain man named Demetrius, a silversmith, who made silver shrines of Artemis, brought no little business to the craftsmen.\f + \fr 19:24 \ft Miniature models of the temple, sold to pilgrims and used as offerings. The temple of Artemis at Ephesus was one of the seven wonders of the world, and the cult was the city's chief industry as well as its chief glory — it drew the pilgrims, it held the deposits, it ran the festivals. Demetrius is not one man with a stall; he speaks for a trade.\f*
\v 25 He gathered them together, along with the workers in such things, and said, "Men, you know that our prosperity comes from this business \add [i.e. he opens with the money, and only then works round to the goddess]\add*,
\v 26 and you see and hear that not only in Ephesus but in nearly all of Asia this Paul has persuaded and turned away a large crowd, saying that gods made by hands are not gods.\f + \fr 19:26 \ft An enemy's confirmation of what the author said at verse 10: not Ephesus only but nearly the whole province. — And the one line he quotes of Paul's preaching is the prophets' line: "their idols are silver and gold, the work of human hands" (Psalm 115:4); the man who makes a god out of the wood he cooks with (Isaiah 44:9-20).\f*
\v 27 And there is a danger not only that this trade of ours may come into disrepute, but also that the temple of the great goddess Artemis may be counted as nothing, and that she may even be brought down from her greatness — she whom all Asia and the inhabited world worship."\f + \fr 19:27 \ft The Ephesian Artemis is not the huntress of Greek myth but an old Anatolian mother-goddess, whose image was believed to have fallen from the sky (see verse 35). And the order of Demetrius's three worries is worth keeping: our income, then the temple, then the goddess herself.\f*
\v 28 And when they heard this they were filled with anger and were crying out, saying, "Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!"\f + \fr 19:28 \ft The cry is the cult's own acclamation, the shout of its festivals — used here as a mob's chant. Nobody answers Demetrius's argument or proposes anything; they simply start shouting the liturgy.\f*
\v 29 And the city was filled with confusion, and they rushed with one accord into the theater\f + \fr 19:29 \ft Cut into the hillside above the harbour road and seating something like twenty-five thousand. It was where the city held its public assemblies as well as its plays, which is why a crowd with a grievance goes there.\f*, dragging along Gaius and Aristarchus, Macedonians, Paul's traveling companions.
\v 30 And when Paul wanted to go in to the people, the disciples would not let him;
\v 31 and some of the Asiarchs too, who were friendly to him, sent to him and urged him not to venture into the theater.\f + \fr 19:31 \ft The Asiarchs were the wealthiest men of the province, holders of an honorary office tied to the imperial cult and the funding of the games. That some of them were Paul's friends is a detail worth sitting with: the man who says that handmade gods are no gods had cultivated the provincial aristocracy, and they took the trouble to warn him.\f*
\v 32 So some were shouting one thing and some another, for the assembly was in confusion, and most of them did not know why they had come together \add [i.e. two years of daily reasoning on one side, and this on the other]\add*.
\v 33 And some of the crowd put Alexander forward, the Jews pushing him up; and Alexander motioned with his hand, wanting to make a defense to the people \add [i.e. most likely to say that the Jews of the city were not behind this and should not be blamed for it]\add*.
\v 34 But when they recognized that he was a Jew, one voice came from them all, crying out for about two hours, "Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!"\f + \fr 19:34 \ft Whatever distinction Alexander meant to draw, the crowd cannot see it. Jews and followers of the Way both refuse the images, and that is as far as a pagan mob's theology goes. Two hours of one sentence, in a theatre holding twenty-five thousand.\f*
\s1 The Town Clerk
\p
\v 35 But the town clerk\f + \fr 19:35 \ft The chief civic officer of the city, who drafted its decrees and answered for its good order — and the man Rome would question if this got out of hand.\f* quieted the crowd and said, "Men of Ephesus, what man is there who does not know that the city of the Ephesians is the temple keeper of the great Artemis and of the stone that fell from the sky?\f + \fr 19:35 \ft Greek \tl diopetēs\tl*, "fallen from Zeus" — the cult image, believed to have come down from heaven; very possibly a meteorite. "Temple keeper" is an official civic title, and Ephesus put it on its coins. He begins by agreeing with them, because he is about to tell them to go home.\f*
\v 36 So since these things cannot be spoken against, you must be quiet and do nothing rash \add [i.e. if it is beyond dispute, there is nothing to shout about]\add*.
\v 37 For you have brought these men here who are neither temple robbers nor blasphemers of our goddess.\f + \fr 19:37 \ft A verdict, delivered in passing: no chargeable offence. They have not robbed the temple and they have not reviled the goddess in public. Compare Gallio at Corinth (18:14-16) — twice now, a Roman-answerable official has looked at the case and found nothing in it.\f*
\v 38 So if Demetrius and the craftsmen with him have a matter against anyone, court days are held and there are proconsuls; let them bring charges against one another.
\v 39 But if you are after anything more than that, it will be settled in the lawful assembly.\f + \fr 19:39 \ft Which is to say: this is not one. The word he uses for this crowd all through the scene is the same word used for a city's legal assembly and for a congregation of believers — and he has just told them that what they are is a mob.\f*
\v 40 For we are in danger of being charged with riot over today, since there is no cause for it, and about this we will not be able to give an account for this disorderly gathering."\f + \fr 19:40 \ft The last clause is broken in the Greek and has been left rough. — The threat behind it is real: Rome allowed a city to govern itself as long as it kept order, and a riot in a provincial capital could cost Ephesus its privileges. And he cannot write a report explaining a gathering that its own participants cannot explain (verse 32).\f*
\v 41 And when he had said this he dismissed the assembly.

\c 20
\s1 Through Macedonia to Greece
\p
\v 1 And after the uproar stopped, Paul sent for the disciples, and when he had encouraged them he said goodbye and went out to travel to Macedonia.
\v 2 And going through those parts and encouraging them with many words, he came to Greece,
\v 3 and spent three months there.\f + \fr 20:3 \ft Most likely at Corinth, and most likely the months in which the letter to the Romans was written: its writer is in Achaia, about to carry a collection up to Jerusalem, and planning to see Rome afterwards (Romans 15:25-28).\f* And when a plot was made against him by the Jews as he was about to put out for Syria, he decided to go back through Macedonia \add [i.e. rather than be shut up on a pilgrim ship with men who wanted him dead]\add*.
\v 4 And Sopater son of Pyrrhus, a Berean, went with him, and of the Thessalonians Aristarchus and Secundus, and Gaius of Derbe, and Timothy, and of the Asians Tychicus and Trophimus.\f + \fr 20:4 \ft "As far as Asia" stands in brackets in NA28 and is not rendered here. — Look at where these men are from: Berea, Thessalonica, Derbe, Lystra, Asia. They are the churches that gave, and Paul had told the Corinthians that each congregation should accredit its own men to carry the gift to Jerusalem (1 Corinthians 16:3). The collection is never named in this book, but here it is walking down the road.\f*
\v 5 These went ahead and were waiting for us in Troas;\f + \fr 20:5 \ft "Us." The first-person narration, which stopped when Paul left Philippi in chapter 16, resumes at Philippi — which reads as though the writer had stayed there in the years between and has now rejoined the party.\f*
\v 6 but we sailed off from Philippi after the days of unleavened bread, and in five days we came to them in Troas, where we spent seven days.\f + \fr 20:6 \ft He keeps the festival before sailing, as he had kept a vow at Cenchreae (18:18) and as he is hurrying now to keep Pentecost in Jerusalem (verse 16). — Five days for a crossing that took two on the way out (16:11); the prevailing summer winds in the north Aegean blow the wrong way for this direction.\f*
\s1 Eutychus at Troas
\p
\v 7 And on the first day of the week, when we were gathered to break bread\f + \fr 20:7 \ft The day after the Sabbath — the day the women found the tomb open (Luke 24:1), and the day Paul tells the Corinthians to set money aside (1 Corinthians 16:2). The author notes the practice without explaining it. "Breaking bread" is the community's shared meal, the thing they devoted themselves to from the first days (2:42, 46).\f*, Paul was speaking with them, about to leave the next day, and he stretched his word out until midnight.
\v 8 And there were many lamps in the upper room where we were gathered \add [i.e. many lamps, in a crowded room, burning the air]\add*.
\v 9 And a certain young man named Eutychus was sitting in the window, sinking into a deep sleep as Paul reasoned on and on; and overcome by sleep he fell down from the third story and was picked up dead.\f + \fr 20:9 \ft The author writes "dead," not "as though dead." He uses the plain word he uses everywhere else.\f*
\v 10 But Paul went down and fell on him and took him in his arms and said, "Do not make an uproar, for his soul is in him."\f + \fr 20:10 \ft The gesture is Elijah's and Elisha's: both stretched themselves bodily over a dead boy and prayed, and the life came back into him (1 Kings 17:21-22; 2 Kings 4:33-35). Paul's words use the same word for the life that returns.\f*
\v 11 And going back up and breaking the bread and eating, he talked with them a long while, until dawn, and then he left \add [i.e. evening to midnight, then the fall, then the meal, then on till daylight]\add*.
\v 12 And they brought the boy away alive, and were encouraged more than a little.
\s1 Down the Coast to Miletus
\p
\v 13 But we went ahead to the ship and put out for Assos, meaning to take Paul on board there, for he had arranged it that way, meaning himself to go on foot \add [i.e. thirty miles alone, after a night without sleep, and the author does not say why]\add*.
\v 14 And when he met us at Assos we took him on board and came to Mitylene.
\v 15 And sailing from there, on the next day we arrived opposite Chios, and on the day after we crossed over to Samos, and on the following day we came to Miletus \add [i.e. a day's sailing at a time, set down like a ship's log]\add*.
\v 16 For Paul had decided to sail past Ephesus, so that he would not have to spend time in Asia, for he was hurrying to be in Jerusalem, if it were possible for him, on the day of Pentecost.\f + \fr 20:16 \ft Pentecost falls fifty days after Passover, and they left Philippi after the festival week. He cannot walk into Ephesus for a short visit; the community there is too large and knows him too well. So he sends for its leaders instead and meets them thirty miles down the coast.\f*
\s1 Paul's Farewell to the Ephesian Elders
\p
\v 17 And from Miletus he sent to Ephesus and called for the elders of the assembly.
\v 18 And when they came to him he said to them, "You yourselves know, from the first day I set foot in Asia, how I was with you the whole time \add [i.e. he appeals to what they watched, not to what they were told]\add*,
\v 19 serving the Lord as a slave with all humility and tears and with the trials that came upon me by the plots of the Jews;\f + \fr 20:19 \ft "Slave of the Lord" is how the Greek Old Testament names Moses, Joshua, and the prophets, and it is how Paul opens his own letters. He describes two years of the most fruitful work in the book without one word of achievement: slavery, lowliness, tears, and plots.\f*
\v 20 how I held back nothing that was of use, but announced it to you and taught you in public and from house to house\f + \fr 20:20 \ft The verb means to shrink back, to draw in one's sail, to keep something out of sight. He is claiming that nothing was suppressed because it would be unwelcome. And the two settings are the two halves of the Ephesian ministry: the lecture hall (19:9) and the houses.\f*,
\v 21 bearing witness both to Jews and to Greeks of the turning back to God and trust in our Lord Jesus.
\v 22 And now, look — bound in the spirit, I am going to Jerusalem, not knowing what will meet me there\f + \fr 20:22 \ft "Bound" is the ordinary word for being tied or fettered. He is going as a man under constraint, and he is going without being told what is at the other end.\f*,
\v 23 except that the Holy Spirit bears witness to me in city after city, saying that chains and troubles are waiting for me \add [i.e. he knows the kind of thing, and not the shape of it]\add*.
\v 24 But I make my life of no account, as worth nothing to me, so that I may finish my course and the service I received from the Lord Jesus, to bear full witness to the good news of the favor of God.\f + \fr 20:24 \ft "With joy" is added before "finish my course" in the majority text and in most older English versions; NA28 does not have it, and the sentence is compressed accordingly. — "Course" is a runner's track. A runner does not lay out his own; he runs the one marked for him, and the finishing is the whole point.\f*
\s1 Watch Out for the Flock
\p
\v 25 And now, look — I know that all of you among whom I went about proclaiming the kingdom will see my face no more \add [i.e. and everything he says from here is said as a man who will not be back]\add*.
\v 26 So I bear witness to you on this very day that I am clean from the blood of all,
\v 27 for I did not hold back from announcing to you the whole purpose of God.\f + \fr 20:26-27 \ft Ezekiel's watchman again (Ezekiel 33:1-9), as at 18:6, but the scope is now everyone he has spoken to in Asia. The watchman who blows the trumpet is clear of the blood of those who will not listen; the one who keeps silent is not.\f*
\v 28 Watch out for yourselves and for the whole flock, in which the Holy Spirit has set you as overseers\f + \fr 20:28 \ft He summoned "elders" in verse 17 and calls the same men "overseers" here: two words for one set of men, one naming their standing and the other their job. And the appointing is credited to the Spirit, not to Paul and not to a vote.\f*, to shepherd the assembly of God\f + \fr 20:28 \ft NA28 reads "the assembly of God"; other witnesses read "of the Lord." — "Shepherd" is the verb of Ezekiel 34, where God takes the flock away from the shepherds of Israel who fed themselves and says, "I myself will shepherd my sheep."\f*, which he got for himself through the blood of his own.\f + \fr 20:28 \ft The Greek \tl dia tou haimatos tou idiou\tl* is either "through his own blood" or "through the blood of his Own" — that is, of his own one. The rendering here leaves the question standing exactly where the Greek leaves it. What is not in doubt is the verb: he acquired this people at that price, as he acquired Israel for himself out of Egypt.\f*
\v 29 I know that after my leaving fierce wolves will come in among you, not sparing the flock,\f + \fr 20:29 \ft The wolf is what a shepherd exists to stand between. "Beware of false prophets who come to you in sheep's clothing but inwardly are ravening wolves" (Matthew 7:15); the princes of Israel are "wolves tearing the prey" (Ezekiel 22:27).\f*
\v 30 and from among yourselves men will rise up speaking twisted things, to drag away the disciples after them.\f + \fr 20:30 \ft Not from outside — from these men, or men like them. And "twisted" is the word Paul threw at the magician on Cyprus: bending the straight ways of the Lord (13:10). It is not new doctrine invented from nothing; it is the true thing pulled out of shape. Note also what they are after: disciples "after them."\f*
\v 31 So keep awake, remembering that for three years, night and day, I did not stop warning each one of you with tears \add [i.e. each one, by name, one at a time]\add*.
\v 32 And now I hand you over to God and to the word of his favor, which is able to build you up and to give you the inheritance among all who have been made holy.\f + \fr 20:32 \ft He appoints no successor and leaves no office behind him. He hands them to God and to a message — and says the message is what has the power to build. "Inheritance" is the word for a tribe's allotted portion in the land (Joshua 13-21); what God's people are given is now spoken of in those terms.\f*
\p
\v 33 I coveted no one's silver or gold or clothing.\f + \fr 20:33 \ft The verb is the one in the tenth commandment, "you shall not covet." Clothing belongs on the list because good garments were stored wealth (compare Achan's plunder, Joshua 7:21). He says it here, immediately after warning about men who will gather a following of their own, because that is usually what such men are gathering it for.\f*
\v 34 You yourselves know that these hands served my own needs and those of the men with me \add [i.e. and paid for the others as well]\add*.
\v 35 In everything I showed you that by working like this we must help the weak, and remember the words of the Lord Jesus, that he himself said, 'It is more blessed to give than to receive.'"\f + \fr 20:35 \ft This saying is in none of the four Gospels. It is a word of Jesus that the churches remembered and passed on by mouth, and this is the only place it is written down.\f*
\s1 The Parting
\p
\v 36 And when he had said this, he knelt down with them all and prayed.
\v 37 And there was a great weeping from them all, and falling on Paul's neck they kissed him,
\v 38 hurting most of all over the word he had said, that they would see his face no more. And they went with him to the ship \add [i.e. walked with him to the water, to keep him a few minutes longer]\add*.

\c 21
\s1 The Voyage to Tyre
\p
\v 1 And when it happened that we put out to sea, having torn ourselves away from them\f + \fr 21:1 \ft The verb is a strong one — wrenched away, pulled loose. It is the word used of Jesus withdrawing from the disciples in Gethsemane (Luke 22:41). The Miletus farewell is still in the writer's throat.\f*, we ran a straight course to Cos, and on the next day to Rhodes, and from there to Patara.
\v 2 And finding a ship crossing over to Phoenicia, we went aboard and put out \add [i.e. found a cargo vessel and took passage on it]\add*.
\v 3 And when we had come in sight of Cyprus and left it behind on the left, we sailed to Syria and came down to Tyre, for there the ship was to unload its cargo \add [i.e. the stop was the freight's business, not theirs]\add*.
\v 4 And finding the disciples we stayed there seven days; and they kept telling Paul, through the Spirit, not to set foot in Jerusalem.\f + \fr 21:4 \ft Read the sentence carefully. What comes "through the Spirit" is the knowledge — the same thing Paul says the Spirit has been telling him in city after city, that chains are waiting (20:23). What comes from the disciples is the conclusion drawn from it: then do not go. The Spirit shows them the cost; they tell him to avoid it; he has already decided to pay it. There is no command from the Spirit here that Paul disobeys.\f*
\v 5 But when it happened that our days there were finished, we went out and traveled on, and they all escorted us, with wives and children, until we were outside the city; and kneeling down on the beach we prayed
\v 6 and said goodbye to one another, and we went aboard the ship, and they went back to their own homes \add [i.e. the whole congregation, families and all, kneeling in the sand]\add*.
\s1 Agabus at Caesarea
\p
\v 7 And we finished the voyage from Tyre and arrived at Ptolemais, and we greeted the brothers and stayed one day with them.
\v 8 And on the next day we left and came to Caesarea, and going into the house of Philip the announcer of good news, who was one of the seven, we stayed with him.\f + \fr 21:8 \ft The Philip of chapters 6 and 8 — appointed with Stephen to the food distribution, then the preacher of Samaria and the man who baptized the Ethiopian on the Gaza road, who "came to Caesarea" (8:40). That was some twenty-five years earlier in the story, and he is still here.\f*
\v 9 And this man had four unmarried daughters who prophesied.\f + \fr 21:9 \ft "Your sons and your daughters shall prophesy" (Joel 2:28), which Peter quoted on the day the Spirit came (2:17). The author notes them and records nothing they said; the warning that follows comes from a visitor.\f*
\v 10 And while we stayed on for many days, a certain prophet named Agabus came down from Judea\f + \fr 21:10 \ft The same man who came down to Antioch and foretold the famine, which then happened (11:27-28). His record is good.\f*,
\v 11 and coming to us he took Paul's belt, and binding his own feet and hands he said, "This is what the Holy Spirit says: 'The man whose belt this is, the Jews in Jerusalem will bind like this, and hand him over into the hands of the nations.'"\f + \fr 21:11 \ft An acted prophecy, in the manner of the prophets: Isaiah going barefoot for three years (Isaiah 20:3-4), Jeremiah wearing a yoke (Jeremiah 27:2), Ezekiel lying on his side and shaving his head (Ezekiel 4-5), Ahijah tearing a cloak into twelve pieces (1 Kings 11:30-31). And the words are the words of Jesus's own passion predictions: handed over into the hands of the Gentiles (Luke 18:32). — Watch what actually happens at verse 33: it is a Roman officer who binds him, and the Jews never hand him over at all. The trajectory is exactly as foretold; the mechanics are not.\f*
\v 12 And when we heard this, both we and those of that place begged him not to go up to Jerusalem \add [i.e. his own travelling companions now, and the author among them]\add*.
\v 13 Then Paul answered, "What are you doing, weeping and breaking my heart? For I am ready not only to be bound but to die in Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus."\f + \fr 21:13 \ft The verb for what their weeping is doing to him means to crush or shatter. He is not unmoved; he is not going to be moved. And he has gone one step past the prophecy: Agabus said chains, and Paul answers with death.\f*
\v 14 And when he would not be persuaded, we fell silent, saying, "Let the Lord's will be done."\f + \fr 21:14 \ft The words of Gethsemane, from the same writer's first volume: "not my will, but yours, be done" (Luke 22:42). The parallel is deliberate, and it has been building since Jesus "set his face to go to Jerusalem" (Luke 9:51).\f*
\s1 Up to Jerusalem: James and the Vow
\p
\v 15 And after these days we packed up and went up to Jerusalem.
\v 16 And some of the disciples from Caesarea also went with us, bringing us to a certain Mnason of Cyprus, a disciple from the early days, with whom we were to lodge \add [i.e. a Cypriot from the first generation, who could house a party that included Gentiles]\add*.
\v 17 And when we came to Jerusalem the brothers welcomed us gladly.
\v 18 And on the following day Paul went with us to James, and all the elders came \add [i.e. the whole leadership, formally assembled — and note that no apostles are mentioned]\add*.
\v 19 And when he had greeted them he set out one by one the things God had done among the nations through his service.
\v 20 And when they heard it they were glorifying God, and they said to him, "You see, brother, how many tens of thousands there are among the Jews who have trusted, and they are all zealous for the law.\f + \fr 21:20 \ft These are believers, and they keep the Torah — they circumcise their children, keep the Sabbath, worship in the temple. Faith in Jesus as Messiah has not taken them out of Judaism. "Zealous" is a loaded word in Judea: it is what Mattathias called for on his deathbed and what Phinehas was rewarded for (1 Maccabees 2:50; Numbers 25:13). Zeal for the law is not a mild sentiment there.\f*
\v 21 And they have been told about you that you teach all the Jews among the nations to fall away from Moses, telling them not to circumcise their children or to walk by the customs.\f + \fr 21:21 \ft The word for "fall away" is the one the Greek Old Testament uses for defection from the covenant. And the report is a distortion. What Paul has argued is that Gentiles need not become Jews; it has come back to Jerusalem as: Paul tells Jews to stop being Jews. Everything the book has shown of his own practice says otherwise — he circumcised Timothy (16:3) and kept a vow at Cenchreae (18:18).\f*
\v 22 So what is to be done? They will certainly hear that you have come.
\v 23 So do this, which we tell you. We have four men who have a vow on themselves.
\v 24 Take these along and be purified with them, and pay for them to have their heads shaved; and everyone will know that there is nothing in what they were told about you, but that you yourself walk in line, keeping the law.\f + \fr 21:24 \ft A Nazirite vow, ending as Numbers 6:13-20 prescribes: the man brings a burnt offering, a sin offering, and a peace offering, shaves his head at the sanctuary, and burns the hair under the sacrifice. Three animals and the grain and drink offerings with them — a heavy expense, and paying it for someone else was a recognized act of piety. Josephus says Herod Agrippa did it for a number of Nazirites to display his devotion to the law.\f*
\v 25 But concerning the nations who have trusted, we sent word with our judgment that they should keep away from what is sacrificed to idols and from blood and from what is strangled and from sexual immorality."\f + \fr 21:25 \ft "That they observe no such thing, except that" is added before this in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — The clause is a reassurance, not a new ruling: the decision of chapter 15 stands, and what is being asked of Paul concerns the Jewish question only.\f*
\v 26 Then Paul took the men along, and on the next day, being purified with them, he went into the temple, giving notice of when the days of the purification would be filled full — when the offering would be brought for each one of them \add [i.e. he agrees at once, and pays]\add*.
\s1 Seized in the Temple
\p
\v 27 And when the seven days were about to be finished, the Jews from Asia saw him in the temple and threw the whole crowd into confusion, and they laid hands on him\f + \fr 21:27 \ft Not Judeans — men from the province where Paul spent more than two years and where his preaching emptied a trade (19:23-27). They are in Jerusalem for the festival, as he is. His old enemies and he have arrived at the same feast.\f*,
\v 28 crying out, "Men of Israel, help! This is the man who teaches everyone everywhere against the people and the law and this place; and besides, he has even brought Greeks into the temple and has made this holy place common."\f + \fr 21:28 \ft The first three charges are word for word the charges against Stephen in the same city: "this man never stops speaking against this holy place and the law" (6:13). The fourth is the one that kills. The temple was fenced by a low balustrade, and notices in Greek and Latin warned that a foreigner who passed it was answerable with his life; two of those notices have been dug up, and one reads, "Whoever is caught will himself be responsible for his death, which will follow." Josephus says Rome allowed the Jews to carry out that sentence even on a Roman citizen.\f*
\v 29 For they had seen Trophimus the Ephesian in the city with him earlier, and they supposed that Paul had brought him into the temple.\f + \fr 21:29 \ft "Supposed." They saw a Gentile with Paul in the city, and assumed the rest. The author tells the reader the charge is baseless before the violence begins — and the man they are about to lynch for defiling the temple is standing in it paying for other men's sacrifices.\f*
\v 30 And the whole city was set moving, and there was a running together of the people; and seizing Paul they dragged him outside the temple, and at once the doors were shut \add [i.e. shut behind him, to keep the killing off holy ground]\add*.
\v 31 And while they were seeking to kill him, word went up to the commander of the cohort that all Jerusalem was in confusion.\f + \fr 21:31 \ft The Roman garrison was quartered in the Antonia fortress, built on the rock at the north-west corner of the temple platform with stairs running straight down into the outer court — placed there precisely because festivals in this city turned into riots. The commander of a cohort had some six hundred men under him. His name comes out later: Claudius Lysias (23:26).\f*
\v 32 And at once he took soldiers and centurions and ran down to them; and when they saw the commander and the soldiers they stopped beating Paul \add [i.e. only the arrival of armed troops ended it]\add*.
\v 33 Then the commander came up and took hold of him and ordered him bound with two chains, and he asked who he might be and what he had done \add [i.e. arrested first, and the questions after]\add*.
\v 34 But some in the crowd shouted one thing and some another, and since he could not learn anything certain because of the uproar, he ordered him brought into the barracks.\f + \fr 21:34 \ft The same sentence, almost, as at the theatre in Ephesus: "some were shouting one thing and some another" (19:32). Neither mob can say what it wants.\f*
\v 35 And when he came to the steps, it happened that he had to be carried by the soldiers because of the violence of the crowd,
\v 36 for the mass of the people were following, crying out, "Away with him!"\f + \fr 21:36 \ft The same two words the crowd shouted at Pilate about Jesus, in this writer's first volume: "Away with this man" (Luke 23:18).\f*
\s1 Paul Asks Leave to Speak
\p
\v 37 And as he was about to be brought into the barracks, Paul said to the commander, "Is it permitted for me to say something to you?" And he said, "Do you know Greek?
\v 38 Then you are not the Egyptian who before these days stirred up a revolt and led the four thousand men of the Assassins out into the wilderness?"\f + \fr 21:38 \ft Josephus tells the story: an Egyptian who claimed to be a prophet, gathered a crowd, led them to the Mount of Olives promising that the city walls would fall at his word, and escaped when the governor's cavalry cut his followers down. The "Assassins" are the \tl sicarii\tl*, "dagger-men," nationalists who carried short blades under their cloaks and killed collaborators in festival crowds, then joined in the outcry over the body. — The commander's guess tells you what Rome was watching for. He sees a mob and assumes the man at the centre of it is an insurgent.\f*
\v 39 And Paul said, "I am a Jewish man, of Tarsus in Cilicia, a citizen of no unimportant city; and I beg you, let me speak to the people."\f + \fr 21:39 \ft Tarsus was the capital of Cilicia and a serious centre of learning; Strabo rated its schools above those of Athens and Alexandria. — And note what he asks for. Not protection, not release. Leave to address the crowd that has just tried to kill him.\f*
\v 40 And when he gave him leave, Paul stood on the steps and motioned with his hand to the people; and when a great silence had come, he spoke out to them in the Hebrew language, saying:\f + \fr 21:40 \ft "The Hebrew language" is what the Greek says, and it is kept; in the first century the phrase most often meant Aramaic, the spoken tongue of Judea. The same at 22:2 and 26:14. — He has just proved his Greek to the commander and now speaks to the crowd in their own language, and the language alone quiets them further (22:2). Picture where he is standing: on Roman steps, in Roman chains, between Roman soldiers, speaking Aramaic to a Jewish crowd at the door of the temple.\f*

\c 22
\s1 Paul's Defense: A Jew Trained in Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 "Men, brothers and fathers, listen now to my defense to you."\f + \fr 22:1 \ft The same opening words Stephen used before the council (7:2) — spoken by the man who had stood over Stephen's clothes while they killed him, and who is about to say so (verse 20).\f*
\v 2 And when they heard that he was speaking out to them in the Hebrew language, they gave still more quiet. And he said:
\v 3 "I am a Jewish man, born in Tarsus of Cilicia but brought up in this city, trained at the feet of Gamaliel\f + \fr 22:3 \ft The pupil sat on the floor while the teacher sat above him, so "at the feet of" is simply how one says that a man was somebody's student. Gamaliel the Elder was the most respected legal authority of the generation, and this book has already shown him restraining the council from killing the apostles (5:34-39). These are not the credentials of a marginal man.\f* in the strictness of the law of our fathers, being zealous for God just as all of you are today.\f + \fr 22:3 \ft He does not accuse them of fanaticism; he says their zeal is what he had. And zeal is a specific thing in Judea, with a pedigree — Phinehas, who took a spear to a man for the honour of God and was given a covenant of priesthood for it (Numbers 25:6-13), and Mattathias, who called out "everyone zealous for the law, follow me" and started a war (1 Maccabees 2:27). Paul is telling a crowd that has just tried to kill him that he knows the feeling from the inside.\f*
\v 4 And I persecuted this Way to the death, binding and handing over to prisons both men and women,
\v 5 as the high priest also bears me witness, and the whole council of elders; from them I also received letters to the brothers, and I was going to Damascus to bring those who were there back bound to Jerusalem to be punished \add [i.e. ask them — they gave me the papers]\add*.
\s1 On the Road to Damascus
\p
\v 6 And it happened to me, as I was traveling and drawing near to Damascus about midday, that suddenly a great light out of the sky flashed around me\f + \fr 22:6 \ft "About midday" is not in the account at chapter 9, and it is doing work here. At noon nobody mistakes a light for a torch or a lightning flash; before Agrippa he will say it was brighter than the sun (26:13).\f*,
\v 7 and I fell to the ground and heard a voice saying to me, 'Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me?'
\v 8 And I answered, 'Who are you, lord?' And he said to me, 'I am Jesus the Nazarene, whom you are persecuting.'\f + \fr 22:8 \ft "Of Nazareth" is added here that was not added at 9:5. Jesus was a common name; this removes the ambiguity for a Jerusalem crowd. The man who spoke to him from heaven is the Nazarene their own council condemned.\f*
\v 9 And those who were with me saw the light but did not hear the voice of the one speaking to me.\f + \fr 22:9 \ft At 9:7 they hear the voice and see no one; here they see the light and do not hear the voice. The tension is in the Greek and has not been smoothed. The Greek verb for hearing takes different cases and can mean either catching a sound or taking in what is said, which may be all that separates the two sentences — but the two sentences are left as they stand.\f*
\v 10 And I said, 'What shall I do, Lord?' And the Lord said to me, 'Get up and go into Damascus, and there you will be told about everything that has been appointed for you to do.'
\v 11 And since I could not see because of the glory of that light, I was led by the hand by those who were with me and came into Damascus.\f + \fr 22:11 \ft "Glory" is the Greek word for the \tl kavod\tl* — the visible weight of God's presence that made Moses's face unbearable to look at, that filled the tent so that Moses could not enter, that filled the temple so the priests could not stand to serve (Exodus 34:29-35; 40:34-35; 1 Kings 8:10-11). It is a deliberate word to use in front of this audience.\f*
\s1 Ananias
\p
\v 12 And a certain Ananias, a devout man by the law, well spoken of by all the Jews living there\f + \fr 22:12 \ft Chapter 9 introduces this man as "a disciple." Here Paul tells a Jerusalem crowd the other true thing about him: he kept the law, and the Jews of Damascus thought well of him. The man who delivered Paul's commission was no renegade.\f*,
\v 13 came to me, and standing beside me he said to me, 'Saul, brother, look up.' And in that very hour I looked up at him.
\v 14 And he said, 'The God of our fathers\f + \fr 22:14 \ft The name God gave himself at the bush (Exodus 3:6, 15). Whatever happened to Paul, it was not a new god who did it.\f* appointed you beforehand to know his will and to see the Righteous One\f + \fr 22:14 \ft Not "to see Jesus" — "the Righteous One," which is a title out of the prophets (Isaiah 53:11; Jeremiah 23:5), and the title Stephen used of him before this same council: "they killed those who announced beforehand the coming of the Righteous One" (7:52; compare 3:14).\f* and to hear a voice from his mouth,
\v 15 because you will be a witness for him to all men of what you have seen and heard \add [i.e. to all men — and there is the word that will end this speech]\add*.
\v 16 And now why are you waiting? Get up, be immersed and wash away your sins, calling on his name.'\f + \fr 22:16 \ft The Greek has both verbs in the middle voice — "get yourself immersed and get your sins washed away" — which English cannot carry, so they are given as plain imperatives. "Calling on his name" is the phrase from Joel that Peter quoted at Pentecost: "everyone who calls on the name of the Lord shall be rescued" (2:21).\f*
\s1 The Vision in the Temple
\p
\v 17 And it happened, when I had gone back to Jerusalem and was praying in the temple, that I fell into a trance \add [i.e. in the temple — in the very building I am charged with despising]\add*,
\v 18 and saw him saying to me, 'Hurry and go out of Jerusalem quickly, because they will not welcome your witness about me.'\f + \fr 22:18 \ft He is standing on the temple steps telling the people of Jerusalem that the Lord warned him the people of Jerusalem would not receive his testimony — and they are in the middle of proving it.\f*
\v 19 And I said, 'Lord, they themselves know that I was imprisoning and beating in one synagogue after another those who trusted in you,
\v 20 and when the blood of Stephen your witness was poured out, I myself was standing by and giving my approval, and keeping the clothes of those who did away with him.'\f + \fr 22:20 \ft Greek \tl martys\tl*, "witness" — the word that would later come to mean "martyr," and here it is used of the first man killed for the testimony. It is also the word used of Paul's own commission five verses earlier. — His argument to the Lord is one from common sense: nobody in this city can dismiss the testimony of the man who held the coats.\f*
\v 21 And he said to me, 'Go, because I will send you far off to the nations.'"\f + \fr 22:21 \ft One word does it. Everything up to here has been built inside their own categories — the God of our fathers, the Righteous One, the law, the temple, a law-keeping Jew of good repute. And the claim now is that the God of Israel, inside the temple, sent him to the Gentiles. Compare Isaiah 49:6 and Peter's "the promise is for you... and for all who are far off" (2:39).\f*
\s1 The Crowd Erupts, and Paul Claims Rome
\p
\v 22 Now they listened to him up to this word, and then they lifted up their voice, saying, "Away from the earth with such a man! For he ought not to live."\f + \fr 22:22 \ft "Up to this word." They heard a light out of heaven, a voice from the sky, a blinding, a vision in the sanctuary — and none of it stopped them listening. The word that stops them is "nations." What they hear in it is that God has gone past them.\f*
\v 23 And as they were shouting and throwing off their clothes and flinging dust into the air \add [i.e. the gestures of a crowd about to stone somebody]\add*,
\v 24 the commander ordered him brought into the barracks, saying that he should be examined with the lash, so that he might learn why they were shouting against him like this.\f + \fr 22:24 \ft The commander has heard the speech in a language he does not follow and seen the result. Examination under the lash is the standard Roman method of getting information out of a man who is not a citizen — and the \tl flagellum\tl* is not a disciplinary strap but a whip weighted to tear flesh.\f*
\v 25 But when they had stretched him out with the straps, Paul said to the centurion standing there, "Is it permitted for you to lash a man who is a Roman and uncondemned?"\f + \fr 22:25 \ft He waits until the ropes are on. And the question is exact on both counts: Roman law protected a citizen from the scourge, and protected any man from punishment before a verdict. Cicero built a prosecution on it — "to bind a Roman citizen is a crime, to flog him an abomination."\f*
\v 26 And when the centurion heard this he went to the commander and reported it, saying, "What are you about to do? This man is a Roman."
\v 27 And the commander came and said to him, "Tell me, are you a Roman?" And he said, "Yes."
\v 28 And the commander answered, "I bought this citizenship for a great sum." And Paul said, "But I was even born one."\f + \fr 22:28 \ft Under Claudius, citizenship could be had for money through the right official, and a new citizen took the emperor's name — which is presumably how the commander comes to be called Claudius Lysias (23:26). Citizenship from birth outranked citizenship bought. How a Jewish family in Tarsus came by it, the book does not say.\f*
\v 29 So those who were about to examine him at once pulled back from him; and the commander was afraid when he learned that he was a Roman and that he had bound him \add [i.e. he had already broken the law when he chained him at the steps]\add*.
\p
\v 30 And on the next day, wanting to know for certain why he was being accused by the Jews, he released him and ordered the chief priests and the whole council to come together; and bringing Paul down he stood him among them.\f + \fr 22:30 \ft Note what a sentence in a legal claim has done. The speech about the God of their fathers moved the crowd to demand his death; one word about his citizenship has emptied the whipping post, unlocked the chains, and put the Sanhedrin under a Roman officer's summons. From here on Paul's safety runs on Roman procedure, and Roman procedure is what will carry him to Rome.\f*

\c 23
\s1 Before the Council
\p
\v 1 And Paul looked straight at the council and said, "Men, brothers, I have lived my life before God in all good conscience up to this day."\f + \fr 23:1 \ft The verb he uses is the one for conducting oneself as a citizen — how a man has carried himself in public life. He is not claiming to have been right about everything; he is claiming that at every stage, persecutor and apostle alike, he acted on what he believed God required.\f*
\v 2 And the high priest Ananias\f + \fr 23:2 \ft Ananias son of Nedebaeus, high priest from about 47 to 59. Josephus says he sent his servants to the threshing floors to seize the tithes owed to the ordinary priests, and that Jewish insurgents killed him in 66 for siding with Rome.\f* ordered those standing beside him to strike his mouth \add [i.e. before a charge had been read or a word of evidence heard]\add*.
\v 3 Then Paul said to him, "God is going to strike you, you whitewashed wall!\f + \fr 23:3 \ft Ezekiel's image for leaders who build a shoddy wall and then plaster it over: it looks sound, and when the storm comes it falls and buries them (Ezekiel 13:10-16). — And the law he presides over allows a beating only after a verdict and only on the guilty (Deuteronomy 25:1-2). He has broken it in the act of enforcing it.\f* And do you sit judging me by the law, and against the law order me to be struck?"
\v 4 And those standing by said, "Do you revile God's high priest?"
\v 5 And Paul said, "I did not know, brothers, that he is high priest; for it is written, 'You shall not speak evil of a ruler of your people.'"\f + \fr 23:5 \ft Exodus 22:28. He does not withdraw the charge; he accepts the restraint on how he may say it. As for not knowing: the council has been convened at an hour's notice by a Roman officer, the office had changed hands repeatedly in those years, and Paul had been out of the country for most of them.\f*
\s1 Pharisees and Sadducees
\p
\v 6 And knowing that one part was Sadducees and the other Pharisees, Paul cried out in the council, "Men, brothers, I am a Pharisee, a son of Pharisees; it is over hope and the rising of the dead that I am being judged."\f + \fr 23:6 \ft "I am," present tense. He does not say he was one. On resurrection, angels, providence, the authority of the whole of scripture, he stands where the Pharisees stand; what he adds is that the rising has begun in one man. And he is putting the quarrel where he says it belongs — not on table fellowship or on Gentiles, but on whether God raises the dead.\f*
\v 7 And when he said this, there was a standing-off between the Pharisees and the Sadducees, and the mass was split.
\v 8 For the Sadducees say there is no rising, and no angel or spirit; but the Pharisees confess them both.\f + \fr 23:8 \ft The Sadducees held only the five books of Moses as fully authoritative and found no resurrection taught in them; Josephus says they denied the soul's survival and any reward or punishment after death.\f*
\v 9 And there was a great shouting, and some of the scribes of the Pharisees' party stood up and fought it out, saying, "We find nothing wrong in this man. What if a spirit spoke to him, or an angel?"\f + \fr 23:9 \ft "Let us not fight against God" is added at the end of the verse in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28 — though the thought is Gamaliel's, from this same chamber (5:39). — Their own theology will not let them rule the possibility out, and if a spirit did speak to him, opposing him is a serious business.\f*
\v 10 And when the standing-off became great, the commander, afraid that Paul would be torn apart by them, ordered the troops to go down and snatch him from their midst and bring him into the barracks \add [i.e. the court has become the danger he must be rescued from]\add*.
\v 11 And on the following night the Lord stood beside him and said, "Take heart; for as you have borne full witness about me in Jerusalem, so you must also bear witness in Rome."\f + \fr 23:11 \ft The hinge of the whole second half of the book, and it comes in a Roman barrack-room after the Jewish court has failed to produce a charge. Note two things. The Lord calls the testimony in Jerusalem a success, though it ended in a riot and a shouting match. And "must" is the word this book uses of what God has determined — the same word used of the Messiah's own suffering (Luke 24:26). From here on, nothing that happens can prevent Rome; everything that happens will serve it.\f*
\s1 Forty Men Under an Oath
\p
\v 12 And when day came the Jews made a plot and put themselves under a curse, saying they would neither eat nor drink until they had killed Paul.\f + \fr 23:12 \ft The Greek doubles the word: they anathematized themselves with an anathema. Behind it stands the Hebrew \tl cherem\tl*, the ban that devotes a thing irrevocably to destruction (Joshua 6:17-18). They have put themselves under it. If Paul lives, they are the ones devoted.\f*
\v 13 And there were more than forty who made this sworn conspiracy.
\v 14 They went to the chief priests and the elders and said, "We have put ourselves under a curse with a curse, to taste nothing until we have killed Paul.
\v 15 So now you, with the council, make it clear to the commander that he should bring him down to you, as though you were going to look into his case more accurately; and we are ready to do away with him before he gets near."\f + \fr 23:15 \ft The plan requires the court's cooperation, and it requires the court to lie: a request for further examination, used as bait. Men who have sworn this oath in defence of the law are asking the guardians of the law to arrange a murder under cover of a hearing.\f*
\s1 Paul's Nephew
\p
\v 16 But the son of Paul's sister heard of the ambush, and he came and went into the barracks and reported it to Paul.\f + \fr 23:16 \ft The only relative of Paul's who appears anywhere in this book, and he is given no name and no explanation. A citizen in military custody awaiting a hearing could receive visitors; Paul is a detainee, not a condemned man.\f*
\v 17 And Paul called one of the centurions and said, "Bring this young man to the commander, for he has something to report to him." \add [i.e. no prayer for an angel this time — he uses the chain of command]\add*
\v 18 So he took him along and brought him to the commander and said, "The prisoner Paul called me and asked me to bring this young man to you, since he has something to say to you."
\v 19 And the commander took him by the hand and drew him aside privately and asked, "What is it you have to report to me?" \add [i.e. took a frightened boy by the hand, and got him out of earshot]\add*
\v 20 And he said, "The Jews have agreed to ask you to bring Paul down to the council tomorrow, as though they were going to inquire something more accurately about him.
\v 21 So do not be persuaded by them, for more than forty of their men are lying in ambush for him, who have put themselves under a curse neither to eat nor to drink until they have done away with him; and now they are ready, waiting for the promise from you."
\v 22 So the commander sent the young man away, ordering him, "Tell no one that you have made this plain to me."
\s1 Transferred to Caesarea
\p
\v 23 And calling two of the centurions he said, "Get two hundred soldiers ready to travel as far as Caesarea, and seventy horsemen and two hundred spearmen, from the third hour of the night\f + \fr 23:23 \ft Four hundred and seventy men to move one prisoner, and moving him at nine at night. It is out of all proportion to forty conspirators and exactly in proportion to the officer's own exposure: he has a Roman citizen in his custody whom he has already chained unlawfully, and a plot running through the ruling council.\f*,
\v 24 and provide animals to set Paul on and bring him safely through to Felix the governor."\f + \fr 23:24 \ft Antonius Felix, procurator of Judea from about 52 to 59, a freedman who rose to govern a province — of whom Tacitus wrote that he wielded a king's power with a slave's disposition.\f*
\v 25 And he wrote a letter having this form:
\v 26 "Claudius Lysias, to the most excellent governor Felix: greetings.\f + \fr 23:26 \ft The commander's name at last. A man who bought his citizenship under Claudius would take the emperor's name with it, which is what "Claudius" here almost certainly means (compare 22:28).\f*
\v 27 This man was seized by the Jews and was about to be done away with by them, when I came upon them with the troops and rescued him, having learned that he was a Roman.\f + \fr 23:27 \ft The order of events has been quietly improved. He rescued Paul first and found out about the citizenship afterwards, when Paul was already stretched out for the whip (22:25-29). As written, the letter makes the rescue a response to the citizenship, which spares its author an awkward explanation. The reader knows better, and the author lets him notice.\f*
\v 28 And wanting to know the charge for which they were accusing him, I brought him down to their council;
\v 29 and I found him accused about questions of their law, but with no charge worthy of death or chains.\f + \fr 23:29 \ft The first of a run of identical Roman verdicts: Festus will say the same (25:25), and Agrippa the same again (26:31). Compare Pilate on Jesus, three times over: "I find no guilt in this man" (Luke 23:4, 14, 22). — And note "their law." The whole affair has been filed as an internal religious dispute, which is where Gallio filed it too (18:15).\f*
\v 30 And when it was disclosed to me that there would be a plot against the man, I sent him to you at once, ordering his accusers also to speak against him before you."\f + \fr 23:30 \ft "Farewell" at the end of the letter is in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*
\p
\v 31 So the soldiers, according to their orders, took Paul and brought him by night to Antipatris\f + \fr 23:31 \ft Some thirty-five miles out, at the point where the hill country gives way to the coastal plain. The night march covers the ground where an ambush would work; past Antipatris the country is open and forty men on foot can do nothing against cavalry.\f*,
\v 32 and on the next day they let the horsemen go on with him and went back to the barracks.
\v 33 And when they came into Caesarea and handed the letter to the governor, they also presented Paul to him.
\v 34 And when he had read it and asked what province he was from, and learned that he was from Cilicia,\f + \fr 23:34 \ft A question about jurisdiction. A case could be heard where the offence happened or in the defendant's home province; Felix could have sent him to Cilicia and chooses to keep him.\f*
\v 35 he said, "I will hear you out when your accusers arrive too." And he ordered him kept under guard in Herod's praetorium.\f + \fr 23:35 \ft Herod the Great's palace at Caesarea, now the governor's residence and headquarters. Paul is held in the governor's own house, not in a jail — the treatment of a citizen awaiting a hearing, and the next chapter records that his friends were allowed in to look after him (24:23).\f*

\c 24
\s1 Tertullus Presents the Case
\p
\v 1 And after five days the high priest Ananias came down with some elders and a certain lawyer, Tertullus, and they laid information before the governor against Paul.\f + \fr 24:1 \ft The high priest himself has travelled the sixty-five miles to Caesarea, which says what weight the case is being given. And they have hired a professional advocate — a man who knows how a Roman court expects a case to be built and spoken. Authority in a Jewish council counts for nothing in this room.\f*
\v 2 And when he was called, Tertullus began to accuse him, saying, "Since we have obtained much peace through you, and reforms are coming to this nation through your forethought,
\v 3 in every way and everywhere we welcome it, most excellent Felix, with all thankfulness.\f + \fr 24:2-3 \ft The standard flattering opening that every rhetorical school taught, and in this case a fiction. Felix was a freedman who got the province through his brother's influence at court. Tacitus wrote of him that he "wielded a king's power with a slave's disposition, in every kind of cruelty and lust," and Josephus records his years as a rising tide of assassinations, banditry, and punitive expeditions. The "much peace" existed in the speech only.\f*
\v 4 But so as not to hold you up any longer, I beg you in your fairness to hear us briefly.
\v 5 For we have found this man a plague\f + \fr 24:5 \ft A pestilence — the standing insult of Greek political invective for a man whose influence infects the body politic.\f*, and a stirrer of uprisings among all the Jews throughout the inhabited world\f + \fr 24:5 \ft This is the charge that matters in this room. The word is \tl stasis\tl*, sedition — the one thing a provincial governor is appointed to prevent — and the scope is stretched to the whole empire. It is the charge that was laid against Jesus before Pilate: "we found this man misleading our nation" (Luke 23:2).\f*, and a ringleader of the sect of the Nazarenes\f + \fr 24:5 \ft "Sect" is \tl hairesis\tl*, which at this date means a party or school and carries no charge of heresy; Josephus uses it of Pharisees, Sadducees, and Essenes alike, and this book uses it of the Sadducees (5:17) and the Pharisees (15:5). "Nazarenes" is the only place in the book where the followers of Jesus are given that name as a group.\f*,
\v 6 who even tried to make the temple common — and we seized him.\f + \fr 24:6 \ft Note the retreat from the original outcry. In the temple they shouted that he had brought Greeks in and defiled the place (21:28); the professional advocate says he tried, because nobody saw him do it. — The words that follow in most older English versions — "and would have judged him according to our law, but the commander Lysias came and with great violence took him out of our hands, ordering his accusers to come to you" — are not in NA28. Verse 7 is absent from the numbering here, and verse 8 begins "From him."\f*
\v 8 From him you yourself will be able, by examining him, to learn about all these things of which we accuse him."
\v 9 And the Jews joined in the attack, saying that these things were so.
\s1 Paul's Defense
\p
\v 10 And Paul answered, when the governor motioned to him to speak: "Knowing that for many years you have been a judge for this nation, I make my defense in my own case with good cheer\f + \fr 24:10 \ft His compliment, unlike the other man's, is true and it is useful: Felix has governed here long enough to know what an intra-Jewish quarrel looks like and not to be stampeded by a description of it.\f*,
\v 11 since you can learn that it is not more than twelve days since I went up to bow down in Jerusalem \add [i.e. twelve days, for a man charged with rousing the whole empire]\add*.
\v 12 And they did not find me arguing with anyone or making a crowd gather, either in the temple or in the synagogues or throughout the city;
\v 13 nor can they prove to you the things of which they now accuse me \add [i.e. and the men who could speak to it are not in this room]\add*.
\v 14 But this I confess to you, that according to the Way, which they call a sect, so I serve the God of our fathers, trusting everything that is according to the law and written in the prophets\f + \fr 24:14 \ft This is a legal argument as much as a confession. Judaism was a permitted religion under Roman law, with the right to its own customs. If the Way is Israel's God worshiped according to Israel's scriptures, then it stands under that same protection, and a quarrel between Jewish parties is not a governor's business — which is exactly what Gallio had ruled at Corinth (18:15).\f*,
\v 15 having a hope in God, which these men themselves also look for, that there is going to be a rising of both righteous and unrighteous.\f + \fr 24:15 \ft "Of the dead" after "a rising" is added in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — "Which these men themselves also look for": he is pointing at the delegation. The Pharisees among them hold this. And the rising he names is not only the vindication of the good — it is Daniel's, where "many who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life and some to shame" (Daniel 12:2). Everyone is raised, and everyone is assessed. Keep that in mind for verse 25.\f*
\v 16 In this I too train myself always to have a conscience without stumbling toward God and men \add [i.e. because everyone is raised, I train myself to be ready for it]\add*.
\p
\v 17 Now after many years I came to bring acts of mercy to my nation, and offerings;\f + \fr 24:17 \ft The one place in this book where the collection surfaces. Paul's letters are full of it — the gift gathered from the Gentile congregations for the poor of Jerusalem (Romans 15:25-27; 1 Corinthians 16:1-4; 2 Corinthians 8-9). The man accused of turning the world against the Jews came carrying money for them.\f*
\v 18 and while I was doing this they found me purified in the temple, not with a crowd or with an uproar. But some Jews from Asia —\f + \fr 24:18 \ft "Purified." The very charge is that he defiled the place, and what they found him doing there was the rite of purification, paying for other men's offerings.\f*
\v 19 they ought to be here before you and accuse me, if they have anything against me.\f + \fr 24:19 \ft The decisive point of the defense. The men from Asia are the only eyewitnesses to the alleged offence, and they have not come. Roman procedure required an accuser to appear and make his case in person, and an accuser who did not was exposed to a charge of malicious prosecution. Their absence is itself evidence.\f*
\v 20 Or let these men themselves say what wrong they found when I stood before the council,
\v 21 other than this one saying I called out while standing among them: 'It is over the rising of the dead that I am being judged before you today.'"\f + \fr 24:21 \ft He concedes one thing, and it is the one thing that cannot be made into a crime. If the sum of the case is that he shouted a sentence about resurrection in a room where half his accusers believe in resurrection, there is nothing here for a Roman court.\f*
\s1 Felix Adjourns
\p
\v 22 But Felix, knowing the things concerning the Way more accurately\f + \fr 24:22 \ft The author notes it in passing and it explains everything that follows. Felix has been in this province for years, his wife is Jewish, and there has been a congregation in this city since Cornelius. He knows this is not a sedition case. He also knows he is not going to say so.\f*, put them off, saying, "When Lysias the commander comes down, I will decide your case."\f + \fr 24:22 \ft Lysias never comes down, so far as the book records, and the case is never decided. The adjournment is the verdict — an indefinite one.\f*
\v 23 And he ordered the centurion to keep him, and that he should have some freedom, and that none of his own people should be kept from taking care of him \add [i.e. guarded, but not in a cell, and his friends let in]\add*.
\p
\v 24 And after some days Felix arrived with Drusilla his own wife, who was Jewish, and he sent for Paul and listened to him about trust in Christ Jesus.\f + \fr 24:24 \ft Drusilla was the daughter of the Herod Agrippa who was struck down at Caesarea in chapter 12, sister of the Agrippa who will hear Paul in chapter 26. She was about twenty. Josephus says Felix used a magician to persuade her to leave her husband, the king of Emesa, and marry him — a Gentile — against her own law. Hold that beside the three subjects of Paul's next sentence.\f*
\v 25 And as he was reasoning about righteousness and self-control and the judgment to come, Felix became frightened and answered, "For now go away; when I get a chance I will send for you" —\f + \fr 24:25 \ft Three subjects, and every one of them lands. Righteousness, to a governor whose administration was a byword for cruelty. Self-control, to a man sitting beside another man's wife. And a judgment to come, to a judge. The word for his reaction is fear — not disagreement. He does not answer the argument; he ends the conversation. And "when I get a chance" is the same deferral he used in verse 22, which is his whole character in the chapter: a man who understands enough to be afraid and never enough to move.\f*
\v 26 hoping at the same time that money would be given him by Paul; so he sent for him even more often and talked with him \add [i.e. sending for the man who preached the judgment to come, and waiting for a bribe]\add*.
\v 27 But when two years were filled full, Felix received Porcius Festus as his successor; and wanting to do the Jews a favor, Felix left Paul in chains.\f + \fr 24:27 \ft Two years of a Roman citizen held without a verdict by a governor who knew there was no case. And the reason given for leaving him bound is not evidence but a parting favour. Felix was in fact recalled and accused by the Jews of Caesarea before Nero, and escaped only through his brother's influence at court.\f*

\c 25
\s1 Festus Refuses to Move the Trial
\p
\v 1 So Festus, having come into the province, went up to Jerusalem from Caesarea after three days \add [i.e. within three days of taking office — he knows where the province is really governed from]\add*,
\v 2 and the chief priests and the leading men of the Jews laid information before him against Paul; and they were urging him,
\v 3 asking a favor against him, that he would send for him to Jerusalem — making an ambush to do away with him on the road.\f + \fr 25:3 \ft Two years have passed and the plan has not changed. It is the same request and the same ambush as in chapter 23, in the same words: get him moved, and take him on the road. Festus does not know this. The reader does, which is why the reader can see what Paul is refusing when he refuses the same offer at verse 9.\f*
\v 4 But Festus answered that Paul was being kept at Caesarea, and that he himself was about to go there shortly.
\v 5 "So," he said, "let the powerful men among you come down with me, and if there is anything out of place about the man, let them accuse him."
\s1 I Appeal to Caesar
\p
\v 6 And after spending not more than eight or ten days among them\f + \fr 25:6 \ft NA28 reads "not more than eight or ten days"; the majority text, followed by most older English versions, has "more than ten days."\f*, he went down to Caesarea; and on the next day he sat on the judgment seat and ordered Paul to be brought.
\v 7 And when he arrived, the Jews who had come down from Jerusalem stood around him, bringing many heavy charges which they could not prove \add [i.e. many and grave, and not one of them evidenced]\add*,
\v 8 while Paul made his defense: "Neither against the law of the Jews nor against the temple nor against Caesar have I sinned in anything."\f + \fr 25:8 \ft Three denials for three charges. The first two have been running since the temple riot. The third is new in Paul's mouth, which suggests the prosecution has sharpened the political edge of the case for the benefit of a new governor: a man who stirs up Jews across the empire is Caesar's problem.\f*
\v 9 But Festus, wanting to do the Jews a favor, answered Paul, "Are you willing to go up to Jerusalem and be judged there before me about these things?"\f + \fr 25:9 \ft Word for word the phrase used of Felix at 24:27. Two governors in succession, both satisfied there is no crime, both willing to trade the case for goodwill. The whole administration of this province runs on favours, and Paul is the coin being offered.\f*
\v 10 But Paul said, "I am standing before Caesar's judgment seat, where I ought to be judged. I have done no wrong to the Jews, as you also know very well \add [i.e. you know it, and your offer is not about the evidence]\add*.
\v 11 So if I am in the wrong and have done anything worthy of death, I do not beg off from dying; but if there is nothing in what these men accuse me of, no one can hand me over to them as a favor. I appeal to Caesar."\f + \fr 25:11 \ft He grants the principle first: if there is a capital charge, let there be a capital penalty. His objection is not to punishment. Then he turns the governor's own word back on him — the verb "hand over as a favour" is the same root as the "favour" Festus is trying to do. A man is not a gift to be given. — The appeal itself is the old right of a Roman citizen to have his case taken out of a provincial governor's hands and put before the emperor, and once it is spoken the governor has no discretion left.\f*
\v 12 Then Festus, after talking with the council, answered, "You have appealed to Caesar; to Caesar you will go."\f + \fr 25:12 \ft The council is the governor's board of assessors, whom he was expected to consult. The answer is a formula, and it is final. — "To Caesar you will go" is the sentence in which the word given in the barracks two years earlier — "you must also bear witness in Rome" (23:11) — becomes a matter of Roman law.\f*
\s1 Festus Lays the Case Before Agrippa
\p
\v 13 And when some days had passed, Agrippa the king and Bernice came down to Caesarea, greeting Festus.\f + \fr 25:13 \ft Herod Agrippa II, son of the Agrippa struck down at Caesarea in chapter 12 and great-grandson of Herod the Great. He ruled territories to the north-east, not Judea — but Rome had given him charge of the Jerusalem temple and the right to appoint the high priest, which is why his opinion is worth having here. Bernice is his sister, widowed young; ancient writers from Josephus to Juvenal repeated the rumour about the two of them, and the author simply names them together and leaves it.\f*
\v 14 And since they spent many days there, Festus laid Paul's case before the king, saying, "There is a certain man left behind by Felix as a prisoner,
\v 15 about whom, when I was in Jerusalem, the chief priests and the elders of the Jews laid information, asking for a judgment against him \add [i.e. they did not ask for a hearing; they asked for a verdict]\add*.
\v 16 To them I answered that it is not the Roman custom to hand over any man as a favor before the accused has his accusers face to face and gets a chance to make his defense about the charge.\f + \fr 25:16 \ft "To destruction" after "hand over any man" is added in the majority text and most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — Two genuine principles of Roman law: the accused must face his accusers, and he must be given room to answer. What Festus leaves out of his account to the king is his own offer, seven verses ago, to move the trial to Jerusalem.\f*
\v 17 So when they came together here, I made no delay; on the next day I sat on the judgment seat and ordered the man to be brought.
\v 18 And the accusers who stood up brought no charge about him of the wicked things I was supposing;\f + \fr 25:18 \ft He had expected something criminal, given the energy behind the prosecution. He got something else, and the next sentence is his attempt to describe it.\f*
\v 19 they had certain questions with him about their own religion, and about a certain Jesus who had died, whom Paul kept saying was alive.\f + \fr 25:19 \ft The whole affair, as it looks to a Roman who has no stake in it: a dead man, and one prisoner who keeps insisting he is not dead. Strip away the charges about the law, the temple, and Caesar, and this is what is left — which is exactly what Paul has been saying all along (23:6; 24:21). Festus cannot see why it matters. He has, without meaning to, put his finger on the only thing that does.\f*
\v 20 And being at a loss how to look into these things, I asked whether he wanted to go to Jerusalem and be judged there about them.
\v 21 But when Paul appealed to be kept for the decision of the Augustus\f + \fr 25:21 \ft \tl Sebastos\tl* — the Greek for the Latin \tl Augustus\tl*, the emperor's title, and at this date the emperor is Nero. The same at verse 25.\f*, I ordered him kept until I could send him up to Caesar."
\v 22 And Agrippa said to Festus, "I myself would like to hear the man." "Tomorrow," he said, "you will hear him."\f + \fr 25:22 \ft His great-uncle Antipas had wanted the same thing about Jesus — "he had long been wanting to see him" (Luke 23:8) — and found nothing in him either (Luke 23:15). The family keeps being brought to look at this, generation after generation.\f*
\s1 The Hearing Convened
\p
\v 23 So on the next day Agrippa and Bernice came with great show\f + \fr 25:23 \ft The Greek word occurs nowhere else in the New Testament, and it means a display, something made to appear — the root of "phantom." Note what the author is arranging in one sentence: a king, a queen, a governor, the military commanders, the leading men of the city, in full state; and a man in chains, led in.\f* and went into the audience hall with the commanders and the leading men of the city; and at Festus's order Paul was brought in.
\v 24 And Festus said, "King Agrippa, and all you men here with us, you see this man about whom the whole crowd of the Jews appealed to me, both in Jerusalem and here, shouting that he ought not to live any longer.
\v 25 But I grasped that he had done nothing worthy of death; and since he himself appealed to the Augustus, I decided to send him.\f + \fr 25:25 \ft The third Roman official to say it: Lysias in writing (23:29), Felix by his refusal to convict, and now Festus in front of a king. Agrippa will make it four (26:31).\f*
\v 26 About him I have nothing certain to write to my lord\f + \fr 25:26 \ft A prisoner sent up to the emperor had to be accompanied by a written statement of the charges, and Festus cannot draft one. There is no Roman statute that covers a dispute about whether a dead man is alive. — "My lord" for the emperor was not yet the fixed form of address it later became; Augustus and Tiberius are said to have refused it.\f*; so I have brought him before you, and especially before you, King Agrippa, so that after the examination I may have something to write \add [i.e. the king is here because he understands Jewish questions, and the governor does not]\add*.
\v 27 For it seems unreasonable to me to send a prisoner and not to signal the charges against him."\f + \fr 25:27 \ft The Greek word is "without reason" — irrational, absurd. He has a prisoner the Jewish leadership wants dead, whom he has cleared of any capital offence, who has appealed to the emperor, and whose case he cannot state in a sentence. He is right that it is absurd, and he cannot see that the absurdity is his own doing.\f*

\c 26
\s1 Paul Before Agrippa
\p
\v 1 And Agrippa said to Paul, "It is permitted for you to speak for yourself." Then Paul stretched out his hand and began his defense:\f + \fr 26:1 \ft The king gives the leave, not the governor, which tells you whose hearing this really is. And the extended hand is the orator's opening gesture — a man about to deliver a set speech, not a prisoner stammering an excuse.\f*
\v 2 "About all the things of which I am accused by Jews, King Agrippa, I count myself blessed that I am about to make my defense before you today,
\v 3 especially since you are an expert in all the customs and questions among the Jews. So I beg you to hear me patiently.\f + \fr 26:3 \ft Unlike Tertullus's flattery of Felix, this compliment is true and it is to the point. Rome had given Agrippa charge of the temple and the appointment of the high priest. He is the one man in the room who can weigh what Paul is about to say instead of reducing it, as Festus did, to a dead man somebody says is alive.\f*
\v 4 So then, all the Jews know my way of life from my youth, which was from the beginning among my own nation and in Jerusalem.
\v 5 They have known me for a long time, if they are willing to bear witness, that I lived as a Pharisee, by the strictest party of our religion \add [i.e. and they can say so, if they choose to]\add*.
\v 6 And now I stand being judged over the hope of the promise made by God to our fathers,
\v 7 which our twelve tribes hope to reach as they serve him earnestly night and day. It is over this hope, King, that I am accused by Jews.\f + \fr 26:7 \ft "Our twelve tribes" — not the two that were left after the exile but the whole nation, including the ten that were lost, whose regathering the prophets promised (Ezekiel 37:15-28; Isaiah 11:11-12). He is claiming that what he is on trial for is not the private conviction of a party but the hope the entire people serves God night and day to see. And Jews are prosecuting him for it.\f*
\v 8 Why is it judged unbelievable among you if God raises the dead?\f + \fr 26:8 \ft The question falls differently on the two halves of the room. To a Jew it is nearly self-answering: the God who raised the widow's son by Elijah and the Shunammite's by Elisha is blessed in the daily prayer as the one who revives the dead. To a Greek it is the same absurdity that made the Athenians laugh (17:32). Paul asks it and moves on.\f*
\s1 What I Did Against the Name
\p
\v 9 So I myself supposed I had to do many things against the name of Jesus the Nazarene \add [i.e. I too once had a sense of what God required, and it was this]\add*,
\v 10 which I also did in Jerusalem; and I shut up many of the holy ones in prisons, having received the authority from the chief priests; and when they were being done away with I cast my pebble against them.\f + \fr 26:10 \ft The Greek is "I brought down a pebble against them" — the voting stone dropped into an urn to convict. The image is kept. — Two things follow from this verse: there were executions besides Stephen's, and Paul is saying in a court that he voted for them.\f*
\v 11 And often, in all the synagogues, I punished them and tried to force them to blaspheme; and being madly enraged at them, I persecuted them even to cities outside.\f + \fr 26:11 \ft "Tried to force them to blaspheme" — the tense says he kept at it, and it does not say he always failed. This is not simply arresting people; it is trying to break them into denying their Lord. — And note the word for his rage: it is the root of "mania." Keep it in mind for verse 24, where Festus uses it of him.\f*
\s1 The Road, and the Commission
\p
\v 12 In this business I was going to Damascus with authority and a commission from the chief priests,
\v 13 when at midday on the road, King, I saw a light out of the sky brighter than the sun shining around me and those traveling with me \add [i.e. at noon, and brighter than noon]\add*.
\v 14 And when we had all fallen to the ground, I heard a voice saying to me in the Hebrew language, 'Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me? It is hard for you to kick against the goads.'\f + \fr 26:14 \ft Two details here that the earlier tellings do not have. The voice spoke in Hebrew — which in that century usually means Aramaic — which authenticates the encounter for a Jewish king: this is Israel's God, speaking Israel's language. And the proverb: a goad is the sharpened stick that drives an ox, and an ox that kicks back only drives the point in further. The saying was current in Greek — Euripides has it, and Aeschylus — so the Romans in the room would catch it too. It says the resistance has been futile and self-wounding all along. — "It is hard for you to kick against the goads" is not in NA28 at 9:5, where most older English versions place it; here it is.\f*
\v 15 And I said, 'Who are you, Lord?' And the Lord said, 'I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting.
\v 16 But get up and stand on your feet\f + \fr 26:16 \ft Ezekiel's commission, word for word: "Son of man, stand on your feet, and I will speak with you" (Ezekiel 2:1). Paul is describing a prophet's call, in a prophet's language, to a man who will recognize it.\f*; for this is why I appeared to you: to appoint you a servant and a witness both of what you have seen of me and of what I will show you,
\v 17 taking you out from the people and from the nations, to whom I am sending you
\v 18 to open their eyes, to turn them from darkness to light and from the authority of Satan to God, so that they may receive forgiveness of sins and a share among those who have been made holy by trust in me.'\f + \fr 26:18 \ft Nearly every phrase is out of Isaiah's servant songs: "to open blind eyes" (Isaiah 42:7), turning darkness into light before them (42:16), the release proclaimed to captives (61:1, the passage Jesus read at Nazareth). And "a share" is the word for a tribe's allotted portion in the land (Joshua 13-21) — what Israel was given. The commission is not to build something alongside Israel but to open Israel's inheritance to whoever comes by trust. Paul is telling a Jewish king that his Gentile mission is the servant's own job description.\f*
\s1 I Was Not Disobedient
\p
\v 19 So then, King Agrippa, I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision,
\v 20 but I announced first to those in Damascus and in Jerusalem, and through the whole country of Judea, and to the nations, that they should turn back and turn to God, doing works worthy of the turning back.\f + \fr 26:20 \ft The last clause is John the Baptist's demand word for word — "bear fruits worthy of repentance" (Luke 3:8). A turning that leaves the life unchanged is not the thing being asked for, and Paul preaches it to Gentiles in the same terms John preached it at the Jordan.\f*
\v 21 On account of these things Jews seized me in the temple and tried to lay hands on me \add [i.e. for preaching that, and for preaching it to Gentiles]\add*.
\v 22 So having obtained help from God up to this day, I stand bearing witness to both small and great, saying nothing beyond what the prophets and Moses said was going to happen:\f + \fr 26:22 \ft The whole forensic case in one clause: nothing beyond. He is not charged with inventing a religion, because he has invented nothing. He has read the books his accusers read and said they have come true.\f*
\v 23 that the Christ was to suffer, that he was to be first out of the rising of the dead, and that he was to announce light both to the people and to the nations."\f + \fr 26:23 \ft Three claims, each one a reading of a text. That the Messiah suffers — which was not the common expectation, and which Isaiah 53 and Psalm 22 make room for. That he is "first out of the rising" — not the first man ever restored to life, but the first of the resurrection at the end, its opening instalment. And that light goes to the people and to the nations — Isaiah 49:6 again, the verse that has been under this whole book since 1:8.\f*
\s1 You Are Out of Your Mind
\p
\v 24 And as he was saying these things in his defense, Festus said with a loud voice, "You are out of your mind, Paul! Your great learning is turning you to madness."\f + \fr 26:24 \ft He shouts, and he shouts at the exact point where the argument arrives at a dead man rising. The word he uses is the one Paul used of himself at verse 11, when he was hunting believers down. Paul was mad once; the governor thinks he is mad now. Only one of those two states was actually against God.\f*
\v 25 But Paul said, "I am not out of my mind, most excellent Festus, but I am speaking out words of truth and good sense.\f + \fr 26:25 \ft "Good sense" is \tl sōphrosynē\tl*, one of the four cardinal virtues of Greek moral philosophy and the precise opposite of the madness Festus has just alleged. Paul answers the charge in the vocabulary the man who made it was educated in.\f*
\v 26 For the king knows about these things, and I speak to him with boldness; for I am not persuaded that any of this has escaped him, since this was not done in a corner.\f + \fr 26:26 \ft A Roman governor was executed at Jerusalem; a movement has spread through every province; this hearing is itself a public event. Whatever else may be said of these claims, they are not the secrets of a mystery cult. They can be checked.\f*
\v 27 Do you trust the prophets, King Agrippa? I know you trust them."\f + \fr 26:27 \ft The question has no safe answer. Say yes, and Paul has just spent twenty verses showing what the prophets said. Say no, in front of a Roman governor and the leading men of Caesarea, and the custodian of the temple has disowned his own credentials. Paul does not wait for the answer; he supplies it.\f*
\v 28 And Agrippa said to Paul, "In a little you are persuading me to make a Christian."\f + \fr 26:28 \ft The Greek holds several things at once and will not be pinned to one. "In a little" can be in a little time or with little effort; "to make a Christian" can be to make me one or to play the part of one. It is rendered here word for word rather than resolved. Whether the king is nearly persuaded, or deflecting with a joke, or acknowledging a good speech and no more, the sentence does not say — and a man in his position had every reason to keep it that way. — "Christian" is the outsiders' word for the movement, coined at Antioch (11:26).\f*
\v 29 And Paul said, "I would pray to God that, whether in a little or in much, not only you but all who hear me today might become such as I am — except for these chains."\f + \fr 26:29 \ft He takes the ambiguous sentence at its best and answers it straight. And look at what the prisoner is claiming in a room got up in "great show" (25:23): that apart from the irons on his wrists, his condition is the one he would wish on the king.\f*
\s1 The Verdict
\p
\v 30 And the king got up, and the governor and Bernice and those sitting with them,\f + \fr 26:30 \ft "And when he had said these things" at the start of the verse is in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28.\f*
\v 31 and going aside they were talking with one another, saying, "This man is doing nothing worthy of death or chains."\f + \fr 26:31 \ft The fourth such finding, and this time it is unanimous and it includes a Jewish king: Lysias in his letter (23:29), Felix by never convicting, Festus in front of this company (25:25), and now the whole room. Compare Pilate on Jesus, three times over, and Antipas after him (Luke 23:4, 14, 15, 22). The verdict is always the same and it never sets anybody free.\f*
\v 32 And Agrippa said to Festus, "This man could have been released if he had not appealed to Caesar."\f + \fr 26:32 \ft The closing irony of the whole legal sequence. The appeal saved his life from the Jerusalem road and the ambush waiting on it; the appeal is now the one thing that keeps him in chains. And it is what puts him on a ship to Rome, which is where he was told two years ago he was going (23:11).\f*

\c 27
\s1 The Voyage to Italy
\p
\v 1 And when it was decided that we should sail off to Italy\f + \fr 27:1 \ft "We." The first-person narration returns for the last time and runs from here to the end of the book (compare 16:10; 20:5; 21:1). What follows is the most detailed sea narrative anywhere in ancient literature outside a sailing manual, and it reads like the memory of a man who was on the ship.\f*, they handed Paul and some other prisoners over to a centurion of the Augustan cohort named Julius\f + \fr 27:1 \ft Centurions come off well throughout this writer's two volumes — the one at Capernaum, the one at the cross, Cornelius, the one who stopped the flogging. Julius will keep the pattern: he lets Paul visit friends, and at the end he saves his life.\f*.
\v 2 And going aboard a ship of Adramyttium that was about to sail to the places along Asia, we put out, with Aristarchus, a Macedonian of Thessalonica, with us \add [i.e. a coasting vessel, to be changed for a westbound ship further on]\add*.
\v 3 And on the next day we put in at Sidon, and Julius treated Paul kindly and let him go to his friends to be cared for.
\v 4 And putting out from there we sailed under the lee of Cyprus, because the winds were against us,\f + \fr 27:4 \ft That is, up the eastern and northern side of the island, using the land as a shield. In late summer the prevailing wind in this sea comes out of the west, and a square-rigged ship cannot sail into it.\f*
\v 5 and sailing across the open sea off Cilicia and Pamphylia we came down to Myra in Lycia.
\v 6 And there the centurion found an Alexandrian ship sailing for Italy, and he put us on board it.\f + \fr 27:6 \ft A grain ship. Rome was fed from Egypt, and the corn fleet ran from Alexandria to Puteoli; these were among the largest vessels afloat, carrying hundreds of tons and, as verse 37 shows, hundreds of people. When the westerlies blew, such ships could not cross direct and worked north-east to Asia and then west along the coast, which is why one is lying at Myra.\f*
\v 7 And sailing slowly for many days, and barely coming off Cnidus, since the wind would not let us go on, we sailed under the lee of Crete off Salmone,
\v 8 and coasting along it with difficulty we came to a certain place called Fair Havens, near which was the city of Lasea.\f + \fr 27:8 \ft A small bay on the south coast of Crete, and a town otherwise unknown to literature. A writer inventing a shipwreck reaches for famous harbours; a man who was there names the anchorage he actually sat in.\f*
\s1 Paul's Warning Ignored
\p
\v 9 And since a good deal of time had gone by, and the voyage was already dangerous because even the Fast had already gone by, Paul warned them,\f + \fr 27:9 \ft "The Fast" is the Day of Atonement, which falls in late September or early October. Roman practice reckoned sailing safe until mid-September, dangerous from then until 11 November, and closed for the winter after that. They are already into the dangerous season and running toward the closed one.\f*
\v 10 saying to them, "Men, I see that the voyage is going to be with damage and much loss, not only of the cargo and the ship but of our lives as well."\f + \fr 27:10 \ft This is Paul's own judgment, not a revelation — he says "I see," and he has been shipwrecked three times already (2 Corinthians 11:25). Note that it turns out to be half wrong: the ship is lost and no life is. The correction comes at verse 24, and the author lets the difference between a man's assessment and a word from God stand where it falls.\f*
\v 11 But the centurion was persuaded by the steersman and the shipowner rather than by what Paul said \add [i.e. by the two men who knew ships, against a prisoner who made tents]\add*.
\v 12 And since the harbor was unsuitable for wintering, the majority set their counsel to put out from there, if somehow they might reach Phoenix, a harbor of Crete facing southwest and northwest, and winter there.\f + \fr 27:12 \ft Forty miles along the coast to a harbour that turns its back on the winter northerlies. On a decent day it is one afternoon's sail. The reasoning is sound; it only requires the weather to hold.\f*
\s1 The Northeaster
\p
\v 13 And when a south wind blew gently, supposing they had gained their purpose, they weighed anchor and coasted along Crete close in \add [i.e. exactly the wind they wanted, and they took it]\add*.
\v 14 But not long after, a wind like a typhoon, called the Northeaster, rushed down from it;\f + \fr 27:14 \ft "From it" — off the island. Crete's mountains rise over seven thousand feet, and cold air spilling over them comes down the southern slopes in violent squalls. The sailors' name for it welds a Greek word and a Latin one: \tl Euros\tl*, the east wind, and \tl Aquilo\tl*, the north. It catches them between harbours, close inshore, with nowhere to run.\f*
\v 15 and when the ship was caught and could not face the wind, we gave way and were carried along \add [i.e. the ship is no longer being sailed; it is being taken]\add*.
\v 16 And running under the lee of a certain small island called Cauda\f + \fr 27:16 \ft NA28 reads "Cauda"; many English versions have "Clauda." It is modern Gavdos, some twenty-three miles south of Crete.\f*, we were barely able to get control of the ship's boat.
\v 17 And when they had hoisted it up, they used supports to undergird the ship\f + \fr 27:17 \ft The Greek says only "they used helps" and does not say what was done. The standard emergency measure was frapping — passing cables under the hull and hauling them tight to hold the planking together, which tells you the seams were already working. — Note what the ship's boat is for: it is the only means of getting off the vessel, and it comes back at verse 30.\f*; and fearing they would run aground on the Syrtis, they lowered the gear, and so were carried along.\f + \fr 27:17 \ft The Syrtis is the great shoal off the North African coast, a byword in antiquity for shifting sandbanks that swallowed ships. Driven south-west by a north-easter, that is where they are headed, and the gear they lower — a drag of some kind — is an attempt to slow the drift.\f*
\v 18 And as we were being violently storm-tossed, on the next day they began throwing the cargo overboard;\f + \fr 27:18 \ft The wheat that Rome is waiting for. Jonah's sailors did the same in their storm (Jonah 1:5) — but their storm stopped, and this one does not.\f*
\v 19 and on the third day they threw off the ship's tackle with their own hands \add [i.e. everyone aboard now, not the crew alone]\add*.
\v 20 And when neither sun nor stars appeared for many days, and no small storm pressed on us, in the end all hope of our being rescued was being taken away.\f + \fr 27:20 \ft Without sun or stars there is no navigation at all: they do not know where they are, which way they are going, or how near the land is. And the verb for hope being "taken away" is the same one used of stripping the ship — as the vessel is stripped, so is the hope. Everything human has now been exhausted, which is the point at which the next paragraph begins.\f*
\s1 An Angel Stood Beside Me
\p
\v 21 And when they had gone a long time without food, Paul then stood up in their midst and said, "Men, you should have listened to me and not put out from Crete, and gained this damage and loss.\f + \fr 27:21 \ft Not said to score a point. He is establishing why they should listen to him now: the last time the professionals were sure and he was not, he was right.\f*
\v 22 And now I urge you to take heart, for there will be no loss of life among you, but only of the ship.
\v 23 For this night there stood beside me an angel of the God whose I am and whom I serve\f + \fr 27:23 \ft He is speaking to pagan sailors and Roman soldiers, and so he says which God he means: the one I belong to and the one I serve.\f*,
\v 24 saying, 'Do not be afraid, Paul; you must stand before Caesar, and look — God has granted you all those sailing with you.'\f + \fr 27:24 \ft "You must" is the word of divine necessity, and it is the same word the Lord used in the barracks: "you must also bear witness in Rome" (23:11). Nothing has changed except the circumstances. — And "granted" is the same verb that ran all through chapter 25, where governors kept proposing to hand Paul over to his accusers as a favour. What men traded as a favour, God gives as one: two hundred and seventy-six lives, given to Paul.\f*
\v 25 So take heart, men, for I trust God, that it will be just as it was told me.
\v 26 But we must run aground on a certain island."\f + \fr 27:26 \ft He knows there will be an island and he does not know which. And the running aground is not the failure of the promise; it is how the promise is kept. The storm is not stilled, the ship is not saved, and everyone lives.\f*
\s1 Soundings in the Night
\p
\v 27 And when the fourteenth night came, as we were being carried across the Adriatic\f + \fr 27:27 \ft In first-century usage the name covered the whole open sea between Crete, Sicily, and Malta, not only the gulf that bears it now. Fourteen days of drift at the rate this wind gives works out at about the distance from Crete to Malta.\f*, about midnight the sailors began to suspect that some land was drawing near to them \add [i.e. in pitch dark, most likely hearing surf]\add*.
\v 28 And taking a sounding they found twenty fathoms; and going a little farther and taking another sounding, they found fifteen fathoms \add [i.e. a hundred and twenty feet, then ninety — the bottom is coming up]\add*.
\v 29 And fearing that we might run aground somewhere on rough places, they threw four anchors from the stern and prayed for day to come.\f + \fr 27:29 \ft Anchoring by the stern rather than the bow is unusual and deliberate: it holds the bow pointed at the shore, so that when light comes they can cut the cables and run her straight up the beach — which is what they try at verse 40.\f*
\v 30 And when the sailors were trying to escape from the ship and had lowered the boat into the sea, on the pretext of being about to lay out anchors from the bow,\f + \fr 27:30 \ft Bow anchors would be a sensible thing to lay, which is what makes it a good lie. What they are actually doing is leaving — and taking with them the only men aboard who know how to put a ship on a beach.\f*
\v 31 Paul said to the centurion and the soldiers, "Unless these men stay in the ship, you cannot be rescued."\f + \fr 27:31 \ft Set this beside verse 24. God has granted him every life on board, and here he is saying that unless certain men stay at their posts nobody will be saved. The promise is certain and it is not a substitute for the sailors' hands. This time the centurion listens to him.\f*
\v 32 Then the soldiers cut away the ropes of the boat and let it fall away \add [i.e. and with it the last way off the ship for anybody]\add*.
\s1 Paul Breaks Bread
\p
\v 33 And until day was about to come, Paul was urging them all to take food, saying, "Today is the fourteenth day you have been waiting and going on without food, having taken nothing.
\v 34 So I urge you to take food, for this is for your rescue; for not a hair from the head of any of you will be lost."\f + \fr 27:34 \ft An old expression for total preservation — "not one hair of his head shall fall to the ground" (1 Samuel 14:45; 2 Samuel 14:11; 1 Kings 1:52) — and Jesus used it of his own (Luke 21:18). — Note also what "rescue" means in this sentence: eat, because you will need the strength to get through the surf. The salvation of these men runs through a meal.\f*
\v 35 And having said this, he took bread and gave thanks to God in front of them all, and breaking it he began to eat.\f + \fr 27:35 \ft Took, gave thanks, broke — the sequence this writer uses of Jesus at the feeding of the five thousand, at the last supper, and at Emmaus (Luke 9:16; 22:19; 24:30). There is no cup here and no word of institution, and the company is pagan sailors, soldiers, and prisoners. What the wording does is set the act where it belongs: a man giving thanks to God in front of everyone, in the dark, before the ship breaks up, for a promise not yet kept.\f*
\v 36 And they all took heart and took food themselves as well \add [i.e. the prisoner has become the one feeding the ship]\add*.
\v 37 And we were in all two hundred and seventy-six souls in the ship.\f + \fr 27:37 \ft NA28 has two hundred and seventy-six; a few witnesses read "about seventy-six." The figure puts a number on what the angel said: all those sailing with you.\f*
\v 38 And when they had eaten enough they lightened the ship, throwing the wheat into the sea \add [i.e. the last of the cargo, to raise her for the run at the beach]\add*.
\s1 Aground
\p
\v 39 And when day came they did not recognize the land, but they made out a certain bay with a beach, onto which they planned, if they could, to drive the ship.
\v 40 And casting off the anchors they left them in the sea, at the same time loosening the ropes of the steering oars; and hoisting the foresail to the wind they made for the beach.\f + \fr 27:40 \ft Three actions at once, and every one of them right: cut the anchors away rather than waste time hauling them, free the two great steering oars that had been lashed since the storm began, and set a small headsail to give steerage way. This is a crew that knows its work — which is why Paul kept them aboard.\f*
\v 41 But falling into a place where two seas met, they ran the vessel aground; and the bow stuck fast and stayed unmoved, while the stern was being broken up by the force \add [i.e. and the ship began to come apart under them, as he had said it would]\add*.
\v 42 And the soldiers' counsel was to kill the prisoners, so that none of them might swim away and escape;\f + \fr 27:42 \ft A guard answered with his own life for an escaped prisoner (12:19; 16:27). With the ship breaking up and men about to be in open water, the simplest way to be sure of the count is to kill them first.\f*
\v 43 but the centurion, wanting to bring Paul safely through, kept them from their purpose, and he ordered those who could swim to throw themselves overboard first and get to land,
\v 44 and the rest, some on planks and some on pieces from the ship. And so it happened that they all came safely through to land.\f + \fr 27:44 \ft Every one of the two hundred and seventy-six, exactly as the angel said — and every one of them arriving on a piece of the wrecked ship. The storm was not stilled and the vessel was not spared. They are saved through the wreck, not around it. — And note the last link in the chain: they live because a Roman officer wanted to keep one prisoner alive.\f*

\c 28
\s1 Malta
\p
\v 1 And when we had come safely through, we then learned that the island was called Malta \add [i.e. they had not known where they were until they were ashore on it]\add*.
\v 2 And the foreigners\f + \fr 28:2 \ft Greek \tl barbaroi\tl*, which in Greek simply means people who do not speak Greek — the word imitates the sound of unintelligible speech. It is not an insult, and the author uses it of people he is about to hold up as models. Malta spoke Punic.\f* showed us no ordinary kindness; for they lit a fire and welcomed us all, because of the rain that had set in and because of the cold.\f + \fr 28:2 \ft The word is \tl philanthrōpia\tl*, love of humankind — reckoned among the highest virtues in Greek moral thought, and used of God's own character. Two hundred and seventy-six soaked strangers are taken in by people who have never heard of Moses and owe them nothing.\f*
\v 3 And when Paul had gathered a bundle of sticks and laid it on the fire, a viper came out because of the heat and fastened on his hand \add [i.e. carrying firewood with everyone else, as he had always worked with his hands]\add*.
\v 4 And when the foreigners saw the creature hanging from his hand, they said to one another, "Surely this man is a murderer, whom Justice has not allowed to live, though he came safely through from the sea."\f + \fr 28:4 \ft "Justice" is \tl Dikē\tl*, the goddess of retribution — daughter of Zeus, who sees to it that the guilty do not slip away. Their reasoning is coherent: the sea was meant to have him, the sea failed, and the snake has been sent to finish the account. It is the reasoning of Job's friends, that misfortune exposes guilt, and it is about to be shown up.\f*
\v 5 But he shook the creature off into the fire and suffered no harm.\f + \fr 28:5 \ft No prayer is recorded and no miracle is described; the author simply says nothing happened to him. The reader already knows why: a man who must stand before Caesar (27:24) is not going to be killed by a snake on Malta. Compare Luke 10:19 — "I have given you authority to tread on snakes... and nothing shall harm you."\f*
\v 6 And they were expecting him to swell up or suddenly fall down dead; but after waiting a long time and seeing nothing out of place happen to him, they changed their minds and said he was a god.\f + \fr 28:6 \ft From murderer to god on the same evidence. The same thing happened at Lystra, where a healing turned Paul and Barnabas into Zeus and Hermes (14:11-13), and there he tore his clothes over it. Here the author records no protest and lets the two verdicts stand side by side. Both are wrong, and they are wrong the same way: they read God's intentions straight off an event.\f*
\s1 Healings on the Island
\p
\v 7 Now in the region around that place were lands belonging to the leading man of the island, named Publius\f + \fr 28:7 \ft "The leading man" — \tl prōtos\tl* — is not a description but the actual title of the chief Roman official on Malta, and it appears as such on inscriptions found there. The author gets these titles right everywhere: magistrates at Philippi, politarchs at Thessalonica, proconsul at Corinth, town clerk at Ephesus.\f*, who welcomed us and gave us friendly lodging for three days.
\v 8 And it happened that the father of Publius was lying down, gripped by fevers and dysentery; and Paul went in to him and prayed, and laying his hands on him he healed him \add [i.e. prayed first, and then laid hands on him]\add*.
\v 9 And when this happened, the rest of those on the island who had sicknesses were coming to him and being healed;
\v 10 and they honored us with many honors, and when we put out to sea they put on board what we needed.\f + \fr 28:10 \ft They arrived with nothing, having thrown even the wheat overboard, and they leave provisioned. The island that took them in as castaways sends them off as guests — which is what the law of Israel asks of Israel toward the stranger (Leviticus 19:33-34), done here by people who have never read it.\f*
\s1 On to Rome
\p
\v 11 And after three months we put out in an Alexandrian ship that had wintered at the island, with the Twin Brothers as its figurehead.\f + \fr 28:11 \ft Three months puts it in February, when the sea reopened. The Twin Brothers are Castor and Pollux, the sons of Zeus, patrons of sailors, whose sign was the electrical fire seen on masts in a storm. Paul sails to Rome under their painted image, and the author reports it without a word of comment.\f*
\v 12 And putting in at Syracuse we stayed three days,
\v 13 and from there we made a circuit and arrived at Rhegium.\f + \fr 28:13 \ft NA28 has a word meaning either "having taken up the anchors" or "having made a circuit"; the second is taken here. A variant reading gives "we went around," which comes to much the same thing — beating up the coast against the wind rather than running straight.\f* And after one day a south wind came up, and on the second day we came to Puteoli \add [i.e. the port of Rome, where the grain fleet unloaded]\add*,
\v 14 where we found brothers and were urged to stay with them seven days. And so we came to Rome.\f + \fr 28:14 \ft Two things in one verse. There are believers in Italy already, whom Paul did not put there and does not know — the message travelled the trade routes ahead of him. And then the destination that the whole second half of this book has been driving at arrives in a subordinate clause of five words. No description of the city, no swell of feeling. And so we came to Rome.\f*
\v 15 And from there the brothers, having heard about us, came to meet us as far as the Forum of Appius and Three Taverns; and when Paul saw them he thanked God and took courage.\f + \fr 28:15 \ft Forty-three miles out and thirty-three miles out, on the Appian Way — a day or two on foot, each way, to meet a man none of them had met. The word for their coming out is the one used of a city sending a delegation to escort an arriving dignitary; the dignitary is in chains. — And note the last clause. The man who has spent the whole voyage putting courage into other people needed some, and got it from the sight of strangers on a road.\f*
\v 16 And when we came into Rome, Paul was allowed to stay by himself with the soldier guarding him.\f + \fr 28:16 \ft "The centurion handed the prisoners over to the captain of the guard" is added before this in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28. — The arrangement is the mildest form of Roman custody: private lodging, and a soldier chained to him in shifts. It is what makes everything in the rest of the chapter possible.\f*
\s1 Paul and the Jewish Leaders of Rome
\p
\v 17 And it happened that after three days he called together the leading men of the Jews \add [i.e. three days, and the synagogue first, in the last city as in every other]\add*. And when they came together he said to them, "Men, brothers, though I have done nothing against the people or the customs of our fathers, I was handed over as a prisoner from Jerusalem into the hands of the Romans.
\v 18 They examined me and wanted to release me, because there was no cause for death in me.
\v 19 But when the Jews spoke against it, I was forced to appeal to Caesar — not that I had anything to accuse my own nation of.\f + \fr 28:19 \ft He is at pains to say that the appeal was a shield and not a weapon. He has not come to Rome to prosecute anybody, and he still calls them "my own nation."\f*
\v 20 So for this reason I called for you, to see you and speak with you; for it is for the hope of Israel that I am wearing this chain."\f + \fr 28:20 \ft The same defence he has made in every court: before the council (23:6), before Felix (24:15), before Agrippa (26:6-7). The hope of Israel is the resurrection, and the claim is that it has begun in one man. He is in irons for believing what his nation prays for.\f*
\v 21 And they said to him, "We have received no letters about you from Judea, nor has any of the brothers arrived and reported or spoken anything bad about you \add [i.e. Jerusalem has sent nothing ahead — the appeal put him out of their reach]\add*.
\v 22 But we think it right to hear from you what you think; for about this sect it is known to us that everywhere it is spoken against."\f + \fr 28:22 \ft "Sect" is the neutral word again — a party, a school, such as the Pharisees and Sadducees were. They know of it and they know it is contradicted everywhere, and they have not made up their minds.\f*
\s1 From Morning Until Evening
\p
\v 23 And having set a day with him, they came to him in his lodging in greater numbers, and he set it out to them, bearing full witness to the kingdom of God and persuading them about Jesus, from both the law of Moses and the prophets, from morning until evening.\f + \fr 28:23 \ft The method is the risen Jesus's own on the Emmaus road: "beginning from Moses and all the prophets, he interpreted to them in all the scriptures the things concerning himself" (Luke 24:27). And the subject — the kingdom of God — is where this book started, with the risen Jesus speaking of it for forty days (1:3), and where it is about to end (verse 31). The disciples had wanted to know whether the kingdom was about to be restored to Israel (1:6). It is being proclaimed in the capital of the empire that rules Israel, which was not the answer they were expecting.\f*
\v 24 And some were persuaded by what was said, but others would not trust \add [i.e. divided, as every synagogue from Pisidian Antioch onward has been divided]\add*.
\v 25 And being out of agreement with one another they began to leave, after Paul had said one word: "The Holy Spirit spoke well through Isaiah the prophet to your fathers,
\v 26 saying, 'Go to this people and say: With hearing you will hear and never understand, and looking you will look and never see.
\v 27 For the heart of this people has grown fat, and with their ears they have heard heavily, and their eyes they have shut, so that they might not see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their heart and turn, and I would heal them.'\f + \fr 28:26-27 \ft Isaiah 6:9-10, the words given to the prophet at his commissioning — spoken not to a foreign nation but to Israel, at the moment Isaiah was sent to it. Note the last clause and where the shutting is done: they have closed their own eyes. And note the last word: healing is what was on offer if they had turned. The quotation is not a door being locked; it is the oldest description in the book of what happens when the word is preached to this people, and Paul says the Spirit spoke it "well."\f*
\v 28 So let it be known to you that this rescue of God has been sent to the nations; they will also listen."\f + \fr 28:28 \ft The third and last time he says it — Pisidian Antioch (13:46), Corinth (18:6), and now Rome. And "also" is doing work: not instead of you, but you as well. The last thing said to a Jewish audience in this book is that Gentiles will hear, and it is said in a room where some of them have just been persuaded (verse 24).\f*
\p
\v 30 And he stayed two whole years in his own rented place, and welcomed all who came in to him,\f + \fr 28:30 \ft "And when he had said these things the Jews went away, having a great dispute among themselves" is added as verse 29 in the majority text and in most older English versions; it is not in NA28, and the numbering here runs from 28 to 30.\f*
\v 31 proclaiming the kingdom of God and teaching the things about the Lord Jesus Christ with all boldness, unhindered.\f + \fr 28:31 \ft The book does not end; it stops. We are not told how the appeal came out, whether Paul stood before Nero, whether he lived or died — and the writer plainly knew. What he chose to leave in the reader's hand is the last word of the Greek, one word, \tl akōlytōs\tl*: unhindered. A man in chains, in the capital of the empire, with a soldier at his wrist, announcing the reign of God to anybody who comes through the door, and nobody stopping him. That is the answer to the question the book opened with, when they asked whether the kingdom was being restored to Israel and were told instead that they would be witnesses to the end of the earth.\f*
